      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WARRINGTON OF. THE FUTURE. THE TOWN PLANNING SCHEME. The Mayor's Plea for Slum Dwellers.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON OF. THE FUTURE. THE TOWN PLANNING SCHEME. The Mayor's Plea for Slum Dwellers.</p>
        <p>SD. THE OBSfACLE.</p>
        <p>The attractive lines on which the future develop- ment of Warrington is to be planned were indicated at an inquiry conducted at the Town Hall on Wednesday by Mr. Thomas Adams, an inspector of the Local Government Board, into the application of the Corpor- ation, under Part II of the Housing, Town Planning Sic. Act, for authority to prepare a town planning scheme with reference to certain lands within the borough. There was a good attendance, including the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), Alderman 13ennett (chairman of the Street Improvement Committee), Mr. A. T. Holloway (deputy town clerk), Mr. A. M. Ker (borough surveyor), and Mr John Fairhurst (borough treasurer). The Warrington Rural District Council was represented by Dr. Murray, Mr. W. Furness and Mr. B. S. Newcombe (surveyor). Mr. W. Woolley (Liverpool) appeared for the London and North- Western Railway Company. Mr. J. E. Seeker watched the interests of the principal estates affected by the scheme—those of the late Lord Winmarleigh, Colonel Ireland Blackburne, Messrs. Greenall Whitley and Company and Mr. T. H Lyon. Mr. F. W. Wright represented the Warrington and District Property Owners' Association.</p>
        <p>- ------ Mr Woolley said the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Company formally objected to certain lands being included in the scheme.</p>
        <p>The Inspector said he wished to make it clear that the object of the inquiry was to determine an area and not to settle any details of the scheme, such as passing over railways, or the questibn of bridges. Those must be left until the next stage. If the company had any objection to the inclusion of any land, this was the proper time to raise it, but not to enter into any details of the scheme. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Town Clerk said the object of the inquiry was not to discuss the scheme of the Corpora- tion but to decide whether the land was suitable for town planning—that was, was it either building land or capable of being made into building land? Having emphasised the fact that Warrington depended entirely upon its manufactures, Mr Hallaway said the continuous growth of the borough was shown not only by the Increase of the population but by the Joon ace in the number of houses erected every year. The area of land they proposed to include in the scheme was 1476 acres. Speaking broadly, it comprised the whole of the un-built upon land in the borough. The question arose as to why they wanted a town planning scheme. To anyone who knew Warrington, the answer was obvious. He could point out parts of Warrington where it was neeessary for a man to traverse a circuitous route of two miles in order to reach a point three quarters of a mile distant. There were cards which had no open spaces and in which there was no place, except the street, for the children to play in. The humber of houses per acre in some districts vcas excessively large. The main streets had had to he WIDENED AT A COST APPROACHING £500,000.</p>
        <p>Some people said that was due to the sine of our ancestors. That, of course was a question of argument. Warrington was a very old town and 30E110 of the sins— if sins they were—were perpetrated some 200 years ago when the streets were wide enough lor the traffio and probably for the traffic of fifty years beyond that time. Ile did not think it was always fair to blame our ancestors. It was quite possible that the time might come when some futare chairman of the Street Improvement Committee would say" I cannot uoderbtand those people in 1911 only I making Bridge-street 60 feet wide. Why did they not make it 100 teat?" The Corporation had had to spend a great deal of money btcanse Worth gton's streets were not up to the present traffic requirements and they hoped that, by a town planning scheme, they might to some extent render it unnecessary for a future genera- tiOn to spend on a similar scale. They hoped,if possible, to make some provision for open spaces in the future development of the town. They were desirous of link- ing up the arterial roads by appropriate cross roads. Warrington had been split up into different portions by the lines of railway, tne river Mersey, aad canals. The Corporation would have to bear tueir part in the scheme and they were prepared to ao so. The scheme was unanimously passed by the Town Council and it was afterwards brought before a meeting of the property owners affected, who, with one or two excep- tions, cordially blessed it. The Corporation welcomed criticism as long as it was open and fair. They could not bring forward a huge scheme like that, without having the co-operation of the landowners. It would be fatal to the Corporation to try and rush the scheme through and it was no part of their intention to do so. They welcomed criticism—they even looked for it—and they asked the landowners to co-operate with them and help them at every step in the scheme. The question would probably be asked : " Why have you confined your scheme to the borough, seeing that outside the borough you have large suburbs grow- ing up, which, to all intents and purposes, form one community of interest and which certainly are within the sphere of influence of Warrington. Why have you not included those areas within the limits of your scheme, as you are entitled to do ?" That subject was brought before the Corporation who met the Warring- ton Rural District Council—the authority on the Lancashire side. That Council expressed willingnesa to co-operate with the Corporation, but they were wishful to include within the boundaries of their scheme a very large area. The same applied to the Runcorn Rural District Council, on the Cheshire aide. While the Corporation were most anxious to co-oper- ate with neighbouring authorities, they recog- nized that to go in for a scheme of of such magnitude—the area which the Warrington Rural District Council proposed to include was 9,000 acres—would involve a great deal of time in the neces- sary preliminaries. While that was time which the Warrington Rural District Council could afford to spend, there was more urgency in Warrington where building was proceeding very rapidly. They might have found, after arranging everything with the three councils, that there was very little left to town-plan. The reason why the Corporation had included the borough area alone, was that they wished to put their scheme in motion at the earliest possible date. The Inspector: With regard to the Rural District Council, the negotiations have not definitely broken off? The Deputy Town Clerk : Oh no, sir. The Inspector: May I suggest that, after this inquiry, it might be useful if the representatives of the Rural District Council would meet the Mayor, myself and one or two representatives of the borough to dis- cuss any questions in common ? The Deputy Town Clerk : We shall be only too happy to do so. The Inspector : What is the position with regard to the Runcorn Rural District Council? The Deputy Town Clerk: As far as we are concerned we are prepared to co-operate with them. The Inspector: But you have not taken any steps in that direction ?</p>
        <p>The Deputy Town Clerk: We have met them and they have defined the area that they wish to be town- Planned, but that area is so large that the same reason applies to them as to the Warrington Rural District Council. As soon as we have got our own scheme in working order we are prepared to co-operate with the Runcorn Rural District Council. As far as I know,they have no objection. They passed a resolution some months ago, intimating their willingness to co-operate with us, and they have not withdrawn it. The Inspector : Has the whole of this development of Stockton Heath taken place since the Manchester Ship Canal was made? The Deputy Town Clerk : Practically so. Most of this has taken place within the last ten years. The Canal was opened in 1894. Stockton Heath is purely and simply an overflow from Warrington. It is more conveniently situated, more pleasant to live in, and the amenities of the district that we hear such a great deal about, are much better than those of Warrington. Con- tinuing, Mr Holloway repeated that Warrington was a manufacturing town and when they talked of town planning, some people were inclined to scoff. They imagined that it was proposed to IMPORT THE SCENERY OF THE LAKE DISTRICT and the blue skies of Bournemouth. (Laughter.) They could not do that, but it was necessary in a manufacturing town that there should be proper means of transit, that the manufac turer should be able to get from one portion of the' borough to another in the shortest possible way, that the people who worked for him should be better housed, that the inhabitants should have proper and adequate open spaces—and those were improvements ' which the Corporation hoped to achieve with the co-operation of the landowners. In conclusion Mr Holloway mentioned with great regret that Mr Longdin, whose name appeared on the plans of the scheme, had unfortunately died and they had not been in the position to have his excellent advice on the subject. Mr Longdin was the pioneer and it was of considerable regret to the Corporation and to the town at large that he had not been allowed to finish the work. It had, how- ever been undertaken by his successor, Mr Rer, and the Corporation felt sure that in his hands it would be done ably and well. _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett then addressed the Inspector in support of the application. He pointed out that for seventeen years the Street Improvement Committee had had town planning proposals in their minds but unfortunately they had not been able to carry out those pious intentions because the law of the land was not in harmony with them. It was only with the passing of the Town Planning Act that they had been able to Lake practical steps forward. They were not only willing but extremely anxiouo to carry out future schemes with the cordial co-operation of the two ad joining authorities because the scheme as it stood—important and urgently necessary as it was—would not carry them very far and they must look for the ultimate larger scheme if the district was to be planned on the best lines. The first and dominant factor_in wishing to put the Act</p>
        <p>into operation was that of economy. He knew there was an idea abroad that town planning might be expensive and land us into untold liabilities but their motive in adopting it was the very opposite of that. When be mentioned that since 1873 they had spent no less than £400,000 on street improvements, it would be realized that there could not have been much town planning in the past, and if they could avoid similar mistakes in the future they would be the true friends of economy. They ' had three of the main streets still to widen and if he said that would cost another £lOO,OOO it would be a very low estimate indeed. £500,000 was therefore a very modest computation of the cost of street improve- ments during toe lifetime of the present generation. That meant something use £7 per head of the popula • tion or, taking the average family of five' .£35 per household or £1 a house per annum for the whole period of thirty-nine years. Those figures were alarming. They meant a rate of 500 pennies—or £2 is 8d in the £ —necessitated absolutely and entirely by street improve- ruents,most of which could have been avoided by reason- able foresight in the past. The actual amount they were paying on street improvement loans to-day was nearly Is. Id. in the £. The Alderman next emphasised the importance of the question from the health point of view. In many parts of Warrington the houses were crowded as many as 40 or 50 to the acre; 40 per cent.of the homes contained only four rooms; two of the wards had not a single open space and in St. John's Ward it was a practical impossibility to obtain one. The actual result of that was seen in the death rates. Taking the four worst wards, in Howley ward the peneral death rate was 20.6 and the infantile death-rate 176 per 1,000; in Orford Ward, general 17.4, infantile 168 ; in St John's Ward, general 17, infantile 127; in Bewsey Ward, general 16 9, infantile 161. He was sure that those VERY HEAVY AND TRAGIC DEATH RATES</p>
        <p>were largely due to the fact that houses were unduly crowded and there were not sufficient opoortunities to enjoy fresh air and healthy recreation. The considera- tion of convenience was equally important. One of the chief things they wanted to do was to remedy the tremendous inconvenience occasioned by the fact that several of the main roads were not proporly connected. There were no direct roads from Chester New-road to Wilderpool-causeway, from Wilderspool-causeway to Latchford, and from Latchford to Manchester-road. They recently built a bridge at Howley, hut there was no direct access to it ; they had to pass over a wilder- ness of brickbats to reach it. Finally, the Alderman said they could not expect to import the blue skies of Italy, but they did contend that, even in a town like Warrington, tney should have some regard for amenities and make the town a little more agreeable, livable and pleasant: . _</p>
        <p>The Borough Surveyor then explained the scheme. Referring to the unsatisractory manner in which the development cf the borough bad proceeded, he p Anted out that in part of Latchford Ward there was no through road, with the exception of the one main road. The streets wound in and out and there was none of that consistency about the lay-out which could have been obtained if the lines had been laid down in advance. Asked by the Inspector as to the reason for including Victoria Park in the scheme, the Borough Surveyor said thut when a wider bridge for vehicular traffic was erected at Howley, it would probably be necessary to arrange tor a road througn the park to connect with linutstord-road.</p>
        <p>The Inspector: Are not the present powers sufficient ?</p>
        <p>The Deputy Town Clerk : The present powers are sufficient for the purpose. The Corporation are pre- pared to take Victoria Park out of the scheme, if the Board wish it. The Inspector : It is simply a question of requiring sufficient reason for its inclusion. If there is any pur- pose in the scheme to be served, I shall be quite pleased to place it before the Board. ihe Deputy Town Clerk : The Corporation have na intention of using the laud for any other purpose than that of a park. The only improvement they may possihlymake is to construct a road right through it and I see no reason why the/ should not do that, under their present powers. THE NIAYOR AND SLUM DWELLINGS. CORPORATION REQUIRES MORE POWER.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, in supporting the application, said he could not help expressing regret, as an old and ardent sanitarian, at the almost i remediable condi- tion of the places where the great majority of the poor had to live. In their present application' the Corporation were acting maihly for posterity. He would like to be able to do something more for those who were living in their midst, under very miserable conditions, as regarded sanitary matters He was not speaking off the book. The Health Com- mittee had made visits of a lengthened and not very agreeable character, to slum property. They had met the owners to arrange what should be done to remedy the grievances, but the houses had been only" patched up." Yet tLe Corporation were obliged to put up with those alterations, which were called improvements, because they satisfied the requirements of the law. Nine-tenths of the horse had been stolen and by the Town Planning Act an attempt was being made to save the remaining tenth. He supposed it was at present. a vain regret, but he hoped legislation might improve matters to such an extent that it would not always be a vain regret. It was a pressing need, not only in Warrington but in other towns, that they should have more practical oppor- tunities of remedying grievances that were most urgent. in the matter of sanitation.</p>
        <p>Ihe Inspector :The question is very largely in your own hands, as far as sanitation is concerned. The Mayor : But we find it practically impossible ' to do anything really effective, in the way of removing the grievances I speak of. £ s. d.—the expense of doing the work—is the great difficulty. I cannot help thinking that the Corporation ought to have some opportunity of taking over these insanitary properties, when the owner is not prepared to improve them. It would be to the mutual good of all concerned. The Inspector: You have not indicated any addi- tional open spaces that you propose to have. The Deputy-Town Clerk : That is a matter for the , future. We hope to be able to arrange those, in con- junction with the landowners. It will be a policy of give-and-take. Mr Woolley, on behalf of the London and North- Western Railway Co., maintained that the scheme of direct communication between Wilderspool and Latch- ford could not be carried through without seriously prejudicing their interests. They had acquired some of the land for the purpose of constructing very important sidings. They were, however, willing to consider a scheme. The Inspector: You would have full opportunity of objecting to any scheme proposed. The Corporation would necessarily have to compensate you for any injury they caused you. Of course,the statutory powers of a railway company are above being interfered with in certain directions. Mr E England supported the suggestion that the Corporation should confer with the adjoining authori- ties, as it was highly important that an arrangement should be arrived at. A good portion of the land which the Corporation proposed to include in the scheme could not fairly be called building land—par- ticularly that in the vicinity of the railway, which was low-lying land and could not be made into good build- ing laud until a considerable sum had been spent upon it. At the same time he welcomed the scheme, because he believed it would be a fine thing for the town. PROSPECT OF NEW WORKS.</p>
        <p>Mr Secker said the landlords were prepared to co-operate in the scheme, on the distinct understanding that the Corporation would take them fully into their confidence with regard to the details. He did not look upon Warrington with such black eyes as Alderman Bennett—(laughter) ;—he did not think it was so bad as all that. However, if the Corporation were going to lay out the scheme on paper, without taking the landowners into their confidence beforehand, it would be bound to lead to difficulties —not merely because they wanted to oppose, but because they had knowledge of schemes that even the Corporation did not know of. A great deal of the land was being held by them for works—and works only— for the benefit of the town. If the Corporation laid out a road—however nice it might look on paper— right across the land, it might seriously interfere with the use of that land for works. They could not object to the preliminary scheme, as it was merely a question of the borough boundaries, but they objected to the whole of the land being treated as if it were going to be built upon for housing purposes. The Deputy Town Clerk, in replying to the points raised, said the objection raised by the London and North-Western Railway Company had almost died a natural death.</p>
        <p>--.-- ------ The Inspector : I think you must try and meet it, as far as you can. The Deputy Town Clerk said the interests of the railway company were amply safeguarded. The Cor- poration could not do anything unless they were in agreement with them, and the company would be amply compensated for any injury that might be done to them. With regard to the land owners, the Corporation were prepared to take them into their fullest confidence and to meet them at every step. The interests of the land owners in that respect were to a great extent the interests of the community at large. The inquiry was then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>COLDS, HEADACHE, CATARRH</head>
