      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>STAY ED IN BED WHILE HIS WIFE WORKED</head>
        <p>STAY ED IN BED WHILE HIS WIFE WORKED</p>
        <p>At Warrington Police Court, on Thursday, Frank Modand, 19, Winvvick-road, was summoned for neglect- ing his two children. Mr A Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, said the chief cause of complaint against the prisoner was that be would not work to support his wife and children. Some months ago he was sentenced to six months' imprisonment for a similar offence, and his wife obtained a separation order. When he came out of prison be so dogged her footsteps that she decided to give him another trial. They lived together, but he again resumed his old habits. The wife went out working in a cotton mill, but he was too lazy to work, and stayed in bed until late in the morning. The Chairman (Mr .1 Rymer Young), in sending prisoner to gaol for six months, said he could hardly find words to express his contempt of a man who stayed in bed whilst his wife went out working.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) Aldermen Smethurst and Bolton, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs S Wright. NV Furness and T Locker. A WIDOw IN TROUBLF..—Mary Preston, a widow of Pembertons-yard was charged with having been drunk in Cnarge of a child on Saturday night.—Sergeant Wooßacot proved the case and said the woman was so drunk that she fell down and the baby's head struck the pavement.—The Mayor severely reprimanded the woman, pointing out that if the child had died she would have been proceeded against for manslaughter. Having regard to the fact that it was her first appear- ance the case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>BROTHERS IN TROUBLE.---TWO brothers, Charles and Francis Kent, 14, Winifred-street, were charged with having stolen during the past twelve months a large quantity of goods from stalls in the market.—The Lhief Constable said that at 9.15 on Saturday night the prisoner Charles stood by the boot stall of Mr Frisby in the market acting in a suspicious manner. Mr T F Frisby, the manager, saw him examine several pairs of slippers and then, putting a pair under his coat, walk away without tendering payment. The stallholder stopped him and he admitted the theft. The prisoner was taken to the police station by P.C. Porter. Sergeant Reddington visited the prisoner's house, where he found a large quantity of property, which Charles admitted having stolen from the market in company with his brother. A remandlwas asked for to complete inquiries and to prefer other charges against the men.— Prisoners were remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>A GIRL'S DESPERATION.—.Iie-17e.cca Aspinall, of Pemberton, near Wigan, although only a young woman was charged on remand with having attempted to com- mit suicide by taking a quantity of benzine in Victoria Park on October sth.—The Chief Constable said it was desired that the girl should be committed for trial at the next Qearter Sessions with a view to her being detained under the Borstal System. She was an exceedingly bad character. From inquiries the police had made and from information they had been supplied with by the various police forces of the country they found that she was almost incorrigible. There were a number of convictions against her at St Helens, Liverpool and Manchester. She had been twice charged previously with having attempted to commit suicide. She had a good home and her parents had done everything possible for her. She had been restored to her parents no less than 18 times. He had received a letter from the ladies on the Borstal Committee at Manchester Prison, advising that she should be committed for trial. About 6.20 on Saturday October sth, the accused was observed by Mr Albert Cooper, 22, Ash-grove, sitting in Victoria Park, acting in a suspicious manner. He saw her take a bottle from her pocket and commence to drink it. Mr Cooper dashed the bottle from her mouth, and found that it contained benzine. When asked by the police to give an account of her actions she said," I am tired of it and want to die." She had a small flask of whisky hut whether that was the thing she was tired of he could not say.—The Bench sent the case for trial to the Quarter Sessions.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr J Hepherd (in the chair),Colonel Edelsten, Councillor C Jackson Holmes and Mr J It Young. A SHARP THlNG.—James Savage, 3, Choiley- street, was charged with stealing two razors value 75., the property of Charles Lee, 33, Green-street, on Friday. The Chief Constable said that Savage called upon the prosecutor and offered to tell a pair of old boots and a pawnticket for sixpence. Lee refused to entertain the offer, whereupon the prisoner left. The prosecutor went into the house, but bearing someone in the shop ran back in time to see Savage walking out. Two razors were missing. Lee ran after the prisoner, who stoutly denied touching the razors, whicb,however, were found to be in the lining of his coat. Other razors bad been missed from various shops in the town. It was desired to make inquiries concerning the pawn- ticket for a new pair of boots which was found in Savage's possession and which were not his size, and a remand was asked for.—Prisuner was remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton and Smett.urst, Messrs S Wright, G Pilling, A .1 Jolley and W Fu.ness. ATTEMPTED TILL ROBBERY.—Two brothers, Arthur and Robert Heaton, were charged with attempting to steal money from the Legh Arms, Mersey-street, on Thursday afternoon.—The Chief Constable said the prisoners were together in the vaults. The barman had occasion to leave the room for a minute or so and whilst he was away Robert got over the counter. A woman informed the barman and prisoner in reply said he was looking for his dog. The barman again left the room and on his return he saw Robert with his hand over the counter. The police were informed and the prisoneras arrested.—The magistrates committed Robert for a month and Arthur for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ASSOCIATION</p>
        <p>(N.A. CENTRE). As long as the weather holds good there will be a good string of anglers trying weekly to upset calcula- tions on the set board. The specimen brigade must have had a anxious time seeing .heir weights week after week remain unbeaten, especially as an angler with only a fair amount of luck could have made a great change on the board. But specimens remain yet in their haunts and all the skill our locals have has failed to draw them. One member has changed the chub column with a fine one from Mallvaley, drawing 21b 2oz 10drs. This, I think, places him second on the list. A dace also has been weighted 6ioz, this also takes second place, but with about two days rain and the weather remaining mild great changes should take place. Several of our members visited a water in North Wales and although their catches were not great they give a good report. They say they only got used to the stream about an hour and half before packing up time and some fair roach and dace were creeled. None were big enough to take the top of the board but still they had fair catches for first timers and they have gained valuable experience for a second visit.</p>
        <p>NORTON. TllO canal here was fished by about a dozen members and not one had a catch worth talking of. J Potter was about top with eight roach; several bad twos and threes but there was not a fish of 80z taken all Sunday last. As time goes on the canal seems to fish worse. It is a repetition of previoua years. This has never been a spot for good fishing after September but then it might be given a trial with one or two of the old bands who promised they would start after all feeders were done with. Now is about their time to show us if they can get better returns than the maggot fisher. I am sure the society wishes them every success and there will be nobody more pleased to see them weigh in than the members who are now on the top and caught them with feeder and maggot.</p>
        <p>MICKLE TRAFFORD. The Gowy was of again last week and and members dcclare they will not pay another visit unless there is rain to colour the water as angling under the present conditions is simply a waste of time. They are still cleaning out and are now work- ing down Stanney Brook. The dreadger at Little Barrow end seems to make slow progress but a great undertaking is going on here. They are widening up to the high banks and are going two feet down. Whether this will make it better for the angler remains to be seen. The part done looks almost like a canal, and there will certainly not be the same shelter for the fish, although they will have more water.</p>
        <p>_ (1-IETER. The Dee and Shropshire Union Canal last week did not return their usual average, and those who visited</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>Saturday was an ideal day for the gime and °air majority of the clubs have got in full swing, at least so far as the novice and junior runners are concernheiq and with the exception of Rylands' the clubs in t" district are not behind band. Runcorn Tally Ho mustered 14 for St Helens C.CJI. meet at Prescot. Bolton United Harriers held a• tb,r7. miles road handicap and their latest acquisiture J Horan proved his usefulness by winning irons tI4 scratch mark in good time. I rd Salford Harriers held an invitation run at Stret•gth which concluded with a race over the last mile WlO. the result that Joe Whatmough annexed the spoils. ri , Whatmough, formerly of Warrington,now of Brought°o was prominent. Although an invitation had be d, extended to Warrington A.C., it was not consider: tie advisable to take part, as the seniors preferred a gen.,e, canter over their own course with a view to he/Pil, their own novices and juniors, therefore there wf muster of 24, chiefly novices, for a run o,ch'e about six miles of good country in „,.`ar neighbourhood of Walton and Moore. v—cl the first mile R Whittaker made the pace. followed by F Shadwell who then took the lead and maintained a steady pace to the finish. C Vose was ably assistel,- by H K Jepson and W Thornley in whipping and Pe'bt ing the novices in useful style, and eventually breng„f one, Joe Hanson, home within a dozen yards ;5 Shadwell ; about ten yards sway Stanley Siddall fourth, closely followed by Jepson who was bringli"V Isaao Sankey along nicely • about 70 yards roll ;a Thornley was holding together a group of prorn'e" novices.</p>
        <p>TO-DAYS FIXTURE. An interesting run is set to take place fro th: m Limerick Hotel, and runners with their friends ttr requested to meet at the oar terminus Sankey Bridge at two o'clock.</p>
      </div>
