      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>NOTES BY " MUST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " MUST."</p>
        <p>Iceither Warrington nor Oldham have yet won a ,71atch at home, but both have won two games on reign soil. The record is remarkable for either .".113, and particularly for Oldham who, up to last 4ason, had not lost a home match for nearly three „Years. And this year they were supposed to be Btr°Dger than ever, with the acquisition of Farnsworth 4,Pd Holbrook. Even money will not buy success, '"ough in the long run it comes very near. The bon sensa- of last Saturday was undoubtedly the defeat of Oldham at home by Swinton, who followed up the "riking success of St. Helens. For an almost purely local team, the Lions are doing wonderfully well.</p>
        <p>Warrington won on Saturday at Barrow, by a solitary point, and did remarkably well. Barrow are ft:Aber lowly side showing distinct possibilities of ildvance and after their draw at Widnes, Warrington's 'chances were not particularly bright. Barrow were Zrtainly very confident of success, but they found the wirepullers in a determined mood. Just now Warrington are playing with a grim -earnestness 'worthy of all praise, and the side carries no Dassengers. They knew they were good enough to at Barrow, and by never slackening for a moment tleY accomplished the feat in the last few minutes. Lie score was only 3 points to 2 points, but it was earned, and on the day's play the visitors were '49 better balanced and speedier team.</p>
        <p>It is rather a curious circumstance that Warrington 'lave had their only try, in two successive matches, ftwarded for obstruction. They are not showing very 'atrong scoring power, but that will come when the laalf-back play reaches the required standard. On taturday, Barrow were leading by a f goal to nil when Woodburn dribbled over the line from half way and as then knocked off the ball. It was obvious that he Oe must have scored and the referee had no hesitation ),.awarding the point. A penalty kick would have "ell ridiculously inadequate. Cox had also been rover the line once with a point which looked legiti- Mate, so it c mid not be said that Warrington got 'tore than their deserts.</p>
        <p>It:a,</p>
        <p>,The most notable feature of the game was the fine "ushlay of the Warrington forwards. They scrum- teaged strongly but in the loose they were brilliant eed far too speedy for the home vanguard. Both Cox "d Fearnley gave a capital exhibition and earned '9leir places, while George Thomas was an excellent leader. Woodburk showed that he had recovered a c't of his form, and with a centre like Tranter to play leith he should be more successful than he was last season. Waywell was a bit disappointing on the attack, though his speed was an important factor in defence on more than one occasion. Rushton played Very smart game and tackled better than usual, hile Renwick seemed more at home in the centre. 'lle makes a mistake sometimes in trying to crash tarough an opponent and would do better to pass °ftener. The League table tip-to-date is as follows : For. /test. Per. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c'nt'ge. 40nslet 66 0 0... 23 30 136-11 5 37-10000 4uirfield b_igan I_,'Webury 5 4 1 0... 10 24 92... 7 6 32... 8010 do 5 3 1 1., 18 13 75...10 5 35... 70'00 ,nr,°,,,lighton 6 3 1 2... 10 15 65... 7 8 38... 66'66 ll tiord ,wittt.on 6 4 2 0... 7 7 35... 4 9 35... 66-66 atlev 4011 5 3 2 0... 17 19 91...11 12 58... 60-00 bakefield T 5 2 1 2... 11 17 73... 9 13 57... 60-00 41411 a, Helens 6 3 2 1... 11 14 64...12 10 54... 5813 idnes Wham 6 2 3 1... 10 10 50...16 10 62... 4P66 ,tradford hdale W 5 2 3 0... 13 15 71... 6 10 42... 4010 5 2 3 0... 8 7 37...12 9 51... 4000 :!arringtbn 7 2 4 1... 5 6 28...12 10 54... 3571 'larllX t ri,_ 1</p>
        <p>C,ttrroW 6 1 4 1... 4 5 23...13 14 68... 2509 )1441gh 6 1 5 0... 4 G 26... 611 45... 16 66 qncorn 6 1 5 0... 58 34... 9 16 66... 1666 5 0 5 0... 4 4 20...17 25 109... 0000 'entry 'eighley 3 0 3 0... 4 3 17...13 16 74... 00'00</p>
        <p>am more than hopeful that Warrington will I;l3laic° the hearts of their supporters by recording I.heir first home victory this afternoon. The defeats Salford and Wigan were both bitter " pills " while '9e loss of a point to Broughton was galling. It's a Pity we cannot meet these two Manchester Clubs ligain at Wilderspool. They both caught the Wire- at their worst. To-day's visitors, Rochdale f'ce'nets, have only won once here in my recollection, `ticl they should be made to bite the dust to-day.</p>
        <p>L Warrington are making a big experiment at half k, but seeing that I should have favoured the 'election of last Saturday's " A "team pair, I cannot jaav,il at the selection. Nicholas, the young Welsh alt showed any amount of cleverness last Saturday 1,4 a hie first game, and promises better than any half ek Warrington have tried for years. Old hands are t'ready comparing his methods to those of" Smack ' ate, and with youth his side he may yet prove as ftteat a player. Certainly he handles the ball and feeds backs better than any man wearing the Warrington 111 el. Rushton is to play outside half, and this is h a experiment I feel doubtful about. Neither man 4PPears strong on the defence, and it will not be all tEttack against Rochdale. But a little of Nicholas icaY go a long way, if he gets the opportunities from forwards—and the latter are the best six 'arringtou can put in the field.</p>
        <p>The team is: Jolley, full back; Waywell, Renwick, ranter and Woodburn, threequarters ; Nicholas and ukhton, hall backs ; G Thomas, Skelhorn, Chester, 611.111eY, Cox, and R Thomas forwards. •</p>
        <p>b•The " A " team is also a strong force, now that 141ekenson has taken them in hand, and they should a big chance with Rochdale " A." The team ---Jordan, full back ; Clare, Carter, Dickenson, lat'anlTwist, threequarters ; Douglas and Daintith, half ; Critchlev, Bevan, Naylor, Cooper, Gibbs and 4k, forwards]</p>
        <p>---- 11°cIldale Hornets have selected the following to re, Vesent them at Warrington : tit) 'eed, full back ; Paddon, Jones, Robinson,Williarn- tP, tlareequarters ; Schofield, Prudence, half backs; od. o . or 8, Fitzstmmons, Dearden, Roman, Carter, Siade, Turner, forwards.</p>
        <p>---- 13ornets " A " v Warrington " A" at Rochdale : ollinrake ; West, Burt al, Lawson, Wilson ; T Bamber ; Mullin, Ashworth,Kerbhaw,Pearson, ichardson, Green, or Brumsley.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES. Ow 4.0 mg to the first round of the Lancashire Cup, the i„gramme is again a mixed one. The following is a of the fixtures and to those of the League matches rb agt aPPended the results of the corresponding games SeaSOII :</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CUP. Salford v Barrow Runcorn v Oldham Wigan v Leigh Swinton v Broughton Rangers Widnes v Coventry</p>
