      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Results of Saturday's Matches.</p>
        <p>YORKSHIRE CUP—FiRsT ROUND. G. T. P. Leeds 2 5 19 Brariley Hull Kingston R. 4 1 11 Huddersfield Hull Keiehley Batley</p>
        <p>G. T. P. 1 2 8 0 1 3 1 1 5 1 1 5 3 2 12</p>
        <p>- NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. St Helens 3 5 21 Widnes 1 0 2 Wigan Swinton Hunslet 5 8 34 Coventry 3 1 9 Rochdale H 2 1 7 Runcorn Warrington Broughton 11 NORTHERN RUGBY COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>Leeds A 1 4 14 Hunslet A 2 3 14 Leigh A 18 Broughton R.A. 1 1 5 Batley Featherstone It Wigan A — 21 Salford A 2 0 4 Wakefield T A 0 0 0 Purston W H ... 0 0 0 Oldham A --17 Swinton A Wigan Hightield... —.26 Pemberton Huddersfield A ... 12 Rochdale H. A 1 1 5 St. Helens A — 11 Widnes A 1 0 2 Warrington's Narrow Win at Barrow.</p>
        <p>Warrington took a somewhat changed team to Barrow on Saturday, but the side could not be considered any weaker for the introduction of Woodburn (for the first time this season), Waywell and Daintith in the back division. R Thomas had not returned from Wales, so the Warrington forward division was made up by the inclusion of Fearnley. Barrow had a full side doing duty, and were confident of the issue in the light of their draw at Widnes the week before. Beautiful weather prevailed, and there was a crowd of fully 5,000 spectators. Teams: Warrington—Jolley, back ; Woodburn, Tranter. Renwick and Waywell, threequarter-backs ; Rushton and Dainteth, half-backs ; G Thomas, Skelhorne, Chesters, Bevan, Cox and Fearnley, forwards. Barrow—Gifford, back ; Morgan, Dobson, Mitchell, and Bamber, threequarter-backs ; Narey and Treloar, balf-backs ; Wilkinson, Tomlinson, Mooney, Parr, Knowles and Brockbank, forwards. Referee, Mr J W Whitely (Wakefield) ; touch-judges, Messrs W Hall (Holme) and W Cross (Preston). Barrow had the advantage of the wind and slope in the first half, and were early assailing the Warrington line, but met a grand defence set up hyJolley, Renwick and Tranter. The latter burst through the Barrow backs and sent the ball along to Woodburn, but the wing was hemmed in. Warrington's front rank now made an impression, and Rushton and Dainteth getting the ball away, the borne defence was sorely taxed. Treloar and Bamber broke away to centre, but Renwick made an admirable return and kept play in safe quarters. A penalty to Warrington gave them a fine opportunity of opening the score, but Renwick missed the goal. Rushton, Renwick. and Waywell combined, the wing man being forced in touch at the corner by Morgan. Barrow now took up the running, and Narey was slow in getting the ball away when near the Warrington line. The same player had a shot at goal, but missed, and again the Warrington forwards raced to thA other end. Thomas and Cox were both nearly over, but fine tackling by Gifford and Morgan saved the situation. Bamber attempted to dodge clear on the Barrow right, but Woodburn downed his opponent promptly. Renwick again had a shot at goal but could not find the mark, and the interval arrived with no score. The second half opened like the first with Barrow on the attack. The Warrington defence never wavered, however, and Tranter was cheered for a wonderful clearance when it seemed odds on Barrow scoring. Daintith cleared, but a slice of luck came in the way of the homesters. Warrington were penalized and Dobson took the kick at goal. He failed at the first attempt, but for some infrin2einent Barrow were allowed to take another kick, and this time they were more fortunate, Dobson steering the ball between the uprig'ats. This reverse caused the Warringtonians to bestir themselves, and they immediately swooped clown ou the opposing goal, and a little more discretion in several of the movements would have yielded some- thing substantial. Play was keen and stoppages were frequent, Rushton being badly knocked and carried off, but was able to resume after a few minutes. Barrow adopted safety tactics and looked liked carrying their lead to the end, snore especially when Gifford beat off a most determined attack. How- ever, the visitors displayed any amount of pluck, and the ball being sent across in the direction of Wood- burn, he executed a magnificent dribble along the touch line from halt-way, and, was in the act of making the try when be was badly obstructed. Referee Whiteley immediately awarded a try. The goal-kick was a failure. The closing stages of the game went in favour of Warrington, whose forwards maintained a capital turn of speed until the close. Result : Warrington one try (three points) ; Barrow, one goal (two points) ST. 1-IELENS v. WIDNES.—A SCRAPPY GAME.</p>
        <p>This League game attracted a " gate " of 10,000 to the St. Helens ground. The proceedings were marred by the introduction of a large amount of undue rough- noes. In the opening half Prescott, Trenwith, and Waddell added tries for St Helens, and Turtill kicked two goals. At the interval St Helens finished with eleven players and Widnes with ten. Crossing over, St</p>
        <p>Helens lost Trenwith and Molyneux and Widnes were without Taylor (injured) and Higgins, who bad been an offender in the opening half received marching orders. Coserove kicked a goal from a mark for Widnes, and St Helens increased their score as the result of two grand tries, gained by Tren with and Prescott, one of which was improved by 'Curtin. Result :—St Helena. 3 goals 5 tries (21 points) ; Widnes, lgoal (2 points). Teams and officials : St Helens— rurtill, back; Woods, Greenwood Browning, and Fisher, tbree-quirter hacks • Molynenx and Trenwith. half backs ; Waddell, PrescAt, Mavitty, Butterfield, Manchester, and IVilcook, forwards. Wicines—Brassington, back; Laws, Pennington, Taylor. and Swinton. three-quarter hacks: O'Gara and Noon, half backs G Aspey S Aspey, Higgins, Cos- grove, Reid and H O'Gara, forwards. Referee, E Tong.. Swinton. Touch judges, H Lowe, Broughton, and J Valentine, Salford. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
