      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ALTRINCIIAM LADS V. LYAIM._</head>
        <p>ALTRINCIIAM LADS V. LYAIM._</p>
        <p>At Moss-lane, Altrincharn on Saturday. The opening was a sensational one, ROSS scoring a brilliant goal for Altrincham Lads in the first minute, only for Barber to reply for Lymm with an equalizer before the game was five minutes old. The exchanges continued fast and even. At the end of fifteen minutes' play Barber securing, easily defeated Hamnett and placed his side in front. Both sets of backs indulged in some tall kicking, without any advantage. On the whole the Lads had slightly the best of matters up to the interval, when the score was Lymm, three goals ; Altrincham Lads, two goals. After the interval play was interest- ing, and Barber again found the net for the visitors and Johnson fur Altrincham Lads. Final : Lymm, four goals; Altrincham Lads, three goals. BROADHEATH v. OUGHTRINGTON AMATEURS. At Navigation-road on Saturday. This was a capitally contested game all through, play being bright and interesting. The scoring was of a most sensational nature in the first half, and although 10 goals in all were scored, yet the respective defences could not be said to have played badly. Tbe shooting of each set of forwards was remarkably good. Final : Broadheath, seven goals; Oughtrington Amateurs, three goals. The goal-scorers were : tor Broadheath, Walton two, Peacock two, Buckley, Holt and Clegg, and for the Amateurs, Gresty two and Owen one. OCGIITRINGTON PARK V. BARNTOY VILLA. At Heatley on Saturday. The defence of each side proved far superior to the attack, with the result that the game ended in a goalless draw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. for agst Pts Weaver Refining Co 4... 2... L.. 1... 4... 6... 5 Latchford Reserve '2... 1... 0... 1... 6... 0... 3 Latchford Rees 3... 1... 1... 1... 6... 3 Newton Common A 3... 1... 1... 1... 8... 6... 3 Padgate Amateurs 4... 1... 2... 1... 9... 9... 3 Stockton Heath Latchford Britannia 3... 1... 2... 0... 3... 9... 2 Oughtrington Amt. 1... 1... 0... 0_ 5... 0... 2 Cadislaead Wesleyan 3... 0... L.. 2... 1_ 5... 2 Warrington CLB 0... 0... 0... 0... 0... 0... 0 LATCHFORD RECREATION V. PADGATE AMATEUR S On Saturday, at Latchford. Padgate had by far the best of the opening exchanges, and opened the score through Millward, the same player adding a second point shortly after. Before the interval the Recs. were awarded a couple of penalties, but missed from both. Half-time : Padgate Amateurs, two goals ; Latchford Recreation, nil. Resuming, the Recreation opened strongly and when Ditchfield opened their account it was fully deserved. On restarting the Amateurs rushed down and one of the home backs handling, Hardman made no mistake from the consequent penalty, once more plscing his side two goals to the good. Play now ruled fast, each goal having narrow escapes. After severe pressure the Recs scored two goals (one from a penalty) in rapid succes- sion and placed the sides on equal footing once again. Interest was maintained right up to the close, as it was only in the last minute or two of the game that Midge broke through and for the first time gave the Recs. the lead and victory of a most sensational game with the score :—Latchford Rees, four goals ; Padgate Amateurs, three goals.</p>
        <p>WIDNES ALLIANCE LEAGUE, GREAT SANKEY V. RUNCORN ALBION. Played at Sankey on Saturday. The locals, who did Most of the pressing were rewarded when E Forster scored with a splendid shot. Runcorn showed some pretty combination, and only the locals' fine defence saved them from disaster. However, Sankey maintained their aggressive attitude, E Forster scoring a second. T Whitfield was fouled in the penalty area when about to shoot and E Forster scored from the ensuing kick Half time : Great Sankey,three goals; Runcorn Albion nib—Restarting, the visitors went away in fine style but E Glover, who had been playing a splendid game, cleared with rare judgment. Nice combination by the Sankey forwards put T Whitfield in possession, and after beating several opponents he scored with a low drive. Three minutes from the finish W Booth added a fifth for the locals. Result : Great Sankey, five goals Runcorn Albion, nil.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Wartum.roN ExcELsions v. WEsToN A.F.C. The above match took place last Saturday and was a strain upon the Excelsiors who were defeated by three goals to nil. The Excelsiors must be complimented on their good display. Weston did not deserve the credit of the whole of the points owing to their rough and somewhat dirty play.</p>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS V. " GUARDIAN " OFFICE. At Lovely-lane. The visitors kicked off with the sun in their eyes, making a rush they forced a corner which was of no avail. The ball was taken to the other end, Challinor soon finding the net. Shortly afterwards Vernon centred, Challinor heading in a second. Half-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>INEXT WEEK: SHOOTING ON THE MOORS.</head>
        <p>INEXT WEEK: SHOOTING ON THE MOORS.</p>
        <p>time, St Barnahaa 2 goals, "Guardian " nil. Resuming even play in midfield was the order for a long time till at last Millington broke away along the right wing and scored. Results :—St Barnabas, 3 goals, "Guardian," 0.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. BANK QUAY RECSy..ORFORD ST. BENEDICT'S.</p>
        <p>Goal, Wa—rhurton ;fnll backs.R Butler and G Redford; half backs. W Appleton, T Worrell and A Keenan ; forwards, H Eccleston, R Spencer, E Myles, J Smith, P Long. EMMANUEL INSTITUTE V WARRINGTON ADULT SCHOOL.—To he played no Arpley Recreation ground at 3.30 pm. Emmanuel Institute: Knight ; Fairhurst. S Hughes: Webb. Hamilton, Ayres; Ashley, Lloyd, T Hughes Mather, Banner. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Result of match, October 12th, Liverpool Farm school 3, Emmanuel Institute 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS ASSOCIATION. Final for the " Creenall Cup."</p>
        <p>Although King Football has been with us for some weeks, he has not as yet monopolised all the interest taken in sports and pastimes, and a large number of bowling enthusiasts assembled on the Horse and Jockey Green on Saturday to witness the final stages in the tenth annual main promoted by the Warrington Works Association. The green was in fine condition and the bowling was of an exceptionally good quality. The " Greens,' "Cup was eventually won by W Gibbons (Crosflelds) who beat J Spilsbury (Whitecross) by four points. The full details of the scores of the games played were as follows :</p>
        <p>SET ENTH ROUND. T Hardman. P&amp; K 15 A Brown, Locker'a l2 J Bibby. CLC l5 J Shaw, Rylands' J Spilsbury, Whitecross 15 G Jones, Whiteoross l2 H Mather, Locker's J Broadhurst, Fletcher's 15 J Clarke, Cable ...... E Clare, Longford l5 W Pilkington, It'm'nds 13 W Gibbons, Crosftelds H Langley wo R Warburton, R'b'ns... ab ...._</p>
        <p>, EIGHTH ROUND.' W Gibbons E Clare l5 H Langley J Spilbury T Hardman l5 H Mather 5</p>
        <p>SEM I-EINAL. 3 Salisbury l5 E Clare l2 W Gibbons l5 T Hardman</p>
        <p>FINAL. W Gibbons</p>
        <p>THE SEMI FINAL. J Spilsbury had a hard task before him to dispose of E Clare (Longford) but be accomplished his difficult feat in 16 ends to the tune of 15-12. The players kept together up to the 11th end when the score was 10 each. Spilsbury gained one on the next end but Clare gained the lead on the following end when he ran a couple well on the jack. The wireman peeled with a single, and running the block on a good length, ran oat with two singles and a double. Gibbons had an easier thing on with T Hardman, (P and K) for he romped home in 13 ends a sure winner by eight points. Gibbons had a lead of 12-5 at the Bth end, having scored on all but one end. He bad little difficulty in maintaining a good lead up to the finish. THE FINAL. _</p>
        <p>Excitement ran high when the finalists took the green for Spilsbury had only an advantage one point over his opponent in the handicap and everyone felt that a keen tussle was to be witnessed. Yavour seemed to rest slightly with the Vhitecross player who, however, got a little over confident when he landed a couple on the first end and ran up to 7-5 in four ends. Gibbons played a floe, cool game, " felt "every length, and was in no way anaken or discouraged by the assurance of his 'opponent. Spilshury led 10-7 at the eighth end but at the 12th end, Gibbons peeled with 11. Both men played good bowls and many block touchers were landed. Spilsbury played a good round peg success- fully on several occasions. On the last four lengths Gibbons managed to get his necessary four with a quartette of singles, Spilsbury not having found the block from the 10th end when his advantage was 11-8. The soapery man was loudly cheered for his accomplish- ment and the players and spectators then adjourned to a corner of the green where Captain George Croklield presented the cup. THE PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. WILLETT (chairman of the committee) in calling upon their president, Captain Crosfield, to pre- sent the cup said it was rather unfortunate that the finalists had come from neighbouring works. However, Crosfields had pulled it off again and he thought Mr. Gibbons had played a good game all through the com- petition, as, also had his partner Mr. Spilsbury. The final game had been a good one, although in finals the players were apt to be rather too careful. They were pleased to have their president with them for he was heart and soul in the association and took a keener interest in the sport now than he ever did. (Applause). Captain CROSFIELD said he understood from their ever zealous secretary Mr. Patrick, that they had no fewer than 602 entries in that competition as against 433 for the previous year. He also under stood that they had got no less than £7O for prizes, headed by the very generous gift of .£5 ss. from Lady Greenall. With reference to the fine contest they had just witnessed, of course he could not help echoing what Mr Willett had said that be was pleased at the old firm winning the competition again. (Applause.) He congratulated the winner on the good game he had played and also the runner-up on the pluck and determination he had displayed. He hoped they all realised and appreciated the hard work which had been done by the Committee in controlling that</p>
        <p>great competition. The atrocious weather had thrown the final late and the Committee had done uncommonly well to get through the whole competition in 10 weeks (Applause). He then handed the cup to the winner amidst loud cheering. Mr GIBBONS briefly returned thanks. Mr WILLETT proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Captain Crosfield and said they were fortunate to secure Captain Crosfleld's services as he was a very busy ma o and had very few week-ends at home. (Hear, hear.) When they could get gentlemen like him to come down and take an interest in the game of bowls for the betterment of the working class, they were indeed pleased. (Hear, hear.) Several gentlemen connected with big firms had said the com- petition was a good and useful one. The men went straight home from their work on Saturday noon with their wages and gess, money to their wives, and then set out to enjoy a game of bowls. On the Monday morning they went to their work better men, not full of beer. The employers got far more work out of them under such conditions. (Hear, hear.) The men who were in that association were making better men, better fathers and better husbands because they were kept on the green on Saturday afternoons and did not get too much of the stuff which knocked a man down so that he could not get up again. Mr. T. DEAN seconded and the vote was heartily carried.</p>
        <p>_ Captain CROqFIELII, in reply, said he saw row the value of the game of bowls, which ten years ago he had thought rather slow. It was a splendid spurt for the men who bad been working hard all the week and they fully deserved to enjoy their sport. The loafer did not enjoy life and he was glad to know that there were so many men in Warrington taking part in that fine game. He hoped we should have still greater facilities in Warrington for men to play the game of bowls. (Hear, hear). The formal presentation of all the ether prizes--151 in number—will take place at the Horse and Jockey on Octobet 26th, when a smoking concert will be held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>HELSBY V. LYMM LADIES. The Helsby Ladies' Club met the Lymm Royal Ladies' Club at Lynam on Saturday, and after a capital game suffered defeat by two goals to one. Results : Stockport A 1 'runnerlev A Timperley B 4 Stockport B Crosfield's 4 Sale Moor 2 Levenshulme Wigan 3 Weaste 3 Wigan 2nd 2 Alic Y.M.C.A. 2nd 2 Urmston 2nd Swieton Church 5 Broughton St John's... 0</p>
