      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>The War in the East is MR. LLOYD the subject of greatest GEORGE AND interest for the moment. THE LAND But undoubtedly the QUESTION. Land Question is a topic</p>
        <p>that will outlast even the War in the East. The mind of the nation will always be coming back to it until it is settled on something that approaches an ' equitable basis. The matter crops,up every , day in the House of Commons at the present time, as it did on Tuesday in particular. Mr. lloyd George is supposed to have in hand .some very sweeping proposals. He'-was des- cribed the other day by the Leader of the Opposition as being an almost unexampled demagogue. This was in advance of any proposals that he has to make in regard to land. Nobody knows at present what his proposals are to be; but his political opponents do not hesitate to give to them a am</p>
        <p>Le and a description. The name is "Con- rfiscation," and the description is "Single Tax." It would be merely reasonable to permit a Minister of the Crown to describe his own proposals before denouncing them. That, however, is not the practice followed by a party which has a bitter feeling about its own exclusion from power. The Opposition in Parliament takes it for granted that, any proposals made in regard to the land question by Mr Lloyd George must be wicked and confiscatory in the extreme. It assumes that he means to advocate the application of the Single Tax to the land conditions of our country. The fact that the Prime Minister has stated in the most explicit manner that jib member of the Government is in favour' 1 aeqi the Single Tax does not seem to matter. The Opposition appears to have adopted the principle that if a falsehood is useful it ought not to be dropped because it is a falsehood.</p>
        <p>The Chancellor of the Exchequer, it need not be said, is not a Single Taxer. He is not Henry George. He has not adopted the opinion of that very remarkable man. It may be pretty safely assumed that he has a more remarkable, at the same time a clearer, mind than the author of the Single Tax proposal with which he does not agree, and with which the Prime Minister, who ought to know, says that the Government does not agree. This surely ought to be enough. It should not be necessary to go on saying day by day that the Government is not a Single Tax Govern- ment. The fact might be taken for granted after such very definite declarations on the subject. However, it is not taken for granted for the simple reason' that "his Majesty's Opposition "does not want to believe, and is very determined not to believe, or (to put the case on its exactly right basis) is resolutely bent on persuading the public that it does not believe that the Government and the Prime Minister, and Mr Lloyd George in particular, are anything else but advocates of the Single Tax. Well we may as well let the pretence go for what it is worth. It is certainly not worth much. No Single Tax proposal will come , from the present Government. It is doubtful whether any such proposal will come from any succeeding Government. The thing is not practicable, and it would not be just. But it is quite a different thing to ask whether the landowner should walk off with the whole of his profits without making an y contribution to the cost of local government. The Selfridge firm has to pay £lO,OOO in ground rent for its premises in Oxford-street. Ten thousand a year is a large fortune. It was supposed to be almost fabulous when Warren wrote his novel under that title. It is a small thing</p>
        <p>now, because some owners of land make a million a year or more. But ten thousand pounds is an uncommonly big thing. Why should not the recipient pay something towards the expenses of the municipality from which he derives this income? That is the question which Mr Lloyd George will put to the country when his mind is free from other important matters, and really we do not see how any reply is to be given which does not suggest a large con- tribution to rates or taxes. The impertinent questions put to the Chancellor of the Exchequer do not impede the coming forward of this sublect. They hasten it.</p>
        <p>One cannot do other TOWN PLANNIKG. than bless the Corpor-</p>
        <p>ation scheme for town- planning. In its details it may be open to criticism, but the broad prin- ciple will be cordially and thank- fully endorsed by every inhabitant who wishes to see the borough extend on modern lines, and co-incidently eliminate those congested areas and inaccessible localities which are a legacy of bygone generations when towns merely grew—like Topsy—without care, let or hindrance. The Warrington scheme embraces 1,476 acres of unbuilt land within the borough, and no doubt much can be done to improve the town in many directions. The Mayor very properly deplored the present ineffective measures for dealing with slum areas and sanitation, and voiced a hopeful feeling in view of future legislation. But the sine qua non for a town-planning scheme which will earn the gratitude of posterity is full co-operation between the municipality and neighbouring rural authori- ties. Fortunately the mental attitude of the rural councils is an enlightened one, if the pace of their movements is slow.</p>
        <p>The inspiring Liberal AN INSPIRING rally in the Co-opera- RALLY. tive Hall on Wednes-</p>
        <p>day evening showed that—Tory braggadocio notwithstanding— the local progressive forces are in excellent fighting trim, and full of enthusiastic loyalty to the people's cause. Seldom has there been a more interesting political meeting in Warrington, for these are stirring and heartening days for all ardent reformers, and the crowded audience had the satisfaction of hearing a variety of important topics expounded in a forceful and convincing way. Mr. A. H. Cros- field who, as usual, had a rousing welcome, expressed himself in no uncertain way on the grave situation in the Balkans, and we feel sure he will carry all friends of freedom and justice with him in his emphatic declaration that good government of those long-suffering States can only be secured by diminishing the control of the Turk over the Christian ' peoples. Naturally Mr. Crosfield had some- thing to say about the Home Rule con- troversy, shortly to be crowned with the attainment of Ireland's long cherished ambitions, and he declined to take seriously the " colossal bluff" of "King" Carson and his satellites, whose recent performances in Ulster have merely added to the gaiety of nations and the disgust of serious politicians. From sturdy, genial Tom Wing, the meeting was treated to a powerful speech, illuminated with flashes of homely wit, on some of the more pressing domestic problems, including land reform, which promises to be the all- dominating issue in the !near future. The ex-M.P. for Grimsby also threw out a cballenge to Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. to debate the fiscal question and it will be interesting to see whether the Member will avail himself of this opportunity of explain- ing how to "make the foreigner !pay "and to cheapen things by taxing them. Mr. P.M. Oliver, who is fighting a courageous battle in the Knutsford division, spoke in his usual lucid and incisive manner on electoral reform and sounded the right note by emphasising the great tresponsibility which manhood suffrage will cast upon the young men.</p>
        <p>Whilst there is a great WOMEN AND agitation among women MUNICIPAL for claiming the Parlia- AFFAIRS. mentary vote, 1 here is a good deal of apathy concerning the franchise which many of them possess in regard to municipal elections. This is not so marked in the provinces, as it is in London, where there exists the greatest indifference to civic politics, and local govern- ment affairs. Every woman " occupier "of land or house, of a yearly •rental of not less than £lO, has a right to the vote in municipal elections, and two women together Occupying a house or flat provided both pay not less than £lO a year rent, may each be registered as "joint occupiers," but husband and wife cannot each vote in request of the same premises. Any woman, who has the right to vote at a county or borough council elec- tion, may also ofter herself as a candidate, but as yet very few have come forward, and there are only about twenty women in all the county and borough councils, of the United Kingdom. It does not seem likely that the number will be materially increased at the forthcoming November elections, although there are, in nearly all communities, women of independent means, with the time, education and intelligence to give to public affairs, whilst there is much useful work which specially calls for women's interest, and services, such as the administration of the Health, Housing Acts, the management of Baths and Wash-houses, and the sanitary arrangements in laundries, workshops, and workplaces, where women and giils are employed.</p>
        <p>It is stated on the A CONFERENCE ON authority of M. Jaures, FOOD PRICES, that the Governments</p>
        <p>of Britain ane. the United States are contemplating the summon- ing of an international conference on the subject of the univeral rise in the prices of food. The problem is a world-wide one, and if the accumulated wisdom and experience of experts from two great nations are calculated to ease the burden the harrassed householder will cordially welcome the proposed confer- ence. Food prices have risen, all over the world during the past decade—and the pros- pect for the coming winter is by no means an alluring one. The draught last year, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>the wet weather this summer, are two lore" disposing causes, and their effect will ue found during the coming month in regard to bacon, milk, cheese, butter; and probablY bread. Record prices have been offered bY I importers for the whole of the dairy pr°," duce of New Zealand, and the demano for bacon is greater than the supPl27' American and Continental farmers were coin" , pelled last year, owing to the cost of feeding stuffs to slaughter immature stock, wllicii, , has resulted in depleting the markets, on' another cause of dearer bacon is that work' ' ing men have ceased to keep pigs to the extent they used to do. Sanitary require' ' ments are now more stringent than in the I past, and local authorities will not permit ! pig-sty close to the back-door. This is very good thing for public health, but bno for the man who likes a rasher of bacon With his breakfast.</p>
        <p>a</p>
        <p>A well-known publi AN AMERICAN man in New Jet-Se ELECTION STORY. U.S.A. (Dr. W. Saunders) has made ,a keen assault on the Protective tariff, whicus, has been put up step by step," he says, " the demand of small groups of men who we to Washington with axes to grind." lie tells this story : "A man stood knocking at the gates of beacee,i Saint Peter called out, 'Who's there? and receivf; the reply that a prominent American Protectiono" wished to enter. 'Are you afoot or on horse' hack? ' asked Peter, and being told that he wae afoot he was denied admission, for Peter said that no one could get in that day who was not mounted' As this Republican sped disconsolate down fleecy path he met a workman who told him the' be was on his wit 7 to the sates of bliss. There is no use your going,' said the other, for I hal just been there and have it frcm St. Peter hira6el, that no one can enter who is not mounted. I'll tell you what we can do. You get down en fours. I'll get on your back, and together we CS; go to the golden gates. When Peter asks ill 13,' mounted I'll say yes, and then the gates will he opened to us.' So on they went, the question wile asked, the answer given, and old Peter promPt'l ' replied, 'Just hitch your horse outside and come "It looks very much," concludes the writer, "as though the record on earth has shown that the golden gates of prosperity have been opened to those who have used a tariff keY for their purpose, while the American workman has been the horse or the donkeY who still stands hitched outside."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>"In modern so-called democratic societies, representative Government is little better than a farce, a cruel mockery, simPlY because the one effective weapon in politics to-day, i.e. material wealth, is controlled bY the few and not by the many."—Rev. Stanlel A. Mellor on "Religion and Democracy" a Cairo-street Chapel. 4, "If every time we bought an article we could pay the price it would cost without tariff, and should then be compelled to male,,t down to the Custom House and pay wriv the tariff comes to, then we would quic,'n's become aware of just what the tariff nu`e for us, and we would find a way to chaak it."—Dr. Woodrow Wilson at New 170 T September 9. • * • pa The "Morning Post," it should be net-4 declares that" prior to the introduction .; the Home Rule Bill the whole eount"i including Ireland, was in a state of profonn" peace." That is at all events a pretty e°113,,11 pliment to six years of Liberal rule, as , as a damaging comment on the " LoYal's in allegation that a large part of Ireland vf 38 a state of disorder and terrorism.</p>
        <p>• • Mr. Lloyd George's reference on SaturdaY_ to the circumstance that he once earned 3. " "honest if precarious" living on the Pal; mentary staff of a well-known journal reco, the cases of not a few other public men sta in the earlier stages of their careers, in the Gallery of the House of CoinrOgir The late Mr. Justin McCarthy was oe, p. Edward Clarke was another, Mr. l• of O'Connor was a third, and Lord Russell a Killowen, if we are not mistaken, Walor fourth, and this list could be considera extended.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. Fulton Smith was discussing ,e Scottish Veto Bill before the Conserval'' e Club members on Tuesday evening, when made the following remarkable statenlen,o "In Warrington they had little reason 06 t I complain, because the people, if they wee It aware of it, were the most sober in Engibni;r, The local breweries must be doing 1)3- though annual balance sheets do not reve 'the fact. If Mr. Smith has any definite' are, reliable information in support of his sta',,, ment, we are sure it would be welcomed " an the Temperance League, which hold° us, entirely opposite, but possibly erroneo view of the sobriety of Warrington. * • Aeral Bill has been introduced in the F,eu, for Parliament of Australia making provlso. the granting of State pensions to The Prime Minister of the CornmonA' has already carried a scheme eau°. ear. mothers at a cost of about £600,000 a Yitici- Australia has many forms of pensions, Woo, ing Civil Service Pensions, old-age PerlAs aY9 journalists' pensions, etc. New ZealantA Pnder £l2 a year for every fatherless child uhild- fourteen years of age, £lB for two such cowl ren, £24 for three, and £3O for more nion- three. Some of the States of the Cara'their wealth already assist widows by makingrding children wards of the State, and h°3 ens. them out with their parent or other Pers * * The news that Buc*kingham Palace °foil length, to have a new front,recalls how„ liope the old House has suffered from op deferred." Just over a hundred years co lirt 1811, we find one of the ladies of the at ttio writing, "I was one of the hapPY feTieuse ball given in Buckingham House, a ~dered had been long anxious to see, as it _ is lii of classical by the pen of Pope and the P:,ditioo Hogarth. . . . It is in a woeful„o"tirteen and 1 hear it is to be pulled down. viatcshrly°r,il hand and d later etvheen then nentit biLilsdinag pa t cool_ affair. It was feared that Parlialnanentireq, never vote the funds necessary for ap_odirig, new palace, and only a " clunis3',.lLuensiona retaining the old height, site, and mil' was possible.</p>
        <p>The smoke nuisance does not seem to have inund its way into any of the election addresses of candidates for municipal honours,, which suggests that public opinion still remains very apathetic on the subject. Alderman Bennett has drawn pertinent attention to the question in a letter,published elsewhere, in which he says he "will not vote for any candidate who is not able to satisfy him that he is prepared to carry out t.he law regarding this important subject instead of winking at its evasion." It is getting rather late in the day, but questions Might certainly be put to the various gentle- men who are seeking the suffrages of the electors.</p>
        <p>The deanery of Durham is worth £3,000 a Year, with a delightful residence. The Dean is ex-officio Warden of Durham University. ,i),ean Lake, who preceded Dean Kitchin, held Balliol College living of Huntspill, 'omersetshire, for some years before Mr. Gladstone appointed him to the deanery in 1869 on the death of Dean Waddington, who was a friend of Macaulay, and a very cele- brated bon vivant. His cellar of wines had a European reputation. Dean Waddington, who was appointed in 1840 by Lord Melbourne, had succeeded Bishop Jenkinson, Who held this deanery in commendam with the See of St. Davids. Until 1840 the average value of the deanery of Durham was about £9,000 a year, and when Dean Lake was aPpointed,his friend Archbisop Tait described the post as being " decidedly the best place °f Preferment in the Church of England."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>LOCAL PARS.</head>
