        <p>-- kkr T to 11, Item_ ort ou—ke p the [wages of Time A B lay by ea% ,t hp Grey Streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPFIUR Ct'STOßEß.whichdeepensto a natural colouc in a few Yrltvb,°_ek)er' maintainn yonthlulanpeamnce arge hots tre. Post free 116. Bedford Laboratory, Lcuttor. S.E.</p>
        <p>Warrington Caledonian Association. The above association opened their winter session with a whist drive and dance at the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, on Thursday evening. Jud3ing from the number present, 132, and' the enthusiasm with which the whist and dance were indulged in, they look like having a successful winter season. Whist was played until 10 o'clock, when refreshments were provided by Mrs Joynson, of the cafe In the absence of the president, Mr J Fulton Smith, and vice- president, Mr J L Davenport, the duties of MC. fell on the able shoulders of the worthy and esteemed Dr Murray, who carried the whist drive and dance through to the satisfaction of all. Daring an interval in the dancing, Mrs W Murray presented the prizes to the following :—Ladies, Mesdames J Tennant, J Richmond, G Marshall, W Stirling, P Lyell, F Thompson, F Love. The Secretary presented Mrs Murry with her bowling prize. Gentlemen, Messrs W Fleming, F Love, J Houk. Ladies Whist prizes : 1, Mrs Crossley, 2, Mrs J Finlayson, 3, Mrs J Buchanan. Gentlemen : 1, Mr Webster. 2, Mr F Rathbone, 3, Mr J Crank. On the motion of Mr Bennett a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mrs Murray for presenting the prizes. Dancing was continued until one o'clock. The songs by Messrs Robertson and Marshall and recitations by Mr Hankev, were greatly appreciated and the Warrington Caledonians' favourite " It's just like bein' at Hame " was joined in by all. A most enjoy- able evening was brought to a close by singing of Auld Lang Syne. Mr B Smith was a most efficient accompanist. •</p>
