      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>GREAT SMITH PARTY.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT SMITH PARTY.</p>
        <p>RIVALS OF "BILL ADAMS."</p>
        <p>Sir Wilfrid Lawson, M.P., speaking at a Home Rule demonstration at Workington at the week-end, said that Home Rule seemed to him a very reasonable proposal; but everybody was not accepting it in Ireland. They had chosen a great leader in Ulster— Sir Edward Carson. (Laughter). He was not crowned yet, but he imagined that that would be almost the noxt thing—(laughter)-- and that as far as Ulster was concerned they would have a lineal successor to William of Orange—Edward the Eighth, (Laughter.) It was a case of" The Minstrel Boy " :</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Carson to the wars has gone, In the Orange ranks you'll find him ; His Union Jack he has girded on, And his blackthorn slung behind him. (Prolonged laughter). He wished to speak of Sir Edward with all respect ; he was a distinguished lawyer, and he hoped he would come to no harm as a result of anything that might happen. If he did encounter any peril, he thought he was more likely to fall with his drawn salary than with his drawn sword in his hand. (Laughter.) The heather was set on fire, and he had gathered to his standard the whole of one political party—the great Smith party. (Laughter.) The Smith party con- sisted of two English members of Parliament and they had sworn a great oath, that, if this sort of thing went on, they were going to take 10,000 men from Liverpool in three ships and finish the whole business before they could say " Jack Robinson." (Laughter.) Well, since the day Bill Adams won th- Battle of Waterloo, he had never heard anye thing to equal that. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>OUR POLITE M.P.</head>
        <p>OUR POLITE M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. was unable to attend the bowling prize distribution at the Conservative Club on Tuesday evening, so he sent a letter of apology in his best and most characteristic style. It contained the following choice sentiments: "Dear Sir Gilbert,—l wish I could join you at the smoking concert organized by the club bowlers. I cannot however, leave our match here, which looks like going on for a long time. We are playing against a dirty side, whose motto appears to be 'Win by fair means or foul.' We are playing on a green which our opponents have deliberately ruined, because with weeds, worm- casts and mud they feel more at home. They have done all they can to injure the crown,' because the Irish members of their side have always hated it. They have crowded the green with a great many more matches than it can accommodate, and are doing all they can to stifle criticism of their un-English methods. But, in spite of all the advan- tages which they thus obtain, we are beating them, and have already become the favourites of the crowd." (Applause).</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith prides himself on being some- what of a sportsman, but he has evidently forgotten that the player who always calls the other side "dirty ' is usually thought very little of by the "crowd " he seems so anxious to propitiate. Mr. Smith would do with a course of the " temper test" so well pictured in our Imps cartoon of last week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>CLOTHING OF A CARMELITE. Miss Cecilia Madden Takes the Veil.</head>
        <p>THE CLOTHING OF A CARMELITE. Miss Cecilia Madden Takes the Veil.</p>
        <p>A touching ceremony took place on Tuesday after- noon at the Carmelite Convent, Knotty Ash, a large building that stands in its own grounds just where the country begins to get the better of the town on the confines of Liverpool. It was the clothing of a young lady in the religious habit of the Order of Carmel by the Archbishop of Liverpool, (the Right Rev. Dr. Whiteside). The Carmelite Order is one of the oldest in existence, as it claims among its saints the prophet Elijah, or Elias, and one of its chief ' patronesses is the great St Teresa, of whom the poets have spoken so often. October 15th, St. Teresa's Day, therefore, was the feast chosen as the the most suitable one for the</p>
        <p>solemn receiving of the veil by Miss Cecilia Madden, the daughter of Mr. William Madden, barrister, who is so well known and esteemed in Warrington and district. - .</p>
        <p>In her earliest youth, and with the prospect tbai brilliant talents gave her, this gifted child has chosen to leave all behind and join one of the ancient orders of the Roman Catholic Church. As she entered the chancel, dressed in rich white satin with a magnificent train falling from her shoulders, she seemed in her wreath and bridal veil just like a lively bride on the day of her espousal. She was given away by her father, who, with her grandmother, Mrs Lynch,and her little sisters and brother, remained close to her during the trying and solemn ceremony. After the solemn intonation of Psalms and ,Canticles by the nuns, who could only be dimly descried through the large Gothic grille, the Bight Rev. Prior, Dr. Wilson, of Seel-street Church, Liverpool, gave an address, particularly to the aspirant novice herself. He took for his text the words of Our Blessed Lord " I have chosen you, not you Me," and exhorted her to persevere through the thorny road that leads from labour to peace. When the sermon was over the Archbishop, with many of the Liverpool clergy, walked in procession up the nave of the chapel, where he was vested with mitre, cope, and crozier After this the whole of the congregation left the building and gathered together in the courtyard to see the Archbishop and clergy descending the steps, followed by the white-robed novice and her relatives. Here in the open-air, and dressed for the last time in the garments of the world, she said" Good-bye "to her nearest and dearest and to all her surrounding friends. This time she passed into the cloisters at the right side of the chapel, not to be seen again but behind the grille. The Archbishop and clergy 0000 more entered the chapel, where, amid plaintive chants, the young novice received the robe of religion at the hands ,of the Arcnbishop: The ceremony concluded with the " Veni Creator Spiritus," the bymm to the Holy Ghost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
