      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Daily DiPpatelt Photo. A SNAP-SHOT .LT THE WEDDING OF DR. SUTHERLAND AND . MISS LONSDALE. , .</head>
        <p>Daily DiPpatelt Photo. A SNAP-SHOT .LT THE WEDDING OF DR. SUTHERLAND AND . MISS LONSDALE. , .</p>
        <p>Taylor Jones. pretty werVing was 'solemnised at the Parish Church. on Tueerli‘. when Mr Percy Besketh Taylor, youug,est son of Mr F Taylor, Green Bank, Manchester- 1 road, married Miss Mary E Jones, younger daughter of Mrs .A Gjoneti, Goyt Villa, Latchfo, d. There was a numerous company of relatives and friends present in the Church; and the' ceremony was performed by the Rey Canon Willis, assisted by the Rev A J Bratt, Mr _Darby officiating at the organ. The bride was becoiningly attired in ivory crepe de chine, with net veil and wreath of mange bloseom, and carried a bouquet of white heather and roses. She was given au ay by her brother, Mr W A Jones, the best man being Mr Arthur Taylor, brother of the bridegroom. Miss Gertrude Jones, sister of the bride, was brides- maid, and wore a dress of pale yellow satin, covered ui,ll figured ninon, and a black velvet hat; her bouquet was of mauve chrysanthemums. , After the ceremony a reception was held at Goyt Villa, and subsequently Mr and MI s Taylor left for a honeymoon in Wales. The bride travelled in a navy coat and skirt, and 'Jlack velvet hat.</p>
        <p>to do with the benefits under the Insurance Act he feels he is well qualified to study the workers' needs. St. John's Ward having no reasonable facilities in the trann route should participate in the motor 'bus service, which is being considered in Warrington. Mr Shaw would immediately press forward the Operation of half-penny tram fares. In his opinion lower paid Corp iration employees should be con- sidered before the higher paid officials. •LATCHFORD WARD. No Truth In the 1* Poor Man " Argument.</p>
        <p>Some of Councillor Wilsoa's friends have been making use of the" poor man" argument with regard to the position of Mayor's auditor which he has held ever since he entered the Council atid which has been worth 14s. or 1. per week to him. Owing to the fact riht the Corporation accounts came under Govern- ment Audit from March last, the offices of Mayor's auditor and elective auditor have been abolished, so thatCouncillor Wilson is no longer entitled to this use ful honorarium. Last year he received SAO This year he will receive nothing whether he remains a Councillor or not. The electors will consider their own interests by voting fur the best man —Mr E Wynne Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Sutherland—Lonsdale.</head>
        <p>Sutherland—Lonsdale.</p>
        <p>On Thursday t. Mary's Church Crumpsall wt.&amp; the' scene of a very pretty wedding, when alias Gladys Lansdale, , elder .daughter Of. Mr. and • Min. H. A. 0. Lonsdale, 70, Bury Old-road, Man- chester, was married t Dr. 'Robert Wilson Sutherland, 1, Balmvra , Square, Warrington. (hon. assistant stirgenn..Warrington Infirmary), ,on of Mr and Mrs George. SUtlierlanir, 3, Qusen Margaret- place, Glasgow. Dressed in white satin with Court train and wearing- a beautifully 'embroidered veil surmounted with orange ,blossom .and carrying a l sheath of white lilies, the bride looked very charming. The bridesmaids were Miss Muriel Lansdale, sitster of the bride, and Miss peril Biirries, who were very prettily aptired in pale blue 'silk, with black picture . hats, carrying ,bouquets of pink carnations. The ceremony was fully choral, with the Rev J Light- foot,• vicar of St. Mary's, and, the Rev 0 W Dickens -son. vicar of Padgate, rfficiating. After the wedding, 'a reception was held at The Lodge, Higher Broughton when a goodly company "Of guests were present. A large number of valuable 'presents had been received by the popular couple, who .later in the afternoon departed for North Wales Where the houeywoou will be spent. ' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CANDIDATES FOR TOWN HALL AND ST JOHN'S WARDS.</p>
        <p>There is great activity in several of the wards and keen fights are promised on Friday, November.lst. Only a few days remain for the parties to place all their candidates in the field, for Thursday next is the last day for the receipt of nominations at the Town Hall. During the past week there have been new• developments. Councillor Jackson Holmes has informed the Liberal Committee of the Fairfield Ward that, owing to ill-health, he will not offer himself for re-election. This brings the number of retiring members who have decided not stand to four, namely—Councillors 0 Toole (L) Bewsey Ward; J Sankey, (C), Howley Ward; W H Bellian (C) Whitecross Ward; and C Jackson Holmes (L), Fairfield Ward. An excellent Liberal candidate for Town Hall Ward is now forth- coming in the person of Mr Win Isaac who, we ate informed, will oppose Councillor W H J Jenkins. Last evening came the announcement that Mr Peter Delaney had been selected by the insurance workers as their candidate in this ward. In St John's Ward Mr Jae Shaw has,to the gratification of all who worked for him in previous elections, yielded to strong requests of a very large number of burgesses, to stand for a third time, and there is every prospect of his wresting the representation of this workingclass ward from Councillor T. S. Steel (C). As announced in our last issue Councillor W. Bellian, through " circumstances over which he has no control " will not seek the suffrages of the electors of Whitecross Ward, and the Conser- vatives have not not yet found a candidate to oppose Mr. Peter Peacock, the popular chairman of the Liberal executive, who has received splendid promises of support from all quarters. The following is the list of candidates up to date : Town Hall Ward: Councillor WH J Jenkins (C). Mr William Isaac (L). Mr. P. Delaney (I.) Whitecross Ward: Mr P Peacock (Li. Latchford Ward : Councillor W,T Wilson (Cl. Mr E Wynne Jones (L) St Austin's Ward : Councillor T H Ashcroft (C). Mr E Osborne (I). Fairfield Ward: Mr Samuel Taylor (C). Howley Ward : Mr John Chrimes (C). Bewsey Ward; Mr S Gough (I) Mr A C Binks (I). Orford Ward: Councillor E Cunnington (L). Mr W Bradbury (0). St John's Ward : Cotincillor T 6 Steel (C). Mr James Shaw (L).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>TOWN HALL WARD.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Committee of this ward have been fortunate in inducing Mr. William kaae, provision dealer, Buttermarket-street (who resides at 72, Bewsey- street), to stand as their candidate. As a business man and a resident who is familiar with all the needs of the ward, be would make a most capable represen- tative and, with the help of an enthusiastic band of workers, Town Hall Ward should, on November Ist, boast two progressive members. Mr Isaac will be formally adopted at a meeting of the Liberal Committee to be held in Wycliffe schools on Monday evening. INSURANCE WORKERS' CANDIDATE. At a meeting held at Friars Green schoolroom, yesterday afternoon, of insurance workers, it NVBS unanimously decided that Mr Peter Delaney be adopted as candidate for Town Hall Ward. MR. JAMES SHAW FOR ST. JOHN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>The Liberals of St. John's Ward are entering into the fray with the utmost enthusiasm. "Support one of your own class this time" is their message to the electors and it is regarded as certain that the third time will pay for all and that Mr Shaw will be at the head of the poll on November Ist. In his address Mr. Shaw rightly emphasises the fact that Bt. John's Ward above all others should be represented by a member of the protiressive party, particularly as the housing question, which is one of the foremost problems, is before the various municipal authorities. As one who has been all along closely connected with the interests of the child-life in the ward, having been labouring for over 40 years con- tinually for their social good, he claims to have a full knowledge of their requirements. lie is, however, in favour of them leaving school before the age of 14, in case of home necessity. For otter 30 years Mr Shaw has been officially connected with friendly society work, and as the Town Council will have a great deal</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>HOWLEY WARD.</head>
        <p>HOWLEY WARD.</p>
        <p>-- - Up to last evening the progressive forces, were without- a candidate for Howley Ward in opposition to Mr. Chrimes, the off-licence holder, who has been nominated by the " other side." We hope the seat will not be allowed to go by default. It will be a disgrace to Liberalism and to the temperance party in Warrington if a "trade " candidate is allowed to enter the Council unopposed. A a general rule licensees and men con- nected with the drink traffic in other capacities, are most unsuitable—though we should be the first to admit that thpre are exceptions—and the Council already contains more than a fair proportion of men connected with the licensed trade. Surely some public spirited townsman will be found to fight the battle of social progress and good government in Howley !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SUCCESS TO THE LIBERAL CANDIDATES.</head>
        <p>SUCCESS TO THE LIBERAL CANDIDATES.</p>
        <p>--- At the mass meeting held by the Warrington Liberal Association in the Co-operative Hall on Wednesday evening, Mr A H CEMPIELD expressed pleasure at the presence on the platform of several of those who were going to help the party in municipal matters and wished them every success in their candidatures. Referring to Mr Peter Peacock, he said it bad been a great access of strength to the Liberal party in Warrington to have him so energetically to the fore. (Applause). Mr. E. WysliE JONES, in seconding a vote of thanks to the speakers, said that duty was originally allotted to " Councillor" Peacock-(applause)—st least, his opponents could not get a candidate to fight him and, even if they did, he did not think there was much doubt about Mr Peac.lek winning Whitecross Ward.;Applause) He (the speaker) was going to make an atteMpt—and a successful iattempt—(applause)—to win Latchford. The opponents already seemed to be in an alarmed state, to inform the Latchford electors to what particular branch of the political forces he (the speaker) belonged. They seemed to forget that party politics ought not to enter 1 into municipal matters at all. The nomination paper did not ask whether a man was Libeml,Labour or Tory. The ballot paper simply asked them to vote for Jones or Smith. (Laughter.) They had to decide who was the better man and in this cause be ventured to say Jones was. Applause.) He hoped that when the result was known; it would be found that the Latchford electors were also of that opinion. (Applause.) Mr PETER PEACOCK said he was not "Councillor Peacock "yet, but he was going to be on November Ist. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>NOMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr Peter Peacock, traffic manager, 64, West-street, is I proposed by Alderman James Evans, seconded by Mr Thomas Dale. Assentors : Messrs John Henry Edwards, Michael Farrington. Thomas Gregson, Mark I Brookes, William Keen, Albert Mansell, Alfred Ernest Ashton, and Walter Mullin. Proposed by Captain George R Crosfleld,seconded by Rev Fr W Rockcliffe. Assentors : Messrs J T Dolan Joseph Mulhall, John Tennant, John Henry Elwell, James West, Charles Simpsou, Frederick Wm Tenniers. and George plum pton. Proposed by Councillor John Wilkinson, seconded by Mr John Upson. Assentors : Messrs Charles Edwin Walker, John Rossiter, John Whitworth, Elijah Brown, Thomas Lindley, Charles It Muir, W II Hartley and James Higham. Proposed by Mr William Charnock. seconded by Mr William Ilipkiss. Assentors : Messrs Joseph Bennion, it Hamblett, Thomas R Ashworth, James Eccles, James Sbaw, George W Birchall, Bertram B Ashbrook and John Batton. ST. JOHN'S WARD. Mr :lames Shaw, 83. Lyth goes-lane, tin plate worker, is proposed by Mr Richard Brown, seconded by Mr Martin Hughes, Assentors Messrs William John</p>
        <p>Hibbert, George Henry Payton, Thomas Shakeshaft Thomas Jackson, John fakin, William Alcock, Edwin Hill and John W Meachin.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr Samuel Gough, 106, Winwick-street, house furnisher, is proposed by Mr Joseph Bradbury, seconded by Mr William Bennett. Assentors : Messrs John Cunningham, Martin Gallagher, John Bennett, Joseph Poole, William Richmond, Thomas Connor, Thomas Wood and 4fartin Donlan.</p>
        <p>Proposecis,by Mr Charles Longsbavv, and seconded by Mr David Kendrick. Assentors : Messrs William Taylor, Robert MoKibbin, Samuel Barton, David Fotheringhamt William Baines, John Thomas Lloyd, John Alfred tlibby and Arthur Teggln. 4 ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr Elijah Connington, 32, Longford-street, Warring- ton, furniture dealer, is proposed by Mr Peter Peacooic, seconded by Mr William Spencer. Assentors : Messrs James Ryder James Gardner, Joseph Thompson, William Scott, Peel,Thomas Vale,John Wainwright and John Piling. Proposed by air Francis McKinley, seconded by Mr Frederick William Savage. Assentors: Messrs Reginald Percy Wilson, Robert Tickle, Richard Edging. ton, John Rudge, jun., Nathaniel Delooze, Thomas Bishop, Edwin Crook and James Quinn. Proposed by Sarah Ann Peacsocs, seconded by Jane Pearsons. Assentors: Ann Taylor, Annie Eliza White, Emma Guest, Ann Walsh, Mary Ann Coggins, Ann Knight, Mary Ann Smart and Mary Cunnington. Proposed 1)y Frederialt Edward Dean, seconded by Ernest Gladstone Hughes. Assentors : Samuel Johnson, Thomas O'Day, Martin Kelly, Thomes Dumman. Patrick Gillooley, Joseph Wood Burrows, Michael , Joseph Tobin, James Joseph Gillooley. ST. AUSTIN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr Edmund Osborne, cashier, 22, Bold-street, is proposed by Alderman D Tinnion, seconded by Mr Henry Gordon Roberts. Aesentors: Messrs Olken Haley, W Miles. John Johnson, Bilis James Divies, John Edwin Jefferson, James Moss, John r W Lake, and Isaac Baker Barlow.</p>
        <p>Proposed by Dr J B Naden, seconded by Mr H M Ash( n. Assentors: Dr Darold Joseph, Dr H Langdale. Messrs J R Evans, S Percy Silcock.l Alfred Henry Clive, W H Payton, Albert Warourton end Bertram Silcock. Propotied by Dr Reginald Smith, seconded by Dr J S 51111154113• ASSIMI.OIII Messrs John Taylor, ILD.S., Wm Warburton, Wm Bibby, Thus Locke, Joseph Bailey, Leas* Hayward, Hedley II Taylor and the Rev Wm Cooper. Proposed by Mr A Tilling, seconded by Mr Joseph Wilkes. As-entors : Messrs Thomas James Rigby, Abraham Roberts, Martin Naughton, Albert Payne, Wm James Payne, Alfred Dennett, James Wm Cox and Jos Elwell, jun.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>TORY MEASURE FOR REFORM OF THE HOUSE OF LORDS.</p>
        <p>The Tory Government in our Local Parliament has made the repeal of the Parliament Act bud reform of the Huse or Lords the first plank in its legislative programme. Before an excellent attendance, a Bill with that dual object was introduced on Thursday evening, and gave rise to an animated discussion. The PuratE MINISTER (Mr E A White), in submitting the measure, said the principal reason for forcing the Parliament Act upon the country was the alleged need of House of Lords reform. The Act was passed on August 18;h, Hill, but up to the present the Imperial Government had not done anything in that direction. In the meantime the country suffered under the evils of the Act by being ruled by a coalition of roinorites which thought it could carry through Acts of Parlia- ment without paying regard to the wishes of the electorate. Once there existed a notorious Govern. meat known as the "Cabal" Government, whose name was formed from the initials of its leaders. The present Government at Westminster might be termed the " Rag" Government, which was an appropriate name. (Laughter.) It had torn up its political principles and had no regard for its past pro- mises. It was necessary to restore the country to freedom,in order that the people might rule themselves, by repealing the Parliament Act. Evils had been created by the Act as, for instance, the passing of the Insur- ance Act. Apart from the Parliament Act, the insur- ance Scheme would not have been passed as it was. Had means of checking and revising it or referring it to the country existed a more practicable Act would have resulted. Theoretically the present House of Lords was unsatisfactory. At the same time he contended that in practice it had worked exceedingly well. Al- though the Second Chamber, theoretically, had very large powers, it had not exercised them to conflict with the will of the people in modern times. In appointing members of the Second Chamber it was con- templated that a class of men should exist—oien eminent for their wisdom, valour, and service to the country—and from that class the House should be formed. In future the majority of peerages which would be created would be peerages for life. The Bill would bring into the constitution of the Upper House premiers ot self-governing Colonies and Imperial repre- sentatives. The suggested fifty peers to be appointed under the King's sign manual at the commencement of each Parliament would be elected on the recommends- dons of the Prime Minister of the day, but not necessarily from the then existing peerage. Fifty representatives would be elected by the Peers them-. selves. In the election of the fifty Peers by the House of Commons he suggested that each M.P. should vote for a certain number, say ten peers, and that scheme, he thought, would ensure a fair and propor- tional representation of each party. The same idea would apply to the members chosen by the Peers. By granting power to the County Councils a lair repre- sented n of the feeling of the country would be ensured. The Bill provided that the referendum should be brought into operation on all matters on which the two Houses of Parliament were unable to agree. If the referendum were agreed to, it would be very seldom necessary to put it into practice. The Government and the Second Chamber would be very chary and shy at insisting upon a referendum unless they were quits sure that there was a strong probability that the majority of the electors was with them. If the question was se doubtful that each party felt confident that Its own view would prevail, then he contended that was the proper time for the referendum to be taken. In all cases where the country showed a clear indica- tion of its will, neither one party nor the other would stultify itself by insisting upon a referendum. Sir Edward Grey had declared that to attempt to govern the country by a single Chamber would mean" death disaster and damnation." The present Liberal Govern= went was trying to do that, and he predicted that the consequence would be the death of the coalition of minorities, and disaster to the Liberal party, but that the country would be saved from damnation by the speedy return to power of the Conservative party. (Laughter and applause.) Mr C E P - ARKEn (L., Dundee) moved the customary Opposition amendment that the Bill be read that day six months. His first objection was that the measure would do away with the Parliament Act which had been before the country twice and bad received its approval. (Applause and " Question.") He also contended that with such a second Chamber as was proposed, the Liberal party would not haves fair or just opportunity of carrying legislation for which it had received a mandate' The reformed House would, to a consider- able extent, be based upon the hereditary principle and I the Liberal party objected to that being introduced at all. This was a democratic country and both Houses should be representative and elected. It was expected that through the Peerage the Conservative party would still be able to retain the power in the country and dominate it. In the words of Mr Balfour the Government wished to ensure that the Unionist party should still control, whether in power or in Opposition, the destinies of the Empire. That would be neither lair play nor good for the country. Referr- ing to Lord Curzon's advocacy of the hereditary principle, on the ground that the greatest danger to any aristocracy was that it should be divorced from public life aud devote itself to weilth and pleasure, Mr Parker asked whether they were to place the welfare of the country in jeopardy, merely to keep the Lords from going to rot with ease and luxury? There , was plenty for the L..rds to do,if they would only do it. I They owned a very large proportion of the land of the country and they might apply themselves to developing it for the common good. He did not think the referendum would satisfactorily fit into our system of party government and he very much doubted whether the occasions when it would be brought into operation would be so few as the Prime Minister predicted. Every time privilege was touched, every time a strong measure of a progressive character was being pressed forward, there would be a collision between the two Houses, requiring a referendum. Moreover he did not think a referendum would ensure the clear verchet of the country upon the single question at issue. Various interests would vote, not on the merits of the question, but because they wanted to harm or oust the Govern- ment. Mr J McDrrofin : Does the same tlaiug apply to an ordinary general election ? Mr PARKER : I think it does, nd further I don't see that a referendum is different from a general election. Finally Mr Parker maintained that if a referendum went against the Government it would mean such a loss of prestige that the ministry would be obliged to relinquish office. Mr J DALEY (Socialist, E Bradford) said the Socialists did not want a Second Chamber of any kind. The House of Lords was as necessary to the existence of the Liberal Party as the devil was to the existence of a medieval Christian, or as an Aunt Sally was to the youthful sportsman at the fair—it was something to shy at. (Laughter.) The reformed Second Chamber would mean the increased representation of interests already over-represented in the House of Commons. If we were to have a Second Chamber at all, the Socialists would rather have the old antique kind, so feeble and weak-kneed as to bend to the slightest breath of passion from the House of Commons or the country, and only proving obstinate</p>
        <p>because of the weakness of the Liberal opposition to it. (Laughter.) They would prefer that to a House scientifically constructed in order to oppose that recon- tiou of society upon which the Socialists had set their hearts, as the only thing worth living for in politics. Hr G F CLARKE (C., Westhoughton), said he was not surprised that the Liberals were disgusted with the House of Lords, after all the efforts they had put for- ward in sending Peer after Peer to the Second Chamber in quick succession—only to find that a very short acquaintance with that. Chamber had led to their depar- ture from the desired path. (Laughter and a VoicE "Senile decay.") He maintained that the Parliament Act,as it stood, had not received the approval of the electors and it most be repealed to give safety and protection to the people. Candidly, he was not so great a lover of the hereditary principle as members of the Opposition— (laughter)—although they sometimes seemed to think that was a monopoly of the Con- servative party. If they were to have an effective Second Chamber, some regard must be bad to the ability and standing of the gentlemen who were to compose it. Rather than that the hereditary principle should be accepted in foto, he would prefer to pin .nis faith to a popularly elected Second Chamber, under certain conditions which would place it outside the voice of popular clamour and enable it to exercise Its judgment independently. The 'Radical party objected to the referendum because it meant a direct appeal to the people and "One man, one vote." Mr P WOODS (L., Stockport,) said it showed base ingratitude on the part of self-respecting '['ones to attempt to reform a House which had stood by them and done so much for them in the past. (Laughter). The Liberals recog- nised that the Parliament Act was not sulFcient. It only partially met the case, for it was not only necessary to limit the power of the Upper Chamber but vitally to alter its constitution. The Parliament Act was a necessary preliminary to reform. How, otherwise, were they to ensure that the Lords would accept the pro- posals to alter the Second Chamber ? Mr. E. RANDLES (Socialist ; Jarrow) said the people did not want measures to increase government ' • they were already governed too much.(Laughter).lt had been stated that the House of Lords bad done exceedingly well in the past—so it had, for the vested interests. (Hear, hear). The Socialists still held to the refer- endum, but they wanted it to come, not from the House of Lords but from the representatives of the people. The PRESIDENT OF THE BOARD OF TRADE (Mr Devonport) did not think a popularly elected Chamber would be a good thing for the country, It would be a duplicate of the House of Commons, and would there- fore serve no purpose. The Rev H OGDEN (L., Oldham) referring to Tory taunts about a coalition of minorities, said the Tories did not object to availing themselves of Nationalist assistance when they were anxlon3 to pass their Educa- tion Bill. As to the proposal to include Princes of the Royal Blood in the retorrned House of Lords, he spoke with all deference to the reigning house, but he did not know that history guaranteed that Princes of the Royal blood possessed the wisdom, intelligence or piety to entitle them to sit in the House of Lords without election. He also asked the Labour party if they had discovered in their dealing with the Lords of Appeal—who were to be included in the new House— that they considered things in an unbiassed light ? Almost without exception, they came down on the side of vested interests.</p>
        <p>The debate was adjourned until next Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LIBERAL BOWLERS.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL BOWLERS.</p>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD PRESENTS PRIZES.</p>
        <p>There was a convivial gathering of Liberal bowling enthusiasts in the pavilion at the Bowling Green, Bank Quay, last evening, when Mrs. A. H. Crosfield distributed the prizes gained in the recent highly successful main. The proceedings took the form of a smoking concert, an •excellent programme being submitted by Mrs. E. A. Sanders, Madame Sara Silcock, Mr. W. H. Dutton, Mr. J. Sephton, Mr. S. Aukland and Mr. E. A. Sanders.</p>
        <p>Councillor Wilkinson presided, and amongst those present were :—Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, Mrs. Osborne, Alderman Tinnion, Mr. Peter Peacock and Mr. George Walker.</p>
        <p>• The Winner of the first prize in the main was J. Davies, with F. Dale as runner-up. MR. CROSFIELD ON TORY INCENDIARISM.</p>
        <p>• Mr. CRosFIELD, in the course of an address on the political situation, said the Govern- ment had got to close grips with practical work,of the most patriotic and blessed kind for the nation. Their record of beneficent legislation was in striking contrast to the procedure of the Tories who, during twenty years of power, failed to fulfil their promise of old , age pensions and legislated for classes and sects at the expense of the general body of the people. Referring to the demoralizing influence of the Protectionist campaign, Mr Crosfield mentioned that the "raging, tearing, propa- ganda" coincided with the introduction of quasi-slavery in South Africa. It was also ' perfectly well-known in the House of Commons that once they had got the yoke of Protection firmly settled on the shoulders of the people, the great bulk of the Tory party in the House were perfectly ready to go in for that insidious, dangerous, and in this country, quite unnecessary and ill-advised policy of conscription. (Hear, hear.) There was an old saying "fore-warned is fore-armed" and he did not make the state- ment without having made inquiries when he was in the House of Commons from those who knew. He gave that as another illus- tration of the kind of reaction and bad influence that seemed to dog the foot- steps of Protection. To-day they had men who claimed to _ represent good</p>
        <p>traditions of breeding deliberately con- sidering whether they should reduce the House of Commons to a bear garden and shout down every opposition speaker. To cap it all, they had the present leader of the Tory party indulging in language which was a deliberate encourage- ment to the law breakers—not of Ulster but of the little Orange faction in the North- East corner of Ulster—language which was a perfect disgrace to him. (Hear, hear.) The Tory party could no longer arrogate to itself the title of " the Constitutional party." Its leaders had been making it pretty clear that to achieve their own ends, they were willing to throw law and order and the Constitution itself to the winds. They were trying—and the Member for Warrington supported them—to estab- lish the doctrine that a very small minority should control an enormous majority. They might be certain that such procedure—which would mean the end of Constitutional government—was not going to succeed. Just as the Fenian was repressed, so the reckless, fanatical Orange law breaker of Belfast would, if occasion arose, be severely dealt with. (Applause.) Mr. Crosfield claimed that the Liberal Government had done the fullest justice to the question of national defence, in respect of every legitimate demand that could be made, and they had also paid their way. (Loud applause). A hearty vote of thanks was passed to Mr. and Mrs Crosfield, on the proposition of Alderman Tinnion seconded by Mr. George Walker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Police Shot-Putting Competition.</head>
        <p>Police Shot-Putting Competition.</p>
        <p>Yes'erday afternoon the annual Police shot-putting competition was held in the Police Station yard for the Hippodrome Silver Cup. These competitions were inaugurated in 1910, the first winner having been Detective-Sergeant Holland. Last year the cup was won by Constable John Manson. The cup was again won by the holder yes-erday, P.C. Flaherty being second, Detective-Sergeant Holland third, P.C. Cameron 4th, Acting-Sergeant Whitby sth, and Detective Craig 6th. 'There were in all twenty com- petitors, and the contest took place under the personal supervision of the Chief Constable, assisted by Inspector Hodgkiss, bon secretary of the Police Athletic Club, and other officers of the Force.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CHURCH AND THE LIVING WAGE. STRIKING ADDRESSES AT CONGREGATIONALIST MEETING. " Starchiness " a Drag on the Church's Progress.</head>
        <p>THE CHURCH AND THE LIVING WAGE. STRIKING ADDRESSES AT CONGREGATIONALIST MEETING. " Starchiness " a Drag on the Church's Progress.</p>
        <p>Some striking views on the attitude of the Church towards modern social problems found expression at a public meeting held in Wycliffe Congregational Church on Tuesday evening in connection with the autumnal gatherings of the Congregational Union which have been held in Manchester this week. Sir Arthur A Haworth, Bart., presided and the principal speakers were the Rev G McLuckie, Great Yarmouth and the Rev A C Hill, London. Sir Aftriturt HAWOFtTFI's speech consisted chiefly of a plea for the churches to adapt their methods to the needs of the time. At the outset he said Wycliffe Church had something of a history—not going back to the days of the very earliest of the churches of 250 years ago—hut still a church that had been a landmark in that neighbourhood for many a day. Although the methods of Congregational churches might differ, all found a welcome for every man and woman who loved his or her Master, and wished to be associated with others who loved their Master, and to enlist in a larger or a smaller degree in His service. It was the beauty of their Congregational churches that their methods were so free, that the people meeting together should decide what was beat, and what was more, net only best for to-day,but for to-morrow,or the day after. The circumstances and surroundings and the habits of the people living near a church were apt to undergo considerable change. In that event a church could at once, without going to any High Court of Parliament, or any Congregational Union for the matter of that, among its own members decide what should be altered in its methods of pro- cedure, and what should still remain. He did not know -very much about Warrington, though he always told his friends he used to work in a mill there, which was perfectly true. He might have been a sort of quarter-timer, hut still there it was. (Laughter.) But changes were coming over their church life and he would like to speak on that aspect, as having had a little bit to do with churches that had bad changes I forced upon them in the more crowded quarters of Manchester and Salford. He did not know the details of their work, but he had been led to the conclusion that many churches had suffered decline through not realizing that things had materially changed and had got into n parlous condition which would have been avoided if they had adopted new methods to meet the altered circumstances of the neighbourhood. From the bottom of his heart he believed that Congrega- tionalism had a message to deliver to all, old and young, wise and simple, rich and poor, strong and weak, but different methods might be necessary, even in one church, to deal with all those classes of people. He prayed that God would give them grace so that when those people came in they would feel that before the table of the Lord there was neither Jew nor Gentile bond nor free, but that all were absolutely equal in the presence of the Lord. (Applause). The Rev 0 McLucxle said that in these days the Church was generally thought to be in rather a bad way Her best friends did not regard her as some victorious army, marching with banners flying and bands playing, under capable leadership, to sure and certain victory. There was too often a feeling that they were in the trough of the sea, rather than on the crest, and at times like these they were in danger of two attitudes. In the first place they had to avoid a cheap and easy optimism—the attitude of the ostrich, burying its head in the sand and saying "There are not any difficulties or obstacles, because I cannot see any." No soldier , ever became a great General by undervaluing the forces arrayed against him. Similarly, no soldier of Christ would ever do the best possible work that was in him for his Master by under-estimating the difficul- ties be had to contend with. On the other hand, they had equally to avoid an attitude of panic and despon- dency. Nothing great had ever been born of panic and he was convinced that the spirit of despondency would never breed in any soul or in any company of souls that dynamic which was needed to send forward the Church and the cause of Christ. Referring to criticism from without, the speaker said there were those who told them that they had lost the great masses of the people because they had failed sincerely and adequa- tely to sympathise with them in the great burdens they had to bear. And set sometimes when a minister ventured to suggest that a living wage was in strict accord with the dictates of the New Testament and the Spirit of Christ, there were those who would cry out "Socialism." They were angry and fiercely critical—one would think that the poor, misguided man who had dared to make the sugges- tion of a living wage was a hot-headed, anarchic agitator and that if they could only search him they would almost surely find some bombs about his person. (Laughter.) The first thing Christians supremely needed was a spirit of conviction that would grip their hearts, and dominate their thinking and activity day by day. Congregationalists had a reputa- tion for being a bit starchy. In their judgment of their fellow members, and in their fellowship with them, they ought to be guided by the spirit so well expressed by Burns "A man's a man for a' that." Were they not sometimes apt to judge a ccording to clothes? He would go so far as to ssy that the man who was not prepared to shake bands with the dust man or the scavenger in the midst of his work was fit to be neither a Congregational minister nor a deacon. The STARCHINESS OF THEIR CHURCH LIFE</p>
        <p>was one of the greatest drags on the wheels of the Church's progress. It was not sufficient to be suave, " " at the church on b'undap. Sometimes they found there was plenty of hand shaking —regular pump handle work—on the Sunday, and then during the week not a sign of recognition, a sudden look heavenwards or into an inviting shop window until a person had passed. They bad to rise to that spirit which was so manifest in their great Master—a spirit of frank brotherliness neither toadying to the exalted nor patronizing the lowly and the poor. [hey</p>
        <p>.*ad to stand absolutely for social justice as between man and man. He was delighted to rend the declara- tion of the Archbishop of York in favour of</p>
        <p>A 'LIVING WAGE. When the great strike of railwaymen occurred last year, did they ask themselves what was at the bottom of it? There were those who would tell them that it was got up by agitators and fire brands. tie did not believe it. He believed—and he did so the more readily when he discovered that a tremendous number of railway men were getting under £l. a week—that in the hearts of many men there was a sense of injustice—the feeling that the were not getting a fair reward for their labour. He was one of those who thought that the Church must not only accept in theory the principle of a living wage, but must be vocal and clamant in her proclama- tion of the demand for it. He quite agreed that the Church, as a Church, had no function or faculty for deciding what a living wage was. That must be left to tbe economists, politicians and men of commerce—to the members of the Church as citizens— but the Church must be vocal as regarded the principle of a living wage for the toiler, a wage that would make decent comfort possible to him and to his loved ones. It might be urged that if they paid more wages, it would mean more drinking. There might be individual cases of that kind, but be did not, believe, for a moment, that it was true as a general criticism. He would go further and say that, even if it were true, be would still hold that the Church's duty was to plead for, nay to demand a living wage for the worker. The man who said " I will underpay so and so, so that he shall not spend too much in drink "was trying to do God's work with the devil's tools. The Church would never make sufficient headway until in the hearts of all the members, there wss the strong, potent conviction that Jesus Christ and His evangel were the one answer to all the difficulties and problems that beset them. They heard a greAt deal about the desirability of what was called economic freedom. There was a popular doctrine that sin and misery were the removable effects of social circumstances. He believed that was the devil's lie. It seemed to assume that whilst the East End was full of squalor, misery, vice and sin of every description, the West End was always fair, pure and free from malice, bitterness and all uncharitableness— which was absurd. There was no necessarf connection between economic re oarity and character fashioned after the type of Jesus Christ. Tbey must stand for the absolute redeemability of the blackguard and the scimp. Man was man, however low he might have sunk. (Applause). The Rev A C HILL had much to say about the spirit of the age which he s-id was characterised by moral sceptism and also by the complaint ,commonly called "swelled head." A learned professor at the British Association suggested t 3 those assembled about him that he had discovered, or would soon discover, or one of his learned brethren would soon discover, the origin of life. He (the speaker) presumed that some future professor would be able to make a Frankenstein for us at his will, and that good men and wise women, saints and heroes, would be produced just as they were needed. We could build a ship that was absolutely unsinkable—every honest and patriotic Belfast citizen believed that that very vessel was being constructed at the present time. Certainly there were tragic moments in history, when the unsink- able boat was swept suddenly down into two or more miles of water, and thousands of men and women were left to mourn, but still we maintained that we could make an unsinkable boat. We had conquered the air, we had chained the lightning, we bad controlled the thunder, and we were a great people. He hoped he would be forgiven if he suggested that, as sober men, they ought sometimes ,to ask themselves how much of all that was really justified ? When they forgot that they were in the world, not in order that they might play with their consciences and palter with right and wrong, but in order that they might do the simple and obvious duty that lay at their door, they were preparing themselves forAreason. The great preachers, continued Mr Hill, were not found in London but in villages where they proclaimed the Gospel not only by eloquent lips but by much more eloquent deeds, and the real heroes of the Church and of the State were not the few elect persons who " strut their little hour upon the stage "and received the plaudits of the crowd ; they were the humble and unknown heroes and heroines who bore the common lot and lived the lowly life as in God's sight. To be "narrow minded" in the true sense did not- mean that they despised other men, or that they were bigots or fanatics ; it meant that in an age which was like a moving swamp of indecision and uncertainty they had found a firm foothold on which they could stand and that, while the London journalist and playwright could tear their morals to tatters and show them as the victims of their own delusions, they knew there were some things that were not delusions but that abided eternally in the soul—the wish liable realities of human life. Deep down in the soul of the average man there lay some- thing that responded to the call of God, of justice and of truth, and there was not a church in the land that did not vocalise that appeal in a more or less perfect fashion. The wit, the humorist and the satirist had made merry at their expense since the day when he was born. The worst calamity that could ever happen to him would be the death of the Christian Church, , for then there would be left only the mother-in-law ,and the Piccadilly dude. (Laughter). There was nothing so easy, contemptible and despicable as to make fun of the great, simple. deep realities of human life. Was there anything so comical as a funeral to the man who had never lost a beloved one? The giggling, jihbering animils on two legs, who went about the world holding up religion to scorn,ought to be making door mats out of oakum, for the general benefit and the common weal. At least let Christians have the courage to confess to all the world that they belonged to those simple people who worshipped God—a great thing—who belived in God—an enormous statement—who found in Jesus Christ the best interpretation and key of the tragedy of human existence and who buoyed themselves up, in the midst of this often dark and dolorous world, with the hope of an eternal bliss where there were neither sighs, tears nor partings. They had a great faith, a great history, and a glorious opportunity. Let them dare to use it as in the sight of God. (Applause). A vote of thanks was passed to the speakers, on the proposition of the Rev JOHN YONGE, seconded by Mr J W SLACK. During the evening the choir sang the anthem "Be glad and rejoice," Mr H Hawthorn being the soloist. Mr W H Payton, Mils. Bac., was at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SOC/AL</head>
        <p>SOC/AL</p>
        <p>New Appointment for the Rev. F. A Screeton.</p>
        <p>Warrington churchpeople will be interested to hear that the living of St. Paul's, Seacombe, vacant by the death of the late Canon Weatherhead, has been given to the Rev F A Screeton, M.A., Leo. Th., vicar of St Thomas's, Hyde. He was selected by the Bishop of Chester, who was requested by the patronage trustees to make the appointment. Mr Screeton was assistant curate of St. John's the Baptist's, Chester, from 1891 to 1897 ; assistant curate of Christ Church, Latchford, from 1897 to 1898 ; and vicar of Christ Church, Latch- ford from 1899 to 1903; since which date he has been vicar of St Thomas's, Hyde. The living at Seacombe is worth about £3BO a year.</p>
        <p>RRINGTON</p>
        <p>dependent upon God's bounty for life, health and vigour, and pointed out that we were responsible not only for working in co-operation with God, but for the right use of God's gift to us. The choir rendered two anthems with much accep- ance, that in the evening being much enhanced by Mr Griffith's solo. A harvest hymn, specially written tor the occasion by Mr Alfred Corney, was sung at the evening service. Mr W H Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ. The collections were on behalf of the maintenance fund. _</p>
        <p>Congregational Union Assembly. The autumnal assembly of the Congregational Union of England and Wales opened in Manchester on Mon- day. Since the Union last met in Manchester eleven years ago Congregationalism in the city has developed to a striking extent. Ten years ago the Congregational Board appealed for an annual income of £7OO for missicin work ; now an appeal is made for £3,000. Mission work was formerly carried on in odd chapels or in rooms over coal yards ; now the chief mission centres are two of the finest buildings of the kind in the country. A central home for the,administration of the various denomination! organizations in Lancashire and Cheshire has been established in the new Congre- gational Church-house in Deansgate, built at a COSL of £20,000. DR. ADENEY ON CALLOUS CHRISTIANS. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Delivering his presidential address on Tuesday the Rev. Dr. W. F. Adeney drove home some straight remarks concerning the Christian ideas of justice and charity. He said : . . . . .</p>
        <p>" ;isit prosperous churches in manufacturing towns of Lancashire and Yorkshire and learn that here they are building a new organ, and there they are furnishing a parlour. It is well, and the gifts mean some sacrifice on the part of the givers. " But I observe that whim the manufacturers live in stately mansions and have fine paintings on their walls, and drive to their offices in swift motor-cars, their workpeople are huddled together in back-to-back houses that double the infant mortality, and I ask myself would not these churches he more true to the mind of the Master if they helped to bring about equable con- ditions in the communities among which they are planted, even at the cost of a third manual to the new organ, or a Turkey carpet in the church parlour ? "Or I visit one of our charming villages in the south country, with its mossy thatch and rose-clad porches, and though I know that the little old chapel can do no more than make a brave fight for existence, I wonder whether the greater resources of the churches in stronger centres might not be forthcoming to secure</p>
        <p>W</p>
        <p>" (MO • "a•ise • _A. •ta • !LIVE, r littrerif • s • • -0 4 RELIC/01/S- es</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from our advertisement columns harvest thanksgiving services will be held at St John's Church to•morrow. The preacher morning and evening will be the Rev. William Reid, minister of the church, who will preach in the morning on the subject of" fhe Prodigality of Providence," and in the evening will have for the subject of his discourse" The parable of the soils."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Colborne-street Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Colborne-street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>The pastor's anniversary was celebrated at the above church on Sunday. Although the Rev H Ogden has been with the congregation only 12 months many improvements and extensive alterations have been made. Rapid progress is being made with the new school premises and the new church front although not as yet completed is so far advanced that one can easily Presume what the great effect will be when the altera- tions are completed. The new school premises will in all probability be opened next month. Pastor and people have a sharp eye upon the necessity of a strong Sunday school. There has been a fair addition of new members and the evening congregation is excellent. Last Sunday morning the Pastor preached from the text, "My God shall supply all your need according to His riches in glory in Christ :Jesus," end emphasized the fact that God is sufficient for all things. The speaker in the afternoon was the Rev. William Cooper, who made evident that the people's sympathy was the measure of the pastor's power to do good. The Rev El Ogden preached in the evening from the words, "But watch thou in all things, endure afflictions, preach the good news, make full proof of thy ministry." The choir, under the leadership of Mr Barnes, rendered special anthems in a creditable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>COMPANION OF LIVINGSTONE AT HOME.</head>
        <p>A COMPANION OF LIVINGSTONE AT HOME.</p>
        <p>Preparations are now being made for the celebration of the Livingstone Centenary, which takes place next March. We reproduce a photograph of Sir John Kirk, surrounded by his African trophies, taken recently at 4: his borne at Sevenoaks. Sir John is the only man living who went with Livingstone</p>
        <p>through the thick of his wonderful work in Darkest Africa. Sir John (who should not be confused with the Sir John Kirk, of Ragged School fame) is an octogenarian, but enjoys good health, and recalls with surpris- ing detail' his varied adventures with his famous companion.</p>
