      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The Wycliffe P.S.A. was presided over on Sunday by Mr. D. Mclischern. A capital address was given by Mr.lWardman on "The Teachings of Christ," or the Sermon on the Mount. In Christ's time, and since,and now, there were the same opposing teachings and teachers. But they had failed, and the laws which govern life here still had their foundation on eternal authority. We were all constrained by Christ's life and teachings to love God and our fellows, and keep true to God and man.— Mr. Yearsley also took Ipart In the meeting. Mr. Payton presided at the organ. The hymns were of a harvest thanksgiving character.—The President referred to the Society's loss 131 the departure of Mr. Arthur Griffiths, who sailed for Toronto, Canada, yeste•day with his wife and members of his family. Mr. Griffiths had been a member of the Brotherhood for some years, and often delighted meetings with his songs.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Church Harvest Festival. Harvest thanksgiving services were conducted at the Emmanuel Church, Bold-street, on Sunday. Good con- gregations assembled throughout the day, and the preacher was the Rev W T Jaye. In the afternoon a flower and fruit service was held. Special anthems were rendered by the choir at each service, Mr J W Jowett presiding at the organ. The congregation in the evening was a very large one. The preacher took for his text the worth : " And they were sore amazed in themselves beyond measure, and wondered, for they considered not the miracle of the loaves, for their heart was lieardened." Some lessons to be derived from the miracle of the loaves were given. We were dependent creatures, and bread was a necessity, and there was absolutely no subititute for it in the wide world. People could live entirely on bread if forced to do so, but there was not a single man who could live without it. Wheat grew in every part of the world, and that showed what a wonderful God was theirs. Chine° did the} call it? They must not think there was any chance in this universe. It was God who had ruled over evervthiog, and Who gave wheat and bread for all men. The harvest was God's response to our natural needs. Jesus Christ was God's complete answer to our spiritual needs ; it was Jesus Who was the true bread of life.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church. Harvest thanksgiving services were held in W3cliffe Congregational Church on Sunday and were well attended. The preacher was the Rev J E Flower. of London, secretary of the Home Missionary Society, who after an interval of twenty-two years once again occupied the pulpit. The interior of the church had been tasefully decorated with flowers and fruit under the direction of the Misses Porritt. In an interesting talk to the children the preacher referred to a visit he paid to Canada and in alluding to the autumnal exhibi- tion in Toronto he drew a useful lesson from the different results obtained from sprayed and unsprayed fruit trees He asked the children to spray their lives with the spirit of Christ so that they might he a blessing to them- selves and those around them. For his sermon he chose the text "And he humbled thee and suffered thee to hunger," S:c. (Deut. viii., 3). He pointed out that while the harvest had been disappointing to many farmers, there was still much to be thankful for. Our losses and disappointments bad a purpose—sometimes to teach us greater efficiency or the still higher lesson of man's dependence upon God. Whilst there never was a more luxurious age there was a tendency to become too materialistic. God intended us to cultivate a life that was worth calling a life—a thinking, reverent and by lug life, one that showed God dwelt in us. "Cive us this day our daily bread " was the subject of the evening discourse. R.ferring to the Lord's Frayer, he said the opening verses reminded us of God 'and His Kingdom, and the succeeding ones of man and his wants. When we had put ourselves in proper relationship with God then we might pray for our- selves. He proceeded to show how everyone was</p>
        <p>Beautifully made frame fitted with lenses to suit any sight 17/07 as sold in Liverpool and Manchester for 35/.</p>
        <p>PLEASE NOTE.—MR. WATSON attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday 11 to 5-30 Thursday 11 to I; Friday and Saturday 11 to 5-30.</p>
        <p>Hours of Business: 9-30 to 7-30 ; Saturday: 9-30 to 9-30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>for the agricultural labourer a living wage and a home that is a habitable cottage, and not merely a picturesque ruin—the greatest economic requirement of England to-day. "If the time machine conld carry us forward some centuries, and we could look back on the England of the present time end see its wasteful wealth and lavish luxury side by side with its hard penury and squalid misery, we should be amazed at the callousness of the Christian people who lived their complacent lives in presence of this appalling contrast. " It is true that we often deplore the evil and denounce the unjust conditions that give rise to it. But all such lamentations and denunciations are our own condemnation, if in our church life and action we are doing nothing to remedy this vast and mountainous denial of the brotherhood of DIM</p>
        <p>" The revolution must begin in the Church if it is to be promoted by the Church—begin, that Is, in a real end effectual brotherhood among its members at the cost of personal sacrifice. Too often the Church has failed in the performance of its plain duty towards mankind."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Guild.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Guild.</p>
        <p>The G uild met on Monday evening to hear a paper on" Savonarola "by Rev W Reid. Mr B Williamson was chairman. The essayist gave a very complete account of the main incidents in the life of the Great Italian worker, from the time he decided to join the Church until he met the martyr's death, through his vehement exposures of the corruptness existing therein Emphasis was drawn to the great moral strength of the man, who, despite the combined powers of Church and State to silence him, ignored offers of advance, dis- regarded threats of violence and continued persistently to denounce the great wickedness of his times. There was a good discussion at the close and Mr Reid was awarded a hearty vote of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>New Church Lectures.</head>
        <p>New Church Lectures.</p>
        <p>The Bev Howarth of Bolton delivered a lecture on Tuesday evening on the subject of "The Deity and humanity of Jesus Christ. " The lecturer laid special emphasis on the teaching of the Holy Word, for only in such a revelation of God is it possible to obtain a knowiedge of God. The oneness of the Godhead was the first principle considered and it was pointed out that in no place in the Divine Word is there any reference to any other than the Lord—no God the Son, no God the Holy Spirit. This fact of the unity and oneness of the God- head is constantly and emphatically declared throughout the sacred Scriptures, so that any doctrine which violates this fundamental principle cannot be in accord- ance with its Divine teaching. Many passages here were cited in condrmation of the doctrine. The tri- personal theory of God has no place in the Scriptures ' and this is constantly evidenced God the Son and God the Holy Spirit are terms which are foreign to the Sacred Scriptures, and no passage which teaches that Jehovah would send another to accomplish that which He Himself promises to perform can be cited. The doctrine of the Incarnation, if it means anything, implies that it was the Divine who could alone accomplish man's redemption, would come into the world by the assumption of a human nature which could meet all the attacks of sin and temptation, and by victory over all subdue the powers of hell, restore spiritual freedom to the human race, and glorify the humanity which bad been assumed. "In Him, Jesus Christ. dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily." The incarnation was the first step in the redemption and the resurrection was the last culminating in the glorification of the humanity which had been assumed. It is not sufficient to teach Christ. Whatever the appearances may be the evidence is that Jesus and God are One During His life in the world there were occasions when the indwelling Divine mani- fested itself in the words and the works, at other times manifestations of the weakness and dependence of the human nature upon the Eternal and Divine Father of all. But these occasions are after all no evidence of a duality or a tripersonality in the Godhead. 'file Lord Jesus Christ is upon Scripture evidence the only true object of Christian worship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Dr. Stanley Mellor on " Religion and Democracy." Who are the People P</head>
        <p>Dr. Stanley Mellor on " Religion and Democracy." Who are the People P</p>
        <p>There was a crowded congregation in Cairo-street Chapel on Sunday evening, when the Rev Dr Stanley A. Mellor delivered the first of a series of special sermons on " Religion and Democracy." After some introduc- tory remarks,in which the rev gentleman said the object of the course of addresses was simply to try to face the facts of the situation as they affected the two forces which should dominate the world in the future, Demo- cracy and Religion,he proceeded to discuss the question: How stands Democracy to-day ? The truth was, he said, that democracy was not simply a method of government—that was one of its least important features—but a form of social order aud an ideal of social life. It was not merely the pdssession of so-called political power ; it required a vast area more than that. Behind all political power there lay economic power, and the former was useless without the latter. It was those who controlled the wealth of a country who, in the end, governed that country. We flattered ourselves in England that we were the freest of free democracies. It was not true, for with us the material ,wealth of our Society was owned and controlled by relatively small bumber of individuals who, for that very reason, did actually rule and govern the land. As a rule we sent to Parliament rich men whose interest consisted in keeping things as they are, or pro- fessional politicians for whom politics was only a game, and a game, moreover, utterly removed from the deadly and serious issues of life. Representative government was little better than a farce, a cruel mockery, simply because the one effective weapon in politics to-day, viz., material wealth, was controlled by the few and not by the many. We lived under an illusion that the basis of democracy was political— an illusion fron which we should have to awaken. The basis of democracy was not political but economic. There could be no genuine democracy in a Society in which wealth was inequitably and unjustly distributed. Is a country in which the material resources are con- trolled by a few and withheld from the many,the appeal of the" will of the people" was nothing but a piece of discreditable hypocrisy. "Government of the people, by the people, and for the people." But who were " the people ?" There were those in whose ears the word democracy had an evil sound, who supposed that by" the people " was meant the uneducated mass, the ignorant crowd, the vast, patient silent host of the denizens of the underworld for whom existence was coloured a dull grey, lightened only by lurid spots of brutish and insensate fury ! In this town there were huge places where, day after day, thousands of men were herded together for long hours, in sweltering heat, in an atmosphere charged with poisonous odours, or darkened by thick, black smoke, and set to labour at mechanical tasks, devoid of beauty and devoid of interest, returning from their work to tiny dwellings, in which there was insufficient room, insufficient, air insufficient light, fromt the painful and insufferable dullness of which many were drawn towards the excitements of drinking and, gambling, "two elements which at least can give colour in a life set in grey." These were the men of "the people." In this town, again, there were places, where pale, weary, sad rigid creitturrs sit all day stitch- ing in front of machines driven by power for which they themselves paid, earning, at the end of a week's weariness, a wage utterly inadequate to keep body and soul together, faced, in the last resort, with the perpetual alternative of starvation or shame. These were the girls of " the people." The women of the people were no better off—they could not see life other than grey verging on black. Was it true or just to describe that Society as a democracy in which the people were so constituted? On the contrary it was palpably untrue. Sometimes, as one viewed the exist- ing order of the world, it seemed as if " tbe people" had nothing before them save either further reduction to a condition of economic servitude or open and active revolt against the whole order and condition of things as they a-e. In conclusion, the preacher said: The vision has dawned, I say ; its coming means the overthrow of the existing order and the fashioning of society anew. Even now, with sure and infallible instinct, the advancing hosts of those who have seen the vision attack the outposts of capitalistic civiliza- tion; they make war against the accumulation of riches In the hands of a few ; they demand higher wages. , Soon they will attack profits, interest, rent, working all the time towards that economic equality, without which true democracy is impossible. But into the ideal must he fused the fire and the passion of religion ; possession of the material resources of earth is not the end but only the beginning of democracy, and the true vision includes all spiritual good. Here is work for the Church,to give the colours of heaven to the earthly hopes and desires of damocracy,to lead the gathering hosts not only to the achievement of material equality, comfort, and prosperity, but also to the further conquest of all spiritual nobility, heavenly truth, divine beauty, and Godly virtue. But, ere that come to pass, who can say what cat?chysms and revolutions, external and internal,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The Four Royal Scots, are a quartette of clever lad,' entertainers who have been well received at the HipP°- drome this week. As vocal harmonisers and instrir mentalists they are highly successful, whilst the homeliness of the act gives an added charm. The singing is exceptionally good and one member of tile troupe is a most able violinist, her imitation of tb.e bag-pipes being very realistic. "Kitty's Hero " the title of a farcical sketch given by Fred Sinclair an' Gracie Whiteford,a couple of clever artistes who highlY amuse and entertain in a clean and palatable mannerci Mr. Sinclair i a past master in the art of make-up nn as the" poor lone widow" as well as the degenerate, husband, be is a decided success. Cann and Coors° create roars of laughter as the "silent burglars," 131.4 even this part of their performance is excelled their tricky and clever antics on the trapeze nn,.. balancing-pole. Devon and Earle present wbe" would now apparently seem impossible—something new in simultaneous dancing. They do not, 8S ifj usual with such turns, claim themselves to b,e "comedians and dancers," and where general the comedy is not discoverable, but the, bring the house down with the extraordinari rapidity and precision of their foutwork" The Three Bollingers are clever rag painters, and onl is considerably astonished to see a green baize covet board transformed, with a few pieces of rag. int° realistic picture of a village church in winter. Perhnl the most popular was the "painting" by means of rill of the humorous pictures" The hope of his side " Out first ball." Carney Bros. give us two cal songs in pleasing style, although one of the two mig r modify his vocal exertions out of consideration f°6, his partner and the occupants of the stalls. 11, Walters is a bright comedienne with an fishing song in which the catch is a husband. Pe angling from behind the footlights had more than spice of humour in it. Ernest Tilbury and Beatrice N. ere, present their "Gems of vocal harmony a pleasing manner. • CHEEKS UNSIGHTLY WITH ECZEMA'</p>
