        <p>The second Sankey Street Chapel. THE JACOBITE REBELLION. all the Galileans, because they suffer such things; , or many years after James 11. had fled neither do I wonder, since such treatment usually ,oh, . attends great guilt, at least shou'd. ' England there existed a party who by Now if such Hardships may apologize for a ,4'saus plots and struggles endeavoured to just vindication of ourselves, surely when our the Stuarts again to the throne. They Places of Divine worship (consecrated to the 6 known to history as the Jacobites, and la service of God) become Victims to the rage and .the Jacobites and Nonconformists came prey of a High C . . . . h Mob, nothing can be somewhat violent collision, some brief more seasonable than to publish to the World a heir activities were for a time confined to of them must be given here. plain and honest Defence of our separation from the peculiarities of the Established Church. It's strange that the Sons of Belial, who cry out tile plots, of which the Assassination plot 16e "The Temple of the Lord" should expend all len,. -6 Was one, and for which some promi- their outrage in the demolition of Protestant tit ' People suffered. But in 1715 they broke Churches,w hen all the Mass-houses in the King- i„o rebellion of the gravest kind in Scotland dom stand film and unthreatened. the north of England. I never'saw it yet recounted for, why conscien- -I,44rfles 11. had died in 1701 leaving a son tir us men should he so generally ill-treated, when 1T,,1712s as his heir. This youth was hailed by men of profligate Minds aud Practices are ;,',Jacobites as heir to the English throne, sheltered under the venerable Name of the i'lcl the object of their plots was to place him Ch .., Ii from the ecmorion censure and Indig- nation of Mankind. e in re. In the stormy days that followed the •,,ee_ i The Doctor was famous as a political dis- sson of King George of Hanover—an senter rather than a theologian and was vent which stirred the Jacobites to angry long regarded as the pillar of the Royal tY •lt fell to eth Whigs and Dissenters c cause in the North of England. Yet he u ecome the succession. defenders of the throne and the devoted himself to his pastorate and the 'c'testant suion. Curiously as it reads .ri teaching of youth with unremitting patience, the Tory and High Church party and his church was perhaps his best ,re largely with the rebels, and since the til memorial. . arts were Catholic. Many Catholics were 4tll. them too. After fifty years of labour be passed away in old age on February 17th 1746 and was , A MEMORABLE BIRTHDAY. buried within the chapel. rr n e Rebellion of 1715 broke out on the Some words of appreciation from his ellth of Ild June' y the birthday of Prince James,, a nephew, spoken after his death,may be quoted a date chosen for the Jacobites for n 111,i_ to show that there was another and much lival display of faction. 1 more lovable side to this great fighter, so Z4rly that morning the bells in Warrington admired of the Hanoverians and so hated of 'la An merry peal. There was no business , g and many were apparrelled in Sunday Ithe s• A riotous crowd paraded the streets . His door ar d his purse were ever open to the distressed and indigent. His house was an Alms Vng "The Church in danger," Dawn with 11.. use ; he was nobly prodigal in doing g od ; issenters," "God save King James III." eyes to the blind; feet to the lame; the po ,r Was so in many a town that day and have lost a tender friend; a Friend did I ear ? 1 rcashire was to remember it for long can scarce forbear saying the poor have lost a 148 to come. A great faction-storm, pre- thousand fiend. As to his knowledge, where is the mine of 'Led by much conspiracy, hovered over Philosophy into which he hid not dug. Where tleashire and it seemed as though War- is the country be bed not travelled over in I is Iton was to feel the full shock of it. But closet? Where are the worlds vt hich be had 1)4 there was only noise