        <p>RED RIOT IN MANCHESTER. t:the storm centre was to be Manchester. a greater mob assembled. The Jacobite a'clers had agreed to concentrate and the itill3wledge had drawn from Warrington and tle surrounding area the most dangerous of oters.Led by music this reckless throng ;itliered to the beating of drums. Half mad gh liquor they attacked the homes of the Itigs and then joined forces to destroy the Bs-street Meeting House. Its doors and tlindows were broken in, the pews and pulpit oftered, the roof torn down and indeed I,lling left save the walls and wreckage. le or several days the town was at their i„kl7. The homes of the Dissenters were 44ndered, the magistrates either unable or Illing to intervene. arching forth they destroyed the Non- formist Chapels at Blackley, Monton and r'o e,nacres. Separate parties marched into ijit shire, Shropshire and Staffordshire and 1,1-lining the title "the Loyal Mob" rarded Shrewsbury with threats to the 11rnnable faction" called Dissenters if they "'lid dare to meet for worship. b CIVIL WAR IN LANCASHIRE. e a r li am en t intervened and sent troops to ,allchester,thus putting Lancashire in which ran 44 Dractically a state of Civil War. The C'°l3ites centred at Preston four or five q4'nsand strong. General Wills, the Corn- er of the Government Force, secured errington with its bridge and ford and 'relied hence to Preston. fr, NONCONFORMITY MILITANT. 06,34eY were stirring days for Nonconfor- 0, for they felt that their existence was Up in the struggle, and wemeed not be )teDrised that there were curious symptoms 111Jcitement. It is here that James Woods VD° kinister of Chowbent Chapel flashes ko4,11• the page of history. This intrepid klitit armed his entire congregation forced them into the field. Accord- to local tradition he held his sword tacj' the only one who seemed afraid and 14,0 e him leave the men and go home to the 4:l'en. A few more hours and he had ,attll'ered the farmers and farm labourers ?nth scythes put upon poles, bill pitch forks and spades, and with this tPelinneous army marched upon the rebels ha„ reston. Right glad the General was to 17f5e them on that famous 14th November, tht , when the battle of Preston was won for 1111(ing• if t e cruelties inflicted upon the Jacobites totll'e hour of their downfall are a disgrace Nbe, Whig Government; but it was perhaps 4) h.,' Just that the Dissenters who had suffered tol.'",n,ch in the conflict should be recompensed klb,Teir many losses. Sixteen hundred %trogns was paid for the restoration of Cross- thk:t,Chapel and the damage upon other b.W.',e,l ,!1 was also assessed and made good. James Woods was defending the: qq4,,With a pitchfork, Dr. Charles Owen was iog him in Warrington no less zealously 4ents pen. And, as it proved, at much ,11)en'i.e. cost, for one of his pamphlets " Plain °lng "was made the ground of an action (.7• He was successful in his defence but ''rtio'c'st of the proceedings made the case kst ruinous might be written about those I,toiltllrbed and curious days, but we are not Nerned here with the broad movement of N„I'Y Save as it gives the background ',tqcilr story and must leave our readers to h',,ltitojr2acobite troubles in the pages of</p>
        <p>Thus a-dorTud and thus qualified Heaven con- tinued him to a good old age yet even in the la•er periods of life he was old in nothing but in years and goodness. His work in Warrington had every token of success. Some statistics prepared by Dr. Evans in the period beeween 1717 and 1729 show that the Warrington congregation numbered 713 persons, of whom 82 were freeholders—figures which proclaim it by far the largest Dissenting meeting in this part of Lancashire. The needs 6f the large congre- gation probably made necessary a second and more commodious chapel and Dr. Owen lived long enough to see the present building which began its course of usefulness in 1745. THE FIRST TRUST AND TRUSTEES. It may be appropriate here, as we take our leave of the venerable doctor, to recite a few details gleaned from the legal documents. The land upon which the chapel and subsequently the chapel house stood,had been purchased in the name of Dr. Owen and were thus held until 1736. It was not until that I year that a trust deed was prepared and the I, property vested in trustees. Unlike many of the early Presbyterian deeds it contained no statement of doctrine. The chapel was I erected for the minister to " preach the word , of God in, to pray in and administer the sacraments, " a broad and simple description which stands in marked contrast from the elaborate doctrinal statements usually written in trust deeds. The first trustees are thus named and described, the date of the deed being April 3rd 1736 : JONATHAN BENT, Meister DR. CHARLES OWLN ELLIS BENT, Linnen Draper THEOPIHLUS BENT, Meister WILLIAM SPENCE, Chapman WILLIAM WOODCOCK, Merchsnt RICHARD NICHOLAS, Tallow Chandler GEORGE BiRD, Sadler WILLIAM BARNES, Sadler ROGER GASKELL, Malster WILLIAM GANDY, Tobacconist THOMAS GASKELL, of Burtonwood, Yeoman HENRY BOOTH, Linen Weaver Seven years later there are indications that a special fund is in preparation, and we shall probably be correct if we assume that the money was intended for the re-building of the chapel. Under date February 19th, 1743, the Trustees execute a declaration of Trust by which it is set forth that a sum of £123 15s. held by them is held for and on behalf of the congregation. This sum is stated to consist of legacies left by various well-wishers whose names and gifts are set forth in a memorandum :--John Bent, £2O; Joseph Lewis, £2O; John Bell, £3O; George Patten, £2O; Mrs. Aspinwall, £5 ; Rebecca Hallywell, £2B 15s. In 1765 upon the death of William Gandy another legacy of £2O was added. An interesting fact concerning William Gandy, tobacconist, which we have been able recently to bring to light is that he was responsible for the building of Warrington's second chapel, the old building in Back Dallam-lane, made over by him to trustees, and henceforward the home of the Metho- dists, a bit of history that will engage us anon. THE SECOND SANKEY-STREET CHAPEL. We set forth the list of contributions because it is the only information which has reached us of the building of the present Cairo-street Chapel. It is understood that the year of its erection was 1745, and if this be so Dr. Charles Owen would have the satisfaction of seeing it completed and prob- ably of preaching in it before his long ministry came to a close. With a little effort of imagination the chapel as it left the hands of its builders can be pictured. The entrance was from the Sankey-street end, and worshippers passed into a porch from which two doorways opened, one giving access to the ground floor and the other to a stairway that led to the gallery. The gallery filled the 'entire length of the east wall. Opposite to it in the centre of the west wall stood the three-decker pulpit, the table and table pew in front of it and the ground floor filled with square and comfort- able family pews. The chapel retained this form until well within living memory, but various alterations have swept away its old- world features and replaced them with rich- ness and beauty. Memorial tablets in some measure recall the past, but it is left to the old clock with its orthodox black face and gilded fingers to remind us that we are within the walls of an ancient Presbyterian meetingihouse, and that the dust of several generations of worshippers to whom, by long association, the place had become holy ground, rests beneath our feet.</p>
        <p>t, IVRA.RLES OWEN AS CONTROVERSIALIST. ortii Owen's " Plain Speaking " is still of perusal as a defence of the Non- th Iluist position. It appeared in the year ebellion and contains a severe attack Nlt 'lle High Church party for its treat- r, °I Nonconformity. As we have not NAti•oted the honoured doctor we may copy 41;!.evf paragraphs from his introduction 4Y 'ain Speaking." These sentences, at rise well to the level of his title. th, we consider what unhappy circumstances bi artifice of Tories bath redurt'd Protestant ei):Benters to they have at least a Privilege to al' for themselves. fat ell the common people know how Noncon- aotNata are made incapable of being employ, d to meted by the Government, it hich they help I,),BaPport, and are unquestionably faithful to ; bli'ran they see them hunted down by the thread- -142 aud odious Name of Scbismaticks and severe 1"a enacted against them as such ; hat,4ll fine, when they see them deny 'd their t6A„ral nights of Educating their children ; when ace their Persons insulted and ridiculed, and te company of a Presbyterian counted pdalous. tQad', in this case people will be apt to conclude 86 Galileans are certainly Sinners above</p>
