      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NEW THREE-HALFPENNY STAMP.</head>
        <p>NEW THREE-HALFPENNY STAMP.</p>
        <p>Another new value in the Georgian set of postage stamps was issued on Tuesday at the special post office at the Stamp Exhibition in the Horticultural Hall. It is 1 d. brown; the frame design is similar to that of the d. stamp, but the portrait is the same profile as the new id.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. COOPER acid the mission of the deputa- tion was to ask that a town's meeting might be convened in order that they might express to the Government their wishes in the matter. It was a non- party rtessure and, as the worthy Caton bad pointed out, there were two sides to the question. (Miss VVARING : " No, no.") Ile was quite prepared to bowl to the opinions of others, but they were afraid that if they were too drastic they might miss what they were striving to gain. On the other hand, if they allowed the matter to pass by default, it might be their last ehance for many years. The Bill passed its second reading on June 10th; it left the Grand Committee on July 17th and would be before the Horse of Commons again shortly. It was presented by Mr A Lee and was hacked by a worthy array of men whose services to their country were deserving of high esteem. The 13111 sought to make the act of procuration a felony and not, as at present, a misdemeanour. 'I hey were afraid the Bill had been so maimed that that clause had gone. The primary object of the Bill was to give more powers to the police than they now enjoyed but, as the measure now stood, no-one in a lower rank than that of sergeant would he allowed to take action without first procuring a warrant. It was on that point that they might disagree and say that there were two sides to the question, but they hoped to meet that in the way they would formulate the resolution to be submitted to the town's meeting. They thought there was a course which would meet all parties and safeguard the public at large. It was a vital national question which struck at the very root of our morality. If they insisted upon the original wording of the Bill, he was afraid there was not much hope of gaining their point. With full information before it, the House of Commons had already allowed that to go, and he believed the promoters of the Bill acquiesced in it. There was other ground, however, upon which they could have perfect unanimity and strengthen the Bill without affecting the freedom of the public at large, which tome feared was threatened. They were not forcing a private 'Bill upon an unwilling Governmee t, nor were they moving in the teeth of police authority. Not only were additional powere sought by the police but the House of Commons were quite prepared to grant them. The great champion of the movement passed away in the deplorable wreck of the Titanic. It was hoped that the Bill might stand as a national monu- ment to his memory, but that expectation had been somewhat dashed by what lad occurred in the House of Commons. They asked for a town's meeting in order that the situation might yet be saved and tbat Warring- ton's reply to the victims of that vile trade might be "We will do our best for you."</p>
        <p>The Rev. Father Witirrix, after reading some criticisms by Mr. Handel Booth, M.P., expressed him- self as being rather puzzled on certain points. If the meeting had something a little more definite before it, a suitable resoluticn might be formulated. Mrs. RANSOM; on behalf of the Warrington Women's Suffrage society, said they ought to be able to formu- late a resolution NN bleb a town's meeting would pass, without going into details as to the motives of these who proposed the amendments. The Bill, as originally drafted, gave any police officer power to arrest a man on suspicion, but the amendment provided that the</p>
        <p>imprisonment and his act was a felony, but a man who destroyed the life of a girl between the ages of thirteen and sixteen was only guilty of a dismeanour and the maximum punishment was only two years Could there be two sides to the question ? The head of the New York Police had stated that in one year 15,000 girls had been introduced into New York for bad purposes and one syndicate made at, least £40,000 a year out of it. London was a great clearing house for the traffic. It was no use attempting to do anything except through the constituencies. What they wanted was to give members of Parliament a prod, to show that the censtituencies did care in the matter. They must show that chivalry described by Charles Kingsley when be said : "The age of chivalry is never dead when there is one wrong left unredressed on earth and one man or woman to say I will redress that wrong or spend my life in the attempt.' " The Rev Father WHITTLE said he hoped none would assume that, because he had not spoken on the subject of the horrible, degrading abomination that was going on, he agreed with it. By no mews. It was a most horrible blot upon our civilization. He abominated it with all his heart, as a man and as a cle,gyman. It was not that there were two sides to the question of stopp- ing the traffic, but only as to the means to be adopted —whether tha Bill should be passed in its original form or as amended.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said he took it for granted that every- body present was against the white slave traffic. He could hardly imagine anyone having any feeling but that of the utmost horror and hatred towards those abomin- able practices.</p>
        <p>Mr' J J FIRTH (Police Court Missionar)) said the feeling in favour of the Bill WWI undoubtedly strong enough to justify a town's meeting. The MAYOR said he was more than willing to grant a town's meeting on the subject and he suggested the formation of a sub-committee to make arrangements for speakers, &amp;o. On the motion of tha Rev W COOPER, seconded by the Rev Father Wurrmn, a representative committee was appointed.</p>
        <p>.. it was decided to hold the naceticg in the Parr Hall on Wednesday nest.</p>
        <p>HOPE FOR THE BILL.</p>
        <p>To a deputation a hich awaited on him at the Home Office on Monday to urge the passing of the Criminal Law Amendment Bill, 1912 (" White Slave Traffic") without delay, and to voice the opposition to amend- ments nhich are regarded in many quarters as destroy- ing its value, Mr 111‘Kenna said : There is a firm determination that the Bill should be carried through all its stages in the course of the pre- sent Session.</p>
        <p>As far as my personal efforts are concerned, I shall do my utmost to endeavour to see that this Bill occupies an early and prominent place. The deputation included representatives of the joint committee of the Jewish Association for the Protection of Women end Girls, the Jewish Board of Deputies, the National Vigilance Association, London Council for the Promotion of Public Moials, National Free Church Council, West London Mission, and the National Union of Women Workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 19, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 19, 1912.</p>
        <p>WHITE SLAVE TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>TOWN'S MEETING TO BE HELD</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>Deputation to the Mayor.</p>
        <p>In response to the request of an influential depute tion, the Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) has consented to call a town's meeting in support of the Criminal Law Amendment (White Slave Traffic) Bill. The deputation, which waited upon his Worship at the Town Hall on Monday morning, included clergy and ministers of all denominations, repressntativrs of the Guild of Help, Woman's Suffrige Society and other organizations. The RECTuR (Rev. Canon Willis), in introducing the deputation, said he recognized that there were two &amp;des to the question' but, after the greatest con- sideration, had come to the conclusion that it was their duty to press for the original draft of the Bill. The objections raiEed to alme of its pro- visions were not made simply by those who were interested in that, horrible traffic but by really good men who thought that mistakes might be made and the Act discr, d.ted. Aftir very careful con,ideration, however, he would be prepared to take all risks and to take action at Once. The ll,ehop of London, who was in a position to know, said that s,ooogirls tere annually exported from this country t ) one great city alone, for immoral purposes. Such able and intelligent men as the Bishop of London, the Bishop of Manchester and others were prepared to take all risks rather than forego this great opportunity of stopping an abomin- able and iniquitous traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>officer must be a sergeant, specially detailed for the work, and that he must have a warrant. That was a weakeniug amendment because it was so unlikely that a sergeant, specially detailed, would be on the spot, and it would be easy for men who were well acquainted with police movements to know exactly where the special sergeants would be, and to avoid them. She suggested that a resoluticn asking for the weakening amendments to be withdrawn, would have the support of the townspeople, and that it might have a good atm if soot to the members of Parliament for the dis rict.</p>
        <p>• Miss WARING said her reason for being present was that she felt very strongly on the question—more strongly, peihaps, than anyone who bad spoken. The point they ought chiefly to bring out was the necessity for the constituency to say what its wishes were in tlce matter, because members of Parlia- ment seemed to think that the people did not care. The Bill had been on the tspis for five years. Mr Allan Burgoyne declared that it, had been brought in about 100 times, and it had been blocked every time, chiefly through Sir Frederick Banbury and Mr Handel Booth, the sworn enemies of the Bill, for reasons best known to themselves. The Bill was supported by eleven very strong committees in London. It was apparently backed to a certain extent by the Home Office and it was promised that facilities would be given, if forty members of Parliament would ballot for it. Only about twenty responded—it NUS the kind of thing apparently,that did not pay in constituencies For other reasons the Bill was left,but finally it was brought in again because the Liberal women were getting so out of hand that the Government thought it had letter do something as a sop for them. Somebody had said there were two sides to the question. That she could not fore moment believe. There could, to those who cared about purity and morality, be only one side to the question. The police must have the power to stop men front doing one of the most dastardly things on earth, just as they had power to stop a thief in the act. According to the laws of cur land, a man who stole oysters from an oyster bed was liable to five years</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>DEPUTAHON TO TIIE HOME SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>DEPUTAHON TO TIIE HOME SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>On Monday last Mr. McKenna, the Home Secretary, receive-1 a deputation representing eleven associations interested in the Criminal Law Amendment—the White Slave Traffic— Bill. Their object was to urge on Mr. McKenna the ne:essity of making certain alterations in the Bill The deputation,</p>
        <p>reading from loft to right, consisted of Mr. W. L. Alexander, LC., Mr. A. Lee, M.P. (who introduced the deputation), Mr. W. A. Cote (Secretary of the National Vigilance Association), Mr. S. Cohen (Secretary of Conjoint Committee), and Mr. Balden.</p>
