      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LIBERALS AND FOREIGN POLICY. SIR J. BRUNNER'S " PERSONAL" LETTER</head>
        <p>LIBERALS AND FOREIGN POLICY. SIR J. BRUNNER'S " PERSONAL" LETTER</p>
        <p>TO CHAIRMEN.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner, president of the National Liberal Federation, has sent a "personal" letter on foreign policy to the chairmen of Liberal Associations throughout the country. Sir John deplores the growth of expenditure on armaments, and declares that the "Foreign Office and Admiralty have yielded to the . clamour of the Jingoyress in its campaign of mendacity and provocation." If this destructive rivalry in naval armaments goes on unchecked, it threatens (says Sir John) to submerge , civilisation and to destroy society . . . To my mind It is the plain duty of the British Government, which forced modern battleships upon the world, to make amends. There has been a lamented failure to devise remedies for a situation the misery, folly, and wickedness of which have been emphasised over and over again by Mr Asquith and Sir Edward Grey. Perhaps the central mischief is that diplomsoy—by secret treaties and dubious understandings—has twisted and perverted the weloome friendship with France into a dangerous entanglement which has spoiled our relations with Germany. Sir John Brunner suggests that the National Liberal Federation should ask for a change of polioy, which would remove the friction and suspicion by which war is generated, and thus lead to a reduction in armaments and recommends Liberal associations to adopt two resolutions. The first urges the Government to make it clear that the existing relations With France do not imply any understanding or intention as to military or naval action against any other power, and expresses the strong opinion that equally friendly relations should be established with Germany. The second resolution urges the Government to enter into international treaties for the purpose of securing all peaceful shipping and merchandise from capture or destruction in time of war.</p>
        <p>THE CROSS AND THE CRESCENT. At a meeting of the Manchester Diocesan Branch of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel, on Monday night, the principal speakers were the Bishop of London and Mrs Knight-Bruce. The former said Islam was the most formidable enemy of the Cross because of its virtues. Yet Mohammedanism could never raise a nation ever higher like Christianity, and it was their duty, therefore, in love of their brethren, to plant the Cross everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TOLD IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>TOLD IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The word of a neighbour stands for truth, and for this reason the following frank outspoken statement by a Warrington woman cannot be questioned. Mrs. E. Sadlow, of 117, Wellfield-street, Bank Quay, Warrington, says :—" My back and kidneys have caused me a lot of suffering at different times, but about eight or nine menthe ago I was very ill. Cruel cutting pains seized me right across the small of my back, and in my sides; I could not shake them off. "After doing a little work I felt quite prostrated. I used to come over so dizzy that I cculd hardly stand, and I always felt drowsy and tired. "I was troubled with nasty headaches and indiges- tion. I became low-spirited and weak. The action of the bladder was irregular, and I could tell that the kidneys were in a bad state. "Being recommended to try Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, I did so; and before I had used one box I could tell they were doing me good. I con- tinued with the pills until the pains had all gone. " I have felt like another woman since—bright and full of energy. If I have any return of the trouble, Doan's pills will be the first thing I shall fly to, for I believe in sticking to a medicine that has done one gar]. "lam very well now, and am pleased to give this account of my cure for publication." 2/9 a box, 6' boxes 1319 ; of all Dealers. Ask for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pills, lske Mrs Sud/ow had.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE INSURANCE COMMITTEE. The Lancashire Insurance Committee met at Preston on Monday. The Sub-committee reported that the County Council Public Health Committee, which had co-opf+rated with it heartily, proposed to take forty beds in the Meathop Sanatorium on the understanding that the Insurance Committee would send patients there. It was also stated that it had been calculated that there would be 2,000 patients a year. for whom 600 or 700 beds would be needed. The Sub-cornm4tee was criticised for not having called a meeting of the full Committee for three months, foNhaving, it was argued, come to arrangements beyond its powers, and for not having taken offices and otherwise carried out instructions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETY NEWS.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETY NEWS.</p>
        <p>Shepherds' Quarterly Meeting. The quarterly meeting of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds (A.U.) Warrington District, was held on Saturday last under the presidency of the District Chief Bro J W Pomfrey, supported by the Deputy Prov. Chief Bro W E Clegg, Bro James Parr, District Treasurer, and Bro Peter L Edgar, District Secretary. P.P.C.S. Bro Jai; Lowe was elected auditor or the next six months. It was unanimously decided to open a Female Branch of the society in Warrington. Also the meeting decided to recommend lodges to open juvenile branches. An interesting item of the proceedings was the presentation of a bands 'me timepiece to P.P.C.S. Bro T S Champion for services rendered to the District as Pro. Chief Shepherd. The presentation was made by Bro. II Taylor in a manner fitting the occasion and was well support( dby many of the delegates. At the clo.e of the proceedings Bro. Champion suitably acknowledged the presentation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Oddfellows' Quarterly Meeting.</head>
        <p>Oddfellows' Quarterly Meeting.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity) Warrington District was held on Stturday last at the Prince of Wales Lode, Pelican Hotel, Warrington, under the presi- dency of P G.M. Herbert Whitelegg, who was sup- ported by the district officers and a full complement of delegates from the nine lodges f rming the district. The minutes of the last meeting having been read and confirmed, the accounts were presented and passed as satisfactory, the capital of the Funeral Fund being £4,769 19s 91.1. The report of the representatives who attended the meeting of the Lancashite and Adjacent Districts Conference at Fleetwood on September 21st was given by PD G.M. G Higham and accepted. The new tables of contributions and benefits were formally adopted, and the openirg of a new Lodge at the Parochial School, Lynam, called the Loyal Victoria Female Lodge, was confirmed. This makes the third lodge for females opened in the district. It NVIS reported to the meeting that the committee of the 'Children's Buiial Society would provide regalia for the three lodges for females and a cordial vote of thanks was accorded to the committee for their generosity. Nominations were taken for the offices of Prov. G.M. and Prov. D.G.M ; for the former office Prov. D.G.M. G. Hik.ham was nominated and for the latter office P.G. H Lawrence (niece of Wales Lodge), P.G. W Webster (Admiral Nt Isola dge) P.G. S 0 Ashton (Navigation Lodge), and P.G. A E Ilurdttt (Constitution Le de, were nominated; the elections will take place at the next meeting on 11th iJanuary, 1913. A. vote of thanks to the cliairmat. brcugbt the Lusiness to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Oddfellows Loyal Olive Branch.</head>
        <p>Oddfellows Loyal Olive Branch.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening last a social and distribution of bowling prizes took place at the above Lodge. There was also a presentation of a Past Grande Jewel suitably inscribed to P.G. George Brown who has recently completed his term in connection with the various (flees of the Lodge. It was all° the District Officers offii ial visit and they met wilh a hearty welcome from the hr thren present. The transaction of the ordinary business of the Lodge was conducted by Noble Grand John Brown after which the chairs were occupied by the District rfficers. Amongst thore present were Prov. G.M. Herbert Whitelegg, Deputy G.M. George Higham P.P.G.M. Frank Webster (district secretary), and I.P.R G.M. Peter Les'er. The officers of the Lodge were NO John Brown, NG. Fred Broadhurst., L.G.M. William Hammon, minute secretary, H Bentley P.G. A Cotterill (treasurer), and P.P.G M. Alban Boyle (secretary). There were many visiting brethren also ,present. At the conclusion of the ordinary business:of the Lodge Grand Master W bitelegg havirg made the presentation to P.G. George Brown , Deputy 0 M Higham then presented the prizes to the successful competitors in the recent bowling main held in connection with the Lodge at the Coffee House Hotel, Oateacre 1, Bro A Glover; 2, Bro J Mather; P.O A Cotterill, PG William Hammon, Bro T A Hardman, P.O John Boyle, sen., P.G. William Houghton, Bros George Savage, F Shoebridge,J Boyle, jun., and P.P.G.M. F Webster. A vote of thanks was unanimously passed to the friends who had kindly furnished the prizes. The social part of the proceed- ings was well sustained and some good songs were rendered by Bros S Taylor, Oswald Turner, P.O Arthur Cotterill, P.O William Webster, P.P.G.M. Alban Boyle and P.O T Seed. Mr Alfred Grounds presided at the piano in his usual able manner and after the singing of the National Anthem a very pleasant evening was brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Ancient Order of Foresters.</head>
        <p>Ancient Order of Foresters.</p>
        <p>The 203rd quarterly Warrington District meeting was held on Saturday list at the Roebuck Hotel, Bridge-street. There were present D.C.R. G Wit Baines in the chair, D.S CR. T Roden vice-chair, P.D.C.R. P Peacock district treasurer, P.D.C.R. R Thornley district secretary, and the following court delegates: Court 133, P.D.C.R. W Hipkiss, P.C.R. W Rnylance, Court 240, P.O R H Emmerson, P.C.R. J Davies, Cout t 430, P.D.C.R. J Chritnes, P.C.R. E Clayton, P.O R. W James, Court 431, P.C.R. J Smith, P.C.R. A Sankey, Court 461, C.S. H Stafford, Cout t 475, P D.C.R. J. Grayson, P.C.R. J H Hollins C curt, 9335, P.D.C.R. A Tilling P.D.C.R. G Maybury, district trustees P.D.C.R. J Walsh, P.D.C.R. It James, also P.C.R. J Janus and P.C.R. P Wilding, sen. After formally opening the meeting the District Chief said be had a duty to per- form before entering on the business and that was to move a vote of sympathy to the relatives of the late Bro Bancroft, District Trustee. The Sub District Chief seconded,and the motion was passed the delegates all standing. The Court returns were then taken but owing to the large Lumber d r.ew members returned this quarter to the District, the largest on record, it was resolved to pass them as written, and any question arising out of same to be settled by the District Secretary and Court Secretary. The cash account was then taken and passed. Bros S B Healey and W James presented their report as District Auditors and said the District had need to congratulate itself on the numerical and financial progress that was being made, and testified to the able manner the District Treasurer and Secretary had kept the accounts. Their report was accepted and they were thanked for the thorough and efficient way they had conducted their audit. Bros W Hipkiss and G W It Baines then gave their report as delegates to the last North-Western- Federation meeting held at West Kirby. They produced very useful information as a result of their deliberations and it was resolved that the District secretary should write to the North- Western Federation Secretary to the Effect that we were in favour of another meeting in January next in order to elicit further information in connection with the National Insurance Act. It was resolved that District Officers, Court Secretaries and Bro. G W R Baines act as advisory committee in lieu of present Insurance Committee.— It was unanimously resolved that Past District Secre- tary Bro. L Davies be appointed District Ttustee to fill the vacancy caused by the death of Bro. J Bancroft. It was also unanimously resolved that D.O R.'Bro. G W R Baines be presented with a P.D.C.R. Ribbon and framed emblem. Bros. T Roden, Court 430, and S B Healey, Court 138, were then formally installed as D.C.R. and S.D.C.R. respectively fur the next twelve months</p>
