      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>WHERE THE TOYS COME FROM.</p>
        <p>Though we have hardly yet begun to think about Christmas, the toys that will soon be decking the shop windows are on the way in large quantities, and the toy makers are very busy. Perhaps my readers would like to know a little about toys and where they are made. Everybody know:- of course that they are imported and that many of them are "made in Germany." The little German town of Sonneberg, in Thuringia, is recog- nized as the largest toy manufacturing centre in the world. In addition to its summer resort business, it has been credited with the annual production of some 24,000,000 toys, the value of which is nearly a million pounds. The manufacture of toys is also largely conducted in the famous walled city of Nuremberg in Bavaria. These two cities have become famed for the quantity of their toy products, supplying about 80 per cent. of all the toys exported from the empire. Each city has its own speciality and in Sonneberg everything is made by hand. There are about 40,000 people engaged in making toys in Sonneberg and in the villages in the Thuringian forests. Fully 75 per cent of this number work in their own homes. , The papier-mache toys are largely made there, and dolls of every imaginable variety are produced. Doll clothing is the special work of Sonneberg women and girls. In the neighbouring villages of Schichtshorn, L Fichtaeh and Effeldere, wagons, trumpets, whistles, etc., are produced in large quanti- ties. Over in Heinersdorf the people make a speciality of carnival masks. In the little village of Neufang, toy animals and fowls are fitted up with furs and feathers. .Nuremberg has little of the house industry. It makes toys with machinery in factories equipped with all modern appliances. The products of Nuremberg are principally of metal—tin soldiers, swords, railway trains, fleets, models of machinery and other toys intended for boys—while Sonneberg uses almost exclusively wood, porcelain, glass and paper in the production of toys best suited to girls. Many hand-made toys come from united Switzerland. Frequently the Alpine traveller comes upon chalets in the valley or high upon the mountain side, which, he is astonished to find, are littered with minia- ture wooden figures. Elephants, horses camels, are mixed up with soldiers, priests, and peasants. Sitting around on rude benches, are the entire family all engaged in toy-making. The work goes on all the year round with no rest save for eating and sleep- ing. For the making of these wooden toys has been taught by parent to child in Switzer- land. for centuries and all the world's children get these toys, from the great markets of Paris, New York and London. The Le Droz family was the most famous of :ingenious toy inventors in Switzerland. The honor among Germans seems to have ' fallen upon a woman—Mrs. Margaret Steiff —who possesses genius of a high order. The animals she designs are inimitable—the most notable being the popular "Teddy Bear" (originally the "Russian bear "). Mrs. Steiff is an invalid. She began making animals as a recreation. The business into which she was led in an accidental way has expanded to great iproportions, and is conducted, in the town of Guigen on the River Brenz, Wurtemberg, Germany, by her sons who have recently erected a huge fire-proof factory. They make not only "Teddy bears," but also many other kinds of toy animals designed by their mother, Mrs. Steiff, such as camels, giraffes, dogs and monkeys. THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>William had just returned from college, resplendent in silk hosiery, a fancy waistcoat, and a necktie that spoke for itself. He entered the library where his father was reading. The old gentleman looked up and surveyed his son. The longer he looked up the more disgusted he became. "Son," be finally blurted out, "you look like a fool l"</p>
        <p>Later, the old major who lived next door came in and greeted the boy heartily, " William," he said, with undisguised admiration, "you look exactly like your father did thirty years ago when he came back from school 1"</p>
        <p>" Yes," replied William, with a smile, "so father was just telling me."</p>
        <p>THE DUKE OF CONNAUGUIr IN CANADA.</p>
        <p>The Duke of Connaught has promised to visit Brandon, Manitoba's second largest city, this month on his return from the Pacific Coast. Brandon's progress of late years Las been on an extraordinary scale. The city holds the proud position of being the largest wheat-shipping centre in the whole of the west. The surrounding district, moreover, offers a notable object lesson of what may be achieved in connection with mixed farming on the prairie, the breeding of high grade cattle, sheep and horses and by dairying and poultry raising. The most important of Canada's experimental farm stations is located just outside the city, and is 'visited by thousands of farmers each year. The annual agricultural show is also the most important gathering of this nature in the west. Brandon has been made a divisional point by Canada's three transcontinental railways, and the facilities for distribution thus provided are rapidly making the city an industrial centre as well. An extensive street rail- way system is new being constructed, which is owned by the corporation.</p>
      </div>
