      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>" TUE REPORTERS HAD LEFT." This, quoted from the report of the Leverson ease at the Warrington County Court, applies likewise to a recent experience of Mr. Victor Grayson, of which an old colleague has told me th's week. ,The erratic young S(cialist champion has been touring South-East Lancathire, and, by a series of remarkable coiocid- ences, has turned up late at one after another. At a meeting at Nelson, the perplexed promoters put up another to occupy the time, and the speaker t ok the opportunity to vigorously denounce Syndicalism. When on the belated lecturer's arrival, the man in pos'ession gave way far him, Mr. Grayson trthwith preached in characteristic fashion the very doctrine, the errors of which the other had been industriously exposing. Within a week or s Dlr. Grayson had somewhat t imilar mishaps in at least thrce other ca, es At Todmorden, for instance, the local comrades had been assured by telegram that he would fulfil the engageruelit. To keep faith with their public, the chairman and supporters duly mounted tie platform and ck mmenced the meeting, explaining that Mr. Grayson had not yet reached the hall, but that he was expicted momentarily. The chairman gave his opening address, and proceeded to call upon a mem ber of the committee to hold forth. This gentleman did his best, but eventually ran down - for the time. Then the chairman had another go at the close of which he begged the other speaker to try again. Still no tidings of Mr. Grayson. As it became ouly too evident that the bewildered chairman and his friend were merely talking against time, reople in the audience began after the manner of the Arabs, to more or less " silently steal away." It was a quarter t nine when Mr. Grayson made his appearance on the platform, saving that, owing to the stupidity of a rail- way poi ter he had teen unavoidably del.,yed down t' e lioe. Attention was then called to the a'arruing circumstance that The reporters had left," Messer,- ,rts were hastily despatched in search of the rnissiog scribes, but only one could be found, and he was dis covere 1 at his home f one distarce away. What occasioned the delay in the other instances I do not know; I kuow that in ne of them the chairman was report d to have c mplained that it was likely to have a bad GI et upon the public. THE STAT lONMA STE R.</p>
        <p>Um award of a prize to Mr. Ge-rge Tar ncr on account of his well-k ept Moore Station, affords a coN- venient opportunity for a word of prais3 of varioos other stitioninasters in these pat ts 1 have the utun s. admiration for these offici Am, and elsewhere gave my Mion of them only the ot er day. The fact is that their ‘37)ik is a great deal heavier and try re ex ictiDg than an occasional passenger or supet ficiA cheerio) might supi 030. Some of us who have had the advan- tage of a glance over the Rule Book, who know at least of the existence if instruction books is-md 13.3 the Goods and Audit apartments, and who hive reason t 3 more than suspect thit thew-3 13y no m-aos exhaust the list of regulations laid down for the guidance of the constantly badgered and sorely- tried officials referred to, could as to these thb,gs unfold a tale well calculated to disarm adverse ethics and set them wondering at the patience and forbear- ance often shown by our stationmasters under most trying cimumstances. The holder of the (ffice in question is " responsible for the faithful and ifficient discharge of the duties devolving upon all the com- pany's servants" employed at his station, so that he should be careful not to err either by undue laxity on the cne band or paltry tyranny on the other. He must give himself no leisure until he has acquired a prac- tical knowledge of every detail of the work, "from the invoicing or abstracting of a parcel or the booking of a passenger to the working of the traffic in cases of accident or obstruction." The responsibility for his employers' business at aid in connection with his station necessarily rests upon his shoulders. As their local agent or representative he is practically at the beck and call of all and sundry for information and assistance of various kinds. Bound to see that the work as a whole is invariably done in strict accordance with regulations, bewildering indeed to the lay mind but usually to be found at his fingers' ends, and at the same time to as far as practicable give personal attention to all that affects the safety tf the line, the diligent and faithful and courteous stationmaster deserves the atm at con- sideration at the hands of the public he so admirably serves. WORSE THAN ELECTION EGGS.</p>
        <p>The paragraph in the "Examiner " respecting a disturbance at a political meeting at which eggs were thrown at the occupants of the platform reminded me of similar experiences in the old days of open voting. I have seen some ugly rushes at the meetings and dl sorts of missiles thrown indiscriminately at candidates and their immediate supporters. Once, at an indoor gathering, a rival faction armed with sticks st lined the platform so suddenly and unexpectedly that we reporters dived beneath the table for shelter, the attacking party using cur table as a ready means of gaining their objective. At the same time it served to "cover our defenceless heads"— and incidentally our notebooks. Moreover we had abundant com- pensation in the shape of good " copy." Oa another occasion in thve lively days, I found a hostile mob menac'ng opponents as they approached a public hall for a meeting during a general election. I happened t.) be among those who " knew something," and so managed to get inside without difficulty. The hall was barricadtd t• as it' in anticipation of a fierce assault, and, as the sequel Proved, these precautions were wise. Before the pro- ceedings ended, the enemy had smashed every window in the building, but, as all had been well boarded up inside, none of the stones hurled from outside did any further mischief. We eventually emerged under strong police escort, and few of those taking part were the worse for the risks they had undergone. SLIPS BY TIIE WAY._ _ _</p>
        <p>o 0</p>
        <p>—-- _ A member of the local Parliament was caught tripping, as mentioned in these column last week. I was somehow reminded of various other instances, and may give two or three examples of a rather different character. At the civic function givcn by way of reception to the Bishop of Peterborough, a local speaker made use of the following words "In the past we have bad in our b;shops heloquence (Magee) and h&amp;udition (Creighton). Now, thank God, we have neither, but a good sound evangelical." Mr. James Milne tells how, when the Duke of Kent, the father of Queen Victoria, was a young man he spent acme time in Quebec on military service. He there formed an alliance with a French-Canadian lady, and it lasted till he married for reasons of State. A Canadian has a little camp bcdotead which belonged to the Duke. He was showing it, s ith other relics to a French lady, who said, " Yes, very interesting, but so very narrow ; only for one—where, where, did Madame sleep ?" " Oh," Troth be "she slept in the arms of Morpheus." Morpheus ! Monsieur Morpheus exclaimed his visitor ; " but who was he ? Ah ! " she added swiftly, "I know ; you mean the Duke's aide-de- camp !" Au Englishmen sitting outside a café in a European city remarked to a fellow countryman, "Oh, the Emperor is a hopeless idiot !" Instantly a plain- clothes policeman, rose from an adjacent seat and said : "Sir, I arrest you for Rtse majestd. You say that the Emperor is a hopeless idiot." "My dear chap," said the Englishman, " I didn't mean your Emperor. There are other Emperors in the world, surely !" " That may be, sir," replied the officer ; " but curs is the only one to whom the description applies. YOI must come with me !" And is the Press wholly exempt ? Alas, no ! A theological student was sent coo Sunday to supply a vacant pulpit in s small town in Yorkshire. A few days later he received a copy of the local weekly paper with the following item marked: "Rev. supplied the pulpit at the Congregational Church last Sunday, and the church will now be closed three weeks for repairs." A Boanergcs indeed ! TOWN-PLANNING. Wednesday's L-cal Government Board inquiry may be said to have appreciably advanced Warrington's town-planning scheme. I recall a Liverpool confer- ence at which a Mr T C Horsfall, of Manchester, pointed out that the Town Planning Act, through one of its clauses, was the most democratic revolution that had taken place in his time, in that it gave the local authority power by limiting the use made of the land to limit the price. As the chairman on the same occasion observed, even th( se who considered the Act under which Warrington is taking these steps was a bad Act must allow that it was a great deal better than no Act at all, and that it was sufficiently good to more than justify all the trouble that was taken to get it on the Statute Book. I further remember how, replying to the toast of his health at a banquet given by the Board cf Manage- ment of to partnership Tenants, Limited, Earl Grey remarked that, thanks to this Act, the path of the reformer bad been rendered more smooth and level. They had diagnosed the disease that lay at tire root of , national inefficiency and had proved that the remedies</p>
      </div>
