      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Who pla3 s the title role in</head>
        <p>Who pla3 s the title role in</p>
        <p>" BELLA DONNA."</p>
        <p>Poisors and ti eir wonderful results always have a scat of fascination for the public, and the picture of one of our favourite and m-st beautiful act: eases con- cocting ccffee with which to ioison her husband Vral3 certainly one of the biggest draws in London during the last two sr asons; and no wonder. It was ttrong enough to draw even the rf doubtable CI ipren, had lie ncrt been so sure that ho was already a draw on his own, at Madame TurrsauTa. Reverting to the Crippen case, we remember at the time of the great trial that Sir George Alexander himself was a con- stant visitor, not for the purpose of staging a great tri,ll scene ; but we know now it was for the purrose of stud)ing the minute results of poisons for use in his groat poison play "Bella Donna," which is being presented at the Royal Court Theatre next week. "Bells Donna" was a huge success in London, and is even a bigger success in the country, and the visit to Warrington is sure to create a most profound impression.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>"Bella Donna" at the Royal Court Theatre, Next Week.</head>
        <p>"Bella Donna" at the Royal Court Theatre, Next Week.</p>
        <p>could be safely administered without injustice to a single individual. Bourneville, York, Sunlight, Letchwoith, Ealing, Liverpool and Hampstead bad each proved that towns built on garden city lines pro- vided ideal homes at a price below that charged for a dog-kennel existence in the slums. Unfortunately local authorities in some parts of the country are bringing their influence to boar to prevent philan- thropic associations from introducing imprord housing accommodation. With the Local Government Board behind such associations indifferent authorities will doubtless be compelled to keep pace with the times. One is proud to think that hereabout there is no need of whip or spur. That we have our slams is undeniable, but public opinion has of late been greatly enlightened and the puh:ic conecierce stirred in rela- tion to the evil, and assuredly the not distant future will see it materially minimised if not entirely eradicated. As Earl (key said on the memorable occasion to which I just now referred, these miserable slums which have been allowed in deference to the sacred rights ot individual liberty to disfigure our civilization too long, are the real cause of much of the misery that has impoverished and enfeebled the life of our people. That the slums are responsible for preventible disease, intemperance, immorality, iosanity and crime, and for much pre- ventible, physical, as well as moral and mental degradation is now pretty generally recognised, and ' true lovers of their kind gladly hail the prospect of the early disappearance of the .vile slums from our midst as well as the duly-controlled and systematic development along scientific lines of the estates over which the environs aro ere long bound to extend. EXAMPLES OF MODERATION.</p>
        <p>At the Licensed Victuallers' meeting here last week, the Mayor of Warrington confided to the members of the League that he had formed a high °Onion of their I character, both as regarded their honour and their mode of life—in a great measure, almost generally, their temperate mode cf life, which was a very important thing in their old and honourable calling. I have myself known some exceedingly temperate men of this class—men who had a good deal more sense than to themselves imbibe much of the stuff they were naturally enough desirous that that their customers should drink freely. I believe you have previously been told of a brewers' traveller of my intimate acquaintance who, oh] iged to call fcr something at each house he expected an order at, habitually took a small whiskey and invari- ably emptied it into his umbrella as soon as the land- lord's back was turned I also knew personally the license of a certain hotel who was so popular that be daily nocived more than the average number of invitations t a glass with this, that and the other customer. On this account he kept a special decanter of toast and water, and the mixture was so carefully prepared that it perfectly resembled the whiskey sup- plied to the friend who was "standing treat." This served a double purpose : it kept the landlord sober, and enabled him to get through considerable quantities of the cheap and harmless decoction at the price of whiskey! It went on for many years before it was detected.</p>
        <p>missionaries we bad sent out the means of erecting large educational establishments which would touch the young men of China and which would put before the China that was to be the great aspect of civilisation which we associated with the name of England. (Applause.) The collections for the society amounted to £l6 12s, and this together with £3lls collected before the meet- ing totalled a sum which was £8 in advance of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WOMEN OF ENGLANDHERE IS YOUR LIBERTY.</head>
        <p>WOMEN OF ENGLAND- HERE IS YOUR LIBERTY.</p>
        <p>To all those women who, day in and day out, year after year, in god weather and bad, have to fight an unending fight against the cares and worries that crowd into a woman's life, there is a message. It is this—that they may now meet these troubles without the heavy hand:cap that has been theirs' hitherto. From to-day on, there is no reason tt hy a woman who reads this should find herself incapacitated any time by those ailments which women alone are called upon to bear. When she feels that frightful pain in her back and sides, when her head feels as though it would burst, and there comes such a sense of depression and bearing down that she does not know which way to turn, she is suffering need- lessly. Her sufferings will become a thing of the past if she will heed the message of a Salford lady, who writes :—" For some years I was a great sufferer from certain ailments of women. I wrote for your Private Text Book for Women, which I found very instructive as a guide. I have taken Lydia E. Pink- ham's Vegetable Compound, which has done me tremendous good, giving me immediate relief, and I now feel a different woman. I shall have the greatest pleasure in recommending your valuable Remedy to all I can," All suffering women should write to-day for this Private Book (to women only). The Lady Manager, c/o The Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead Road, London, N.W., will send it free. She will also send the medicine post free, or it can be had from any chemist at 2s. 9d. a bcttle, or 4s. 63. for a larger bottle.</p>
        <p>, NEW K.C.'s. Seventeen newly appointed King's Counsel were "called within the Bar," with the usual formalities, at the Courts of Justice on Tuesday.</p>
      </div>
