      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>S.P.G. MEETING.</head>
        <p>S.P.G. MEETING.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF THE REV. LORD WILLIAM CECIL</p>
        <p>The interest which is taken in the work of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign parts, in Warrington, was manifested at the annual public meet. lug of the Society which was held in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening. The spacious hall was crowded almost to over-flowing. The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) presided, and he was supported by a large number of the local clergy and the Right Rev. the Lord Bishop of Bunbury (W. Australia), the Right Rev. the Lord Bishop of Guiana and the Rev. Lord William Cecil.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said the cause which they were met to support was a grand and noble one in which all ought to take an interest. He was glad to observe from the proportions of the audience that Warrington did take aninterest in the society. They who enjoyed the benefits of Christianity, and the advantages of Christian example ought to do their utmost to spread that Christian knowledge to those who were ignorant, and to diffuse the light of Christianity amongst those who sat in darkness, fie hoped that that meeting, along with others they would hold, would bring about the noble object for which they were assembled. (Applause). , The BISHOP OF GUIANA greatly interested the audi- I ence with his tales of life in the diocese of which he was the head. lie spoke of the generous way in which the blecks gave their services to help the Church and said Englishmen were just beginning to think that they might pay something for their religion ; the black people paid a definite sum per head for the maintenance of the Church amongst them. Whcle churches there were built with free labour. At present, Christians were allowed to minister to heathens and pagans. Gradually those people would step more and more into the places of leaders themselves. They should tieze the opportunity now because it would pass away in the development of the world's history. The 13teHor OF BUNBURY raid there were certain links between his diocrse in Australia and the district of Warrington. The Archdeacon (Mr Louch) who was administering his diocese was thirty years ago a curate with the Rev EC E Carleton. The Secretary of the Warrington Junior Clergy Missionary Association (Mr Adams) had sent his elder brother to Western Australia to take charge of the cathedral pariah. Ceding with the work which was being done in Australia his Lordship said Australis always felt that she had been badly treated by the Old Country, becauee when the first cargo of convicts atd soldiers was sent out, England never took the trouble to send out a chaplain, and Mr Johnson had gone out of his own , accord. (Applause.) England was saved from shame with regard to Australia almost exclusively by the splendid work and devotion of tbe S.P.G. and the S Australia was going to be one of the big nations of the world, but was it going to be a Dives or not? That remained for the English Church in some degree to ray. (Hear, hear). The Rev Lord WILLIAM CECIL said the question before the world et the present time was that Western civilisation with its goodness and its badness hed hitherto merely existed among the white men. When it came across other races it had stopped. Now, for Instance, it had stopped. It had never conquered the Turk. Superficially it had touched Japan. The problem was the awakening of China. The great revolution in China was not merely a revolution like we had had in 1 England or in France. It was more far reaching than either of those. It had been the absolute throwing on one side of the whole of their Eastern knowledge and the whole of their civilisaticn. They had cast off their great philosophic Confucianism with the result that they were really withopt any principles. A quarter of the world's population had cast aside old ideas, traditions and religion and to-day China was in the melting pot. At this moment there was within our power a means of making that vast force for good or evil. We should further educate the men who went out there so that the Chinese would get the right ideas. (Hear, bear.) There were great elements of evil in Western civilize- tion,and the elements were being interpreted to China by men of different nations. Which ampect of civiliza- tion would China take? If it were only to be left to Englishmen one would have to speak in very pessimistic tones indeed. One would have to say that the Chinese would accept merely a materialistic interpretation of our civilization, missing all that was noble and grand In it. The people who bad done the most good in China were not the Eoglish, but the Americans—and he said it in shame—who had seen that a country could be altered by educating and altering its student classes. They had erected in nearly every town splendid educa- tional establishments, and from these had proceeded the great reforming influences of China. He would not say that the Englisff had been wholly negligent in that work, but nc-ene could go through China and doubt which nation had done the most work. The new China was made in America. Wes that as it ought to be ? In conolus;on his lordship said we were apt to look at the past with regret. Was it not sometimes wiser to think of the future ? Let them look at the question of why the Wesleyana separated from the church and read the life of Wesley,who was a High Churchman, and then ask why the Wesleyans were allowed to cut themselves off?—The answer was because the Cherch was asleep and did not realize the importance of the Wesleyan move- ment. In years to come the question of China would be one of the great questions of regret if we did not realise the importance of the yellow world. We bad something to give to China which America could not give—some power of vitality and an aspect of the truth which we alone saw, and it would be the greatest misfortune if English Christanity did not find worthy exponents in China—if we were not ready to give</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH IRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH IRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>GRIEF STRICKEN MOTHER'S SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>Mr. T, Ridgway conducted an inquest at the Stockton Heath Police Station on Monday on the body of Mrs. Emma Houghton (59), a widow, the proprietress of the Bon Marche drapery establishment, who was found asphyxiated in her bedroom on Saturday morning.—Mr A J Willett represented tho relatives and Mr F R Roberts was present on behalf of the executors. From the evidence it appeared that the deceased lady was in her usual spirits when Miss Marian Latham her niece, of Mill House, Bold, left her about 10 o'clock on Friday evening. This witness also stated that she heard no noise during the night and in keeping with her custom and the wishes of her aunt she did not go in the bedroom in the morning but left the house at S 10. Iler aunt had never complained of her health and she had not appeared, depressed. She had not seen the gas tube (produced) before. Her aunt was in the habit of getting up in the night as she suffered from indigestion. In reply to Mr. Roberts she said she had been staying with her aunt a fortnight. She could not say whether there was a reading lamp in the room. Miss Isabella Sherwood, assistant at the Bon Marche said that when she left the shop on Friday evening about 020 Mrs. Houghton was very cheerful. They had been to the theatre at Manchester on the Thursday and the deceased used to go to Liverpool end Manchester often. She opened the shop on Saturday morning at 8.80. Mrs Houghton usually came down at any timo up to 10.30 and about 10.55 witness went upstairs to her bedroom. She found the door closed and on entering the room saw Mrs Houghton in a chair with a shawl over her bead but she (witness) thought she had put the shawl on herself to keep warm. Mrs Houghton was, however, dead and witness sent for the doctor. She did not notice the gas tube nor did she smell gas. Mrs Houghton lost her son about two years ago at sea and had worried about it. Replying to Mr Willett, witness said that the mat was against the door, and the door, window and ventilator were closed. She did not 5m311 any gas as she bad a cold. She had noticed the dead son's diary by the washstand. The deceased, who had joked about suicide, appeared very placid and there was no die- colouration. She had DO teed to trouble about money, as she had £lOO per year coming in from an estate. By the Coroner : The tube was used to connect the Fas bracket to an incandescett reading lamp, which was on a chest of drawers.</p>
        <p>The Foreman : Was there any message or communi- cation other than the diary ? Witness : No ! The diary was not hers, but the one of her son's which was sent home with his belongings when he died at sea.</p>
        <p>Mr W Mc'ape said he was called to the Bon Marche about 11 o'clock. When he went upstairs he noticed a strong smell of gas on the landing. He saw Mrs Houghton in the chair. Her head and shoulders were completely covered by a shawl and one end of the gas tube lay on her breast and the other end was hanging over the back of the chair. There was also a piece of gas Labe on the gas bracket. The gas was turned fully on.</p>
        <p>Miss Sherwood, recalled, said she moved the shawl from Mrs Ifoughton's face when she went upstairs and ehe did not know whether she replaced it In the same position.</p>
        <p>Sergeant White said he saw the shawl over the face of the deceased. A reading lamp was at the far corner of the room. In answer to the foreman, the sergeant said the gas pipe would reach from the gas bracket to where the deceased was sitting. A verdict of " Suicide while temporarily insane " was returned, and the jury expressed their sympathy with the relatives Mr Willett returned thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ROUNDERS.</head>
        <p>ROUNDERS.</p>
        <p>,‘ FULTON SMITH" ROSE BOWL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>FINAL. Sr. JAMES' V. FAIRFIELD. This trophy will be held for the en.uing year by St. James' who, in the final with Fairfield at Bank Park on Tuesday, scored 85 runs (for five out) against 60. The above teams with St. Baruabas' tied for the league championship and imasession of the rose bowl. An extra two matchrs were ordered to be played and another tie was the result, each team winning one match. A draw was then made as follows :—St. James' vSt Barnabes', Fairfield a b”.. List week St. James' were successful in defeating St. Barnabas' by CO runs, and met Faiifleld as reported above.</p>
        <p>THIRST ON THE TWO-DREADNOUGHT STANDARD. Judge Rentoul, at the Old Bailey on Tuesday said the workingmen of this country drank about two millions sterling worth of beer per week—the price of a Dread- nought.</p>
