        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>-- Golden Autumn.—For those who delight lU charms of an autumn landscepe,a ramble in the coati. at the present season affords a rare pleasure. In ice;:l",,, land, hedgerow, and orchard, there is glorious displs., Seldom does Nature disclose such an intensity f; beauty and rarity of tint. Occasional frosts at nigr and strong winds have brought down the leaves In shoals, so there is still a riot of colour in the foliage_ The rambler's eye rests upon well-ordered fields wber° the plough is turning up the soil which has yielded 1;0' increase ; rifts in the trees let light and sunshine Isne'd the heart of the wosds, and smiling homestead, /Wen with newly-thatched stacks of grain, and her, ere cattle grazing in the meadows, help to make a plc of rural peace, and let us hope, of plenty. Rubel 'the Care of Indoor Plants.—lt is always diffiult matter to keep indoor plants healthy and lyne. fires, and long hours of artificial light in our slates. roomy, they need great care and constant attentiose The ferns suffer most from the dry, heated atmosPb°,ris of warm rooms, and should be removed occasions%', from these adverse conditions, but, like all inte plants they must be kept out of draughts and set 14'. is as much fresh air as possible. Perfect cleanliness essential, to the well-being of all plants kept in roopbso and, after sponging the leaves of pelms, they amnia dried vtith a soft cloth. Plants and ferns with Issues can only be entered end left to drain. winter table greenery, there is nothing better this 'el ruSellium, whist' is pretty, aleays Leeps its shaeet"./s proportions and is exceptionally timely. No plant Toil however, thrive in a dark corner, or in a draught9 or landing, and in no CA6O should the leaves he allots to touch a wall or curtain. ere In Aid of D gestion.—A promenade on all after every meal, is cow recommended by the I 11€• eOl profession to sufferers from indigestion. An ern'""Sre food specialist, assures us that such crab-like proced works wonders and that it is infinitely to be prefererie to all the pills and powders ever devised. There srnit'ol however, appear to be difficulties in the carrying o,ll"ist such a scheme. Of course, when taking one's WO be the seclusion of ore's own home, nothing could _d eesior than to retire to one's private apartment, sad there crawl, comfortably, for the prescribed fift,ebs minutes, telt at a luncheon, dinner, or supper peel, a notion scarcely seems to be felsible. Frocks, Tsg trousers would be hopelessly ruined, and a dra,c'el room full of eras line guests c uld scarcely present edifying appearance. Pearls —Although the chemists and scientists ngrie. discue-ing the possibility of creating life from Inglis, mate matter, they carnot yet 'produce artifieial ee,%te which can be sold vs natural ones. They can innythe some a the preci( us stones so nearly, that„ol artiflciel are almost indistinguishable from the na,'"ibe gems, Ste, pearls defy all eff rts to g ropuce them 19 ice, laboratory. Heal pe ids are over in cuasing in Per„ce and their value is unaffected by the attempts that '),i been made to cultiva, e their formation naturallP;sto ilitroducing a smell pliers of ro'cre, or seed pear "ers the middle of the shell when the oyster is three Ye sf o'd, and returning it to the sea. This metbocc teeming the pearl oyster to do its duty attracted,ois sidereblo attention some yeere ago, tut it is enliose Japan that pesrl oyster farming is carried on for, °the mercial purer sea. Women dive s are employed 'el is work of trate plantieg and raising the oysters, as lbaa found that tLoy can remain under water longer tdlo men. The oyster, ef er the operation, is returee ,be the see, sod lel for from four to live years, but "cults re peer 's"thus formed are usually delectsse'osg Call cbilla frees Squ'rrele—Furriers have for 'cot time taxed their ingenuity to produce a substitute,a, the eautif al chinchilla pair, hich has become se sr-ced and consequently so costly, that it a ill soon be be) tie per eof even a millionaire. Ei cry year it bennro s e., and is enjoyed only by the few. This seesjted di nct novelty, with chasers quite its ower has rest! te et from the preparing and dyeing of squirrel to iOl t be chinchilla. Clever as the imitation is, it Inrs ore admitted thet the silvery effects ef the reel 'ties, altogether lacking. Nevertheless the scarves, 5"-Dle and muffs of dyed squirrel are quite attractive. es. process of dyeing, however, is both difficult and esePtbe sive, as only the very best skins can be used, wit" eh, result that sets are high in price. They will, o°ll6 be very much worn by those who cln afford thetas tbe The Wide Skirt.—A designer, well 'moan °diva world of fashion as an autocrat and leader, and °renter witi the responsibility of having introduced the n'oe'S shirt crude and vivid colouring of this sens„bell fashionable dresses, has decreed that his patrons 'Ws wear wide skirts, and is accordingly making then' that customers will doubtless obey his mandate,. bn„tst at will not make the skirts popular. The fact Is t" ere the beginning of every season varieties of styles, eo introduced, of which one or two, fur reasons ttn' sod one can satisfactorily explain, become the vogue', eo by that time the autocrat has many other desigril to hand, and the wide skirts will probably be relegate the background. Wel/ Plain Materials.—Many evening dresses are en tale of lace, mounted over satin, with coats in the disen,test, style of rich brocade, or moire velvet of the sn' most supple texture. Other dresses are of .1;301 materials, and these are much trimmed with erl embroideries in the loveliest shades that give tbe iso glowing touch of colour. Tulle and chiffon ate ilea largely used for long tunics and draperies, but le effects are less seen than was the case last year. ..„fide The Hatpin Danger.—Many attempts have been ',loot, to solve the problem of the unprotected hatpin, 0, it in these days of crowded tubes, trams and ornoihsseicsyS _a real and present danger. In Manchester the Irn4 earl Committee have put up a warning notice in concerning the wearing of unprotected betel with Munich, the tramway conductors are provide ot, to small corks, and it is their duty to affix Or coo' dangerously projecting points, whilst inroe°,',ltioo?' tinental cities, the police have adopted regu"„isstl° respecting the wearing of betides. Combs, esother bandeaux of fine steel, covered with velvet 811“cji the devices, have been invented to take the Place plia.,lo lethal irotrurnent, but none of these things is 0%0 ilO and effective as the hatpin, which continues to '41.090° own in spite of the perils which lurk in it. S'os ietn developments of recent years have made hat pl ØII things of beauty, which the most utilitarian it etitues is not likely t 3 supersede, but there witn te. t reason why the points should not be proseo sWig unobtrusive balls, or, it fashion so decree ;siton intricate devices of strange metals, and ren_stespose enamels, such as now render the heads of these "</p>
