      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LYIVIM.</head>
        <p>LYIVIM.</p>
        <p>, Pooß Railt.—At the Altrincham Sessions held on eunday the Bench approved of a poor rate for High ,l'eßh for the next half year of 2s 2d on buildings, and 44 Id on land.</p>
        <p>ROCKET CLCE.—The Lymm Royal Hockey Club held SUCCeSBfRI whist drive and dance at the Conservative ...""lub on Wednesday evening. There was a large number tr.esent who took part in both amusements. The prize "Inners were Mr H Mercer, and Mrs Green who tied "Fith Mrs Lsthom, but was fortunate in obtaining the Iht draw. Excellent refreshments were provided. WRIST DRIVE.—The newly formed Lymm branch of 'he National Amalgamated Union of Shop Assistants, Warehouse Men and Clerks is in a flourishing condition 11:1d held a successful whist drive on Wednesday even- In Hurst's Pavilion. Lady prize winners were :- 4, Miss Horsfield • 2, Miss Evans ; 3, Miss Hearsey ; Ivhilst. the fortunate gentlemen were :-I,Mr Piercey ; 'I, Mr Brett ; 3, Spencer.</p>
        <p>ST. MARY'S MISSION.—The thankseiving services in c,Ounection with the St Mary's Newfield Mission were on Sunday night last. The Rector, the Rev G B khurston, M.A., preached an excellent sermon from the Parable of the barren fig tree, Luke xiii., 6-9, to a Crowded audience. The room which has just been ltlovatscl, looked quite charming with the additional geoorations of numerous gifts of flowers, fruit, and getables, which a willing band of workers had die- -I)`43'ed to the best advantage. A collection for the lission funds was taken up. Miss F Hatton presided the organ.</p>
        <p>v, WESLEY GUILD.—The winter session of the Lymm nresiey Guild was commenced on Monday night when a teries of progressive games was played and thoroughly Zioyed under the presidency of Mr Hudson. Mr H S t,r,own was the winner of the gentlemen's prize, and Vas ;Jessie Pearson, the ladies. Vocal solos given by ss Jones. Mr E Appleton and Mr A B Price. con- gibuted to the enjoyment of the evening. Mrs F town and Mr H S Brown acted as accompanist. Light ,efreshments were partaken of. The fruit and vege- 'tbles, with the exception of the grapes (which were fitit to sick members) left from the Harvest Festival ervices, were disposed of by sale, Mr Hudson being ""s auctioneer ; Li Os Gd was received from this source.</p>
        <p>n,WESLE VAN HARVEST FESTIVAL.—The Wesleyan elurch harvest festival services were celebrated on Vinday morning and evening. The preacher was the W A Beckett, superintendent minister of the :arrington circuit, who delivered seasonable sermons both services. The singing throughout the day was a hearty description, in the morning the children of 'elle Primary School singing their harvest hymn " The ekle and vales are thick with corn," and the choir re the anthem " I will magnify thee "(A. Hallam elPson) the solo being taken by Miss Maggie stanley. The decorations of the interior of the nrch were as pretty as ever, the pulpit ornaments- ,l,l,l3na and arches over the aisles being special features. decorators were :—Mesdames F Brown, Finch, A H I,indson, T Newton, Smith ; the Misses Egerton, turness, Goodier, Hazlehurst, Jones, Maim, Pawlyn, i'°garson, Waterworth, Winstanley, Whitfield, and ,lessrs Brown, A Hiliudson, T Newton and AB Price. vaertories for the circuit and local funds were ollected. Mr H S Brown played the organ in his ftl)able style.</p>
        <p>i CONGRE G ATIONAL UNION MEETING.—A special service 'll connection with the Congregational Union, which it'iaa been sitting in Manchester during this week, was eld in the Lymm Congregational Church on Wednes- '43, evening. Previous to the commencement of the 4aryice Mr A Leather played several organ voluntaries. preacher was the Rev F A Russell of Southport, nno chose as his text " For I am not ashamed of the Z?spel of Christ, for it is the power of God unto "4. iVation to every one that believeth, to the Jew first also to the Greek." He said that as Paul t'4tified his existence and loyalty by this .aftt so must the members of the church to-day 17..iltify their existence. by enthusiasm for Christ. ''" Proceeded to give a brief account of the low I,l;:ite of affairs in England prior to the Evangelical evival, the brutal, abused, and hardly recognisable `°Pclition of the people, the poor statesmanship, and "it of the country : but when this revival came -ugh such men as the Wesleys and Whitfield,England ,;.ftsme filled with a new and generating life. Men and Omen were lifted to a higher plane ; conditions of r, especially in regard to that of women and i:oldren, were remedied ; a new and better type of Otesmen was made ; the obligation of England )wards the other nations of the world was laid th„ta and it was then, and not until that England sent out her missionaries. e,asking of that class of people who did not believe in ,ufistianity, but who still bad their ideals of perfect conditions, he claimed that the churches not made Christians but citizens. Three things were weaasary in our lives :I,"A Theory of Lineage" he spoke the advantage gained from being of good parentage ht"cl gave as an example the respect which is corded Englishmen in all the civilized parts of the t orld. The churches, he said, proclaimed a high 111 :',elittage, for all men are from the living God. Our Vvllege is, one humanity in Jesus Christ. 2, "A ~..aory of Human Responsibility. He declared that 6!P.onsibility for our human brethren was taught by ; to'atuft and that we should exist " Each for all and all ta:,each.'"fhe danger of living for self was a very one and he desired that a better sympathy should ctlll,t amongst mankind, and gave an example from r13,1e. 3, "A Feeling of Irresistibility." He then fo'selt on the necessity of overcoming all difficulties, out of struggle came conquest. He believed the i I at,ne sa Paul " For I have learned n whatsoever state g 'a) therewith to be content." The church, he said Sv o the power to achieve this glory of cozquest. I,,,‘"nrruAsY.—Last week-end saw the death of two 14all-known Lymm inhabitants. Mrs Collins, wife of E: ,81'dney George Collins, licensee ,of the Spread %it, Hotel, passed away on Sunday night after , pain- illness. Mrs Collins had a long and serious illness llm,' Year but bad improved during the summer only to fatal relapse a few weeks ago. The funeral, was largely attended, took place on Wednesday 14. e Parish Church. `kllnta, Warburton, widow of the late Mr .3 Warburton ' Cr,; Rept the old established grocer's shop near the y4a, died on Monday morning. Mrs Warburton who 11et.,,:75 years of age died of heart failure. The funeral let tit,oB was conducted by the Rev G B Thurston, M.A., /0431. a Parish Church on Thursday. The chief mourners %I. e: Mr Frank Warburton (son), Mr H Warburton ' Po°_tber-in-iacy), Mr Percy Houghton (grandson), Mr , it..rest and Mr Hesketh (nephews). Mr R H Gotobed 4 the undertaker. LYMM SESSIONS. 'tstuy.—Before Messrs A Watkin (in the chair), T -ay, L Holden, and A W Callender. r REINS.—John Ellis, coal dealer, South in; was summoned for haying been in charge and lurry and riding without reins to guide 1 Statham-road on October Bth. It was stated osecution that the defendant was sitting on of the lurry and jumped off when he saw the ling. Mr T S Steel who defended, submitted fendant did not ride on the lurry at all. He</p>
