      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>MAL</head>
        <p>MAL</p>
        <p>TED4,MIII(</p>
        <p>along with his partner had been delivering coal. They were returning, and the defendant was walking along- side the horse when he noticed that a rug was falling on to the horse's legs. To prevent the horse being startled, he jumped on the lurry and put the rug right, and jumped off again as soon as he had done so.—Mr Steel's statement was borne out by several witnesses.— A tine of 2s 6d including costs was imposed. CART LEFT UNATTENDED.—CharIes Bison, Rush Green, Lymm, was summoned for being the driver of a pony and cart and not having control over the same in Lymm on October 10th.—It was stated that the defend- ant bad left the conveyance and was in a billiard room for ten minutes.—A fine of 5s including costs was imposed. MOTORIST WITHOUT LICENCE.—Andrew Bertram Broomhall, motor engineer, Manchester Road, Cheadle, was summoned for driving a motor cycle and side car in Penny Hill, Lymm on September 17th and failing to produce his licence.—Sergeant Washington said the car was travelling at a very fast pace. Defendant was driving with one hand and when asked for his licencehe said be had not one in his possession. He also said that the vehicle did not belong to him, but to his brother.— The defendant was fined 10s. and costs—(l9s. 6d. in all.)</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON POACHER FINED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>THOUGHT LAND BELONGED TO EVERYBODY</head>
        <p>THOUGHT LAND BELONGED TO EVERYBODY</p>
        <p>_ John Sloane, tan-yard labourer, 23, Crossley-street, Warrington was summoned for committing a trespass by entering and being, in the day time, on land in the occupation of Frederick Leah and Thomas Cook at Thelwall on the 17th August, in search of game.—Mr W H 3 Jenkins prosecuted and said that for a consider- able period the tenants and keepers on the laud had been pestered with poachers. A closer watch had been kept lately and on the day in question the defendant was seen by John Austin, the prosecutor, and the under- keeper, Reece. There were two men carrying guns and they also had a dog with them. They were beating the field. Austen saw the defendant fire a gun but he could not say whet he aimed at. They still kept watch and the defendant, along with the other man, left and went to the land in the occupation of Thomas Cook, and there, they continued their pursuit of game.—The statement of Mr Jenkins was supported by the evidence of Austin and Leah.—The defendant when asked what he had to say remarked that there was no sign board warning trespassers. Mr Jenkins : If that is all he has to say, well, I leave it to the bench.</p>
        <p>Defendant : I was given permission to shoot on the land last year by Mr Leah. We only shot at a martin and did it for sport. About 5 years ago there was no keeper and we thought we could shoot there regularly. The Chairman (to Mr Austin) : Is that so? Mr Austin: It is not.</p>
        <p>_ Defendant, who would not be sworn, further stated that they did not get Is. worth of stuff off the land. There was nothing to shoot. The Chairman : That has nothing to do with the case._ _</p>
        <p>Defendant: I did not know the land was preserved. The Chairman : Is it your land 7—No. The Chairman : Had you any right to be there ? Yes sir, because I have had the permission of Mr Leah. Mr Jenkins : Did you ever threaten to shoot ?</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Oh, no. Mr Jenkins : All right. Defendant: We have been shooting down there for a bit and I have never been cautioned before.</p>
        <p>Mr Jenkins : He evidently thinks it is his land. The Chairman : He is evidently one of those persons who think land belongs to everybody. Mr Jenkins : Yes. Defendant : If it has no notice board up. The Chairman: You will get into serious trouble if you think that. Defendant, on being fined 10s and costs (£2 5s 6d in all) said be had finished taking out a gun licence because he could not shot anywhere. Another case in which Peter and Norman Hodgkin. son. 97, Wellfield-street, Warrington, were summoned for a similar offence on September 3rd on land in the occupation of William Scragg at Thelwall, was adjourned, the defendants failing to appear. The magistrates sanctioned an application for a warrant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>MOORE. WESLEYAN CHURCH CENTENARY CELEBRATIONS.</head>
        <p>MOORE. WESLEYAN CHURCH CENTENARY CELEBRATIONS.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyans at Moore celebrated their centenary on Saturday and Sunday last and the services are to be continued to-morrow when the Rev E H Jackson, of Grange, will preach. A special service on Saturday afternoon was attended by a large congregation when the Rev T Galland Hartley (chairman of the Liverpool District) preached a very helpful sermon from 2 Timothy ii., 19. At the close of the service a beautiful stained glass window was unveilved by Mr J Jackson. The window has been erected by himself and his wife in memory of his father and mother. The Rev J Gunnell, who knew Mr Jackson's parents personally,spoke of their devotedness to the Church at Mo ore. The gift was received by the Rev NV A Beckett on behalf the trustees of the Church. The subject of the window is the Ascension. The central figure is that of Christ as He is ascending to Heaven surrounded by angels ; underneath are the eleven disciples. The window contains 21 figures in all A public meeting was also held in the church in the evening, and in the absence of Mr Guest was very ably presided over by Mr T Dutton. The following speakers took part : The Revs T Galland Hartley, E H Jackson, W A Beckett, .1 Gunnell, and H Williamson, Alderman Bennett, Mr W Silcock,and Mr J Lawton. Mr Lawton in making his statement, said the church had been decorated throughout, the old windows had been replaced with new stained glass,and a new and improved system of ventilation had been fitted, besides several other minor improvements, the cost being estimated at about £7O. Mr Lawton on behalf of the officers of the church expressed their warm and hearty thanks for the way in which all their kind friends had so liberally responded to their appeal,and he thought with the day's collections they would have £5O towards the amount, and by the time all the services were concluded they would be quite out of debt. (Applause). The Rev W A Beckett said he brought with him the congratulations of the Warrington Circuit upon the work that was being done at Moore and wished them God-speed and still further success. He did not know those whom they were honouring that day, but he had intimate knowledge of those who were carrying on the work at the present time. He believed their great Methodist Church was rousing itself to take hold of God, and that what every church wanted was more and increasing prayer. (Applause). The Rev T Galland Hartley said he brought with him congratulations from the Liverpool district, because the Warrington Circuit held a high position in the thoughts of the Liverpool district. A Methodist cause which had been established a century was worthy of the support of all Methodists. Mr Hartley said their atten tion must be drawn to the future as well as the past and he hoped that the young people at Moore would see to it that the second century was begun with as much vigour as the old one which had been completed- (Applause). PRESENTATION TO MISS BESSIE DUTTON. Mr. Lawton said the Church had a great history, and it was with great pleasure that they looked back upon the noble men and women who had carried on the work in the past. He also mentioned many who were earnest workers at the present time, the most prominent among them being Miss Dutton, who was a class leader, leader of the mothers' meeting, and a Sunday school worker. She had recently completed 50 years as a Sunday school worker' and he had great pleasure in asking Mrs. Cartwright to present Miss Dutton with a silver lamp.—Mrs. Cartwright made the presentation, expressing herself in very suitable words.--Miss Dutton replied, and said she was delighted to think she had such a warm place in the hearts of the friends at Moore, and that it vas one of the pleasures of her life to take part in the work of the church and school. The follow- ing inscription was engraved on the lamp: "Presented to Miss Bessie Dutton as a token of love and apprecia- tion from friends at Moore, to mark the completion of 50 years as a Sunday School worker." The Rev, J. Gunnell said the movement of their 1 Church had been steadily forward from its establish- ment up to the present day, lie also drew some interesting comparisons between membership and finances 50 years ago and now. Alderman Bennett said the church at Moore was founded in the same year that Warrington was made a separate circuit. The circuit at that time consisted of only three chapels, viz. : Warrington, Runcorn and Lymm. He was proud of Methodism and very hopeful for its future. Mr W Silcock and Bev E H Jackson also spoke very pleasingly of the happy times they had had at Moore in years past. A vote of thanks to all the helpers was proposed by Mr Evans and seconded by Mr Warburton. The services on Sunday were conducted by the Rev.</p>
        <p>H. Middlewood. The collections on Saturday and Sunday amounted to £2l 2s. Bd. WESLEY GUILD.—The opening devotional meeting of the winter session was held on Wednesday last when an address entitled "The retrospect aspect, and prospect of the Church "was given by the Rev. E. H. Jackson whose remarks proved most interesting and edifying. At the close of the meeting the new syllabus was dis- tributed and members enrolled for the coming session. HATTON.</p>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVAL.—The harvest festival services in connection with St. Luke's Mission, Hatton, were held on Tuesday evening last week, the preacher being the Rev LJ T Darwall, vicar of Walton. On Sunday last, in the afternoon, the preacher was the Rev T 'l' Lee Jones. The sacred edifice was most tastefully decorated with fruit and flowers, and the services were well attended. Several anthems were sung, the solo on both occasions being rendered by Miss Elsie Bannister.</p>
        <p>PADGATE. WESLEY GUILD.—The usual weekly meeting was held on Monday evening, when the members attended to take part in a debate on "Should capital punishment be abolished." Owing to the indisposition of one of the leaders, Mr J H Mason, the debate was postponed and a very able and interesting lecture on "Robert Hall " was given by Rev E Morgan. Mr W F Newton presided and a vote of thanks was accorded the lecturer on the proposition of Mr W G Protitt, seconded by Mr Wood.</p>
        <p>rStOCKTON HEA.M.</p>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVAL.—The harvest festival services were held at Hill Cliffe Baptist Churcb, Stockton Heath on Sunday. The special preacher was the Rev. Tinsley Such of Blackley Chapel (Huddersfiild District) who gave appropriate sermons to large congregations at morning and evening services and gave the children a special address in the afternoon. The morning subject was based on Matthew ix., 37-38 ; the evening on Galations 0., 7, and the children's address was on " Potatoes and what they teach us." The church had been lavishly decorated with flowers, fruit and vegetables. The choir gave special hymns and anthems. Mrs H Moore and Miss Ada Grounds sang a duet in the afternoon, and Madame Lily ti'airhurst was the soloist M. the evening service. Mr Brocklehurst officiated at the organ in the morning, Miss Ada Grounds at the afternoon and evening services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
