      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held in the Hall Cafe on Tuesday evening,lMr WMc Intyre presiding. There were also present :—Messrs F Pearson, J Whitley, J Hallows, C W Hallows, J R Wood, F Warburton, J McDermid, G F Ashton (clerk), S Wright (architect), J Farrington (inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>TWENTY_ STEPS_ BRIDGE DANGER.</head>
        <p>TWENTY_ STEPS_ BRIDGE DANGER.</p>
        <p>The question of --tl3-0-13.CC-41Cis-C-t --Twenty Steps Bridge was again before the committee, the following letter having been received by the clerk from Mr F A Eyre, secretary of the Ship Canal C. dated September 18th:—"With further reference to my letter of the 22nd July I send you herewith copy of a letter addressed to the Company's Advertising Agent by the Secretary of the Warrington and District Bil!posting and Advertis- ing Company, Ltd., to whom the hoardings belong. It would appear that the case may be met by the provision either by the Motor Union or by the District Council, of signs as suggested in the letter." The letter from the secretary of the Billposting Com- pany was as follows :—"September 17th. At a meeting of my directors held yesterday, I was instructed to inform you that this company do not wish to remove the two hoardings at the Stockton Heath Swing Bridge and they wish to point out that even if the hoardings were removed it would not do away with the danger of an accident taking place, although no accident has to their knowledge taken place. At present an avenue of trees planted by the District Council hides both the hoard- ings and the bridge and danger signals. The company has been in communication with the Automobile Association and the Motor Union, and they have written to say that when next an official of the associa- tion is in the district an inspection will be made of the place, and if thought necessary they will have much pleasure in providing the signs which are suggested, these being of a much more prominent character than the ordinary small danger signals at present in use." The LEW{ said that he replied to the Secretary of the Ship Canal Co., saving that he would lay the letters before the Parochial Committee. He was sorry to hear nothing was being done with the hoardings to remove the danger. From his own knowledge the place was a most dangerous one and he felt sure a most serious accident would occur there on some occasion. If one did occur it would be the duty of that committee to point out that they had drawn the attention of the Billposting Co. to the hoardings. Mr McDermid : The trees are an obstruction to the view, but that is no reason why the Billposting Co. should shelter themselves behind that fact. The obstruction of the trees could easily be remedied. The Chairman said if the company gave an under- taking to take down the hoarding they were prepared to undertake that the trees should not obstruct the view. Mr McDermid : Are we completely at a standstill ? The Clerk said he did not know whst power they had to compel the company to remove the hoardings. The bye-laws did not cover the point desired by that com- mittee. _ _</p>
        <p>Mt McDermid moved that the Council protest to the County Council that the present bye-laws adopted under the Advertisement Regulations Act were inadequate to deal with the existing circumstances. The Chairman : I don't think they appreciate the difficulty from our point of view. Mr Whitley seconded the proposition, which was carried. NEW FIRE ALARM. A letter was read by the Clerk from the Clerk to the County Council stating that the new Police Station Sub-Committee was prepared to recommend the Stand- ing Joint Committee to grant permission to the Parish Council to fix a tire alarm bell in a certain position in the yard at the rear of the new Police Station, provid- ing the Parish Council entered into an agreement, to be prepared by himself, securing to the Standing Joint Committee the payment of a nominal sum for the per- mission granted, and on condition that all expenses that might be incurred in connection with the erection and upkeep of the bell, as the Chief Constable might consider necessary, were paid by the Parish Council,and subject to the further condition that the existing fire- but be removed to a position at the rear of the Police Station site. The Chief Constable desired him to say that the police would accept no responsibility with regard to the bell or the hanging of it. It was decided that the suggested arrangement be approved. BILL CLIFFE BAPTIST CHURCH. SUCCESSFUL SALE OF WORK. With the object of augmenting the church funds, a sale of work was held at Bill Chile on Thursday, in connection with the Hill Chile Baptist Church. The room presented a very inviting appearance, the various stalls being set Out in attractive style, There was a large attendance at the opening ceremony which was performed by Mrs H Roberts. The Chairman was Mr J W Scholrfield of Liverpool. The stallholders were as follows : Refreshment stall.—Mrs Brocklehurst (Grappenhall- road), Mrs Renworthy, Mrs Brocklehurst (Whitefield- road), Mrs T Williams and Miss Simpson. Work stall.—Miss Booth, Mrs H Dodd, Mrs J Moss, Mrs Ansdell and Mrs Capell. Sunday School stall.—Miss Ada Grounds,Miss Speak- man, Mrs Wild, Mrs II Moore. Dairy stall.—Mrs B Bradford, Mrs Billington, Mrs 3 Bradford and Miss Jackson. Flower stall.—Mrs T Moss, Miss Moss, Miss Sher- wood, Mrs A L Kenworthy, Mr G Moss, Mr W Wild and Mr J Moss. Men's stall.—Messrs Brocklehurst, T Grounds, H Grounds, 8 Brocklehurst (Whitefleld-road), P Fletcher, H Moore, and others. The tea room was under the supervision of Miss Grounds, Miss Dodd, Mesdames J Grounds, Moore, J Bradford, C Kenworthy, Webster, Drewitt, Ault, Robinson, Parker, and Miss Harding. After the usual preliminaries, the Rev G Curtis stated the object of the sale. That, he said, was a growing district, and their needs were correspondingly growing. They wanted to advance with the times, as far as accommodation and extension in church work were concerned. They wanted more funds to meet the incidental expenses and to carry out alterations at some future date. £33 15s 21jd had already been raised by the sale of a booklet and other means and be did not think they would have much difficulty in increasing that to £lOO by that sale. (Applause). The Chairman said be had an interest in Hill Cliffe inasmuch as he was one of the trustees of that building After dealing with the historical associations of the church, he said their predecessors bad bad diflicultiee and had overcome them, and what they had done in their day the pressnt congregation would do now—(hear, hear)—With a better heart and be thankful that the difficulties they had to face would be rapidly overcom!. They had not the same enemies to contend with as in olden days. They, at the present time, had to fight the stolid indifference of a large number of people. They did not oppose the preaching of the Gospel because they did not take any notice of it. They were not going to win .the battle by buildings, money, social influence or music. No church could really be prosperous or grow until the message of the Gospel of Christ bad taken possession of it. Men should preach because they must and not simply because they wanted to. (Applause). Mrs Roberts in declaring the sale open wished them every success in their effort. She could appreciate all the hard work that had been done in the preparation of the various articles on the stalls. No active church I could manage without money and that was why the women folk were called in to help so much. She hoped they would not only realise £lOO but that it would be £l5O. and they could do that by putting their shoulder to the wheel and working all together. (Applause.) The Rev H Ogden proposed a vote of thanks to the ' Chairman and Mrs Roberts, and spoke of Mr Schofleld's connections with the Baptist Churches of the kingdom. He supposed that the name of Roberts was associated r and linked up with every great, noble and heroic effort in the town. (Hear, heat.) One knew how the family • were ever willing to give and work for all types of</p>
        <p>noble causes. The cause they were met to support was an extremely noble one,for that great and historic work carried on there. (Applause). Mr W H Bradford (treasurer) seconded and the vote was heartily passed. Miss Dora Bradforel presented Mrs Roberts with a bouquet and Mr Curtis received a spray from the hands of Miss Lily Bradford. The gross proceeds amounted to £ll7.</p>
      </div>
