      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>GREAT SAN KEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SAN KEY.</p>
        <p>WHIST DRIVE AND DANCE.—On Friday of last week at St. Mary's Sunday School, Sankey, a whist drive and dance took place. The arrangements were excellent and a most enjoyable evening was spent. Mrs G Clarke had charge of the refreshments. The winners in the whist drive received prizes kindly given by the com- mittee. The accompanists were the Bros. Ray.—On the proceeds of this and other entertainments it is hoped to raise £l5O for the alterations and decorations of the Infani's School, Penketh. Suzh a worthy object ought to receive the generous support of the residents in this neighbourhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>PENKETH FRIENDS' SCHOOL.—On Monday evening one of a series of Lectures was given at the Friends' School in the Lecture Hall, by Mr W A Mackie, of the Everton Camera CIA. The subject was "On Tramp in Dicken's Land," with lime-light views, and the lecture was very much enjoyed by a good company. Mr W E Brown introduced the lecturer to the audience, and the best thanks of the meeting was expressed by two students of the school.</p>
        <p>PEICKETII LIBERAL CLUB.—The annual meeting of the above club as held on Thursday evening, Mr R Routledge presiding. The winter session's work was discussed, and certain interesting items decided upon. The officials re-elected were the following :—Mr Routledge, president ; Mr El Davies, secretary ; Mr W Dwerryhouse, treasurer. The following were elected for the Committee :—Messrs R Acomb, R Darlington, F Dendy, J J Foden, E Houghton, W Lightfoot, H Molyneaux and W Scott.</p>
        <p>LABOUR MBETING.—The Labour party held a success- ful meeting on Thursday evening, Mr G Faulkner pre- siding. A hearty reception was accorded to Mr J A Seddon, candidate for the division, who, in criticising the Government's foreign policy, contended that it ought to be taken out of ~the bands of one individual and defined in the open House. Such procedure would be more in accordance with the spirit of true demooracy He also pleaded for a limitation of armaments and in this connection referred to hi, mission to America, during which he was charged to convey an expression of the goodwill of the workers of England to the American people. Mr Seddon also strongly supported Home Rule and said the religious difficulty only applied to a small section of Ulster.—The Rev W Cooper referred to the Labour programme, the reduction of armaments and the attitude of the party towards the white slave traffic. He appealed for support for Mr Seddon at the next election. PENKETH PARISH COUNCIL. A meeting of the above Council was held on Monday evening in the Council School. Present:—Dr Murray (chairman), Mr J G Withinshaw (vice chairman), Miss Tinsley, Messrs William Davies, J Ditchfleld, R Rout. ledge and E Foster (clerk). FIDDLER'S FERRY FOOTPATH. This matter again came before the Council, the Clerk having received the following letter from Mr Woodward (who was unavoidably absent). "I would like you to draw the attention of the Penketh Parish Coun- cil to the fact that for the first time in thirty yenrs I was debarred from going along the river at Fiddlers Ferry on August 18th 1912 a gate having been put there and locked' The Council should take immediate steps to have it removed."</p>
        <p>Mr Woodward sent a further letter with a suggested resolution asking the Council to order the removal of the fence within fourteen days. Mr Davies thought the Council should call a special meeting of parishioners before passing the resolution. The Chairman agreed, and explained that if they as a Parish Council were to go down and remove the fence they must be prepared if the landlord claimed damage for trespass. Mr Ditchfield said that the landlord would prosecute the first man who touched the gate, and make a case of it.</p>
        <p>It was proposed by Mr Davies and agreed that the resolution be referred back until Mr Woodward could be present. ItESIGENT LEAVES FOE SOUTH AFRICA.—To-day (Saturday), Mr. Frank Sampson of Station-road, Penketb, sails from Southampton for South Africa by the S.S. Edinburgh Castle. Mr. Sampson having lived in the village all his life is well-known and respected. He is a member of the Liberal Club. Mr. Sampson has been in the employ of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company Limited, Warrington for a number of years at Bewsey Offices, and on Saturday last Mr. Trustlove on behrlf of the office staff presented him with a gold watch (suitably inscribed).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>TRINITY HOUSE AND ITS WORK.</head>
        <p>TRINITY HOUSE AND ITS WORK.</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>Thoro probably no institution whose his. tory mirrors more faithfully the development of England's commerce, and importance as 5 maritime Power, than that comparatively HUI; known Government department described as rrinity House. Its history dates practically from the first dawning of England's sea power, when in the reign of King Henry VIII. it re ceived its charter of. incorporation, although there are several accounts, mostly of a tradi. tional nature, of its existence and duties prior te that date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>RELIGIOUS BROTHERHOOD.</head>
        <p>A RELIGIOUS BROTHERHOOD.</p>
        <p>It is, however, quite certain that, like most of our ancient benevolent institutions, the Trinity House was originally a religious organi. sation, as is indeed suggested by the style of " Brethren" still used in describing its chief officials. It was situated first at Deptford, when the, Brethren had "a Maneon Howse and SOME almshouses" near to the Church of St Nicholas, and it probable enough that the be. gintrings of our present coast-lighting system are to be found in the charitable lighting bf beacon fires for sailors' guidance, upon the tower of seaside churches, a kindly duty which was one of the multifarious tasks then performed by parish priests or dwellers in monastic institutions.</p>
        <p>However that may be, it was early in King ITenry VHl.'s reign that the Charter was granted to the Mariners of England, under the name of the" Master, Wardens, and Assistants of the Guild, Fraternity or Brotherhood of the Most Glorious and Undivided Trinity and of St. Clement in the Parish of Deptford in the County of Kent." This same Brotherhood it Li which to-day has its headquarters in Wyatt's building overlooking Tower Hill, and which still has the exclusive charge of many of the duties which it performed on its first ineorporar tiom</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ITS GROWTH.</head>
        <p>ITS GROWTH.</p>
        <p>Tho most important duties of the Trinity house In modern times are, of course, the con- trol of the coast-lighting system of the kingdom, and the charge of the piloting arrangements, :16 well as of the buoying of harbours and of dan- gerous shoals; and when one reflects upon the tremendous tide of shipping which daily eet4 shoreward and seaward along our coasts, it is. 'wonderful to think what a mighty organisation has taken the place of, or rather has gradually grown out of, that little religious brotherhood, and later ,association of mariners at Deptford Strond in the county of Kent. Although a statute was passed in 1566 em- powering the Trinity Brethren to "make, erect, an(i set up beacons, marks and signs for the sea," it was not until 1836 that the lighthouses o Etrigland passed entirely under the control of the Trinity House. Before that date many lighthouses were held by private individuals, by patent acquired from the Crown, and, on payment of a rent, fira holders had the right to collect the tolls, drid although private indivi- duals in some instances did noble and enduring work on behalf of the sailors—such men, for example, as Winstanley, the builder of the first Eddystone lighthouse—it stands to reason that the system was not a workable one where so vast an amount of shipping had to be regulated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>COAST LIGHTING: NOW AND THEN.</head>
        <p>COAST LIGHTING: NOW AND THEN.</p>
        <p>In the oldest lighthouses the illuminants used were wood, pitch, and later, coal, and .in Smea'ton's Eddystone lighthouse, so late as 1810, candles were used, which were after that date, when the lighthouse came under the jurisdiction of Trinity House, superseded by oil lamps. Now, of course, the whale and sperm oil have in their turn given way to those magnificent glares of gas or electric light, which make so imposing a spectacle on ea dark night at sea. As well as a distinguished engineer-in-chief who superintends the designing and erection of the lighthouses, the Elder Brethren retain the services of a highly-qualified scientific adviser to assist in the piestion of coast fog-signals and the lights on lightships and lighthouses, and in this capacity have acted successively Professor CaradaY, Professor Tyndall and Lord Rayleigh; and the first-named has left it on record that na work delighted him more than that which he did in this connection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ANGIER*t4uLSiON</head>
        <p>ANGIER*t4uLSiON</p>
        <p>Anr,ier's Emulsion has been prescribed by the medical profession and used in the hospitals for over twenty years, and is now universally recognised as a standard approved remedy for coughs, bronchitis, asthma, consumption and for all catarrhal affections of the respiratory or digestive organs. Angier's Emulsion is soothing and healing to throat, lungs, stomach and intestines, and it has a most invigorating, tonic influence upon the general health. It is pleasant to take and agrees perfectly with delicate stomachs. Of all Chemists, 1/li, 2/9 and 4/6.</p>
        <p>"A Real Boon in Chest Complaints."</p>
        <p>45, Aldridge Villas, Westbourne Park, W. Dear Sirs.—As a nurse I have had much experience with Angler's Emulsion, and can Speak of it in the very highest terms. I frequently recommend it for coughs and lung affections, and it always gives satisfaction. Its effect in relieving bronchial attacks is simply wonderful. and it also greatly assists nutrition. I think Angier's Emulsion is a real boon to all sufferers from Chest complaints. (Signed) (Nurse) A. CLAVERING Free Sample Name Coupon. Address E.X. Fill in coupon and send ANGIER CHEMICAL. CO., Ltd., se 11!111111111111I1</p>
        <p>with 3d. for postage to the lerkanwell Road, London, E.C. 1 -Er</p>
        <p>r i 11101111 1111 i 1 I II IW - _ CV.4I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ORGANISATION.</head>
        <p>ORGANISATION.</p>
        <p>The coast is divided into districts, each in charge of a Trinity House official, whose duties are to superintend the relief of the lightship crews, and of the lighthouse keepers, to supply them with provisions, to remove dangerous wrecks, and to look after the efficiency vf the countless buoys which mark the perilous shoals along our coasts. And when it is reflected how terrible would be the consequences of a neglect of any one of these arduous duties, it will te seen that, down to the least of its ser- vants, the Trinity House requires vigilant and unrelaxing service. It would not be out of place to mentio-A here the sources of the revenues for coast-lighting purposes. They are derived from dues paid by ships entering and leaving our ports, which are paid into a " General Lighthouse Fund," under the Board of Trade, through which department it is ;dis- bursed to the lighthouse authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ITS MANY DUTIES.</head>
        <p>ITS MANY DUTIES.</p>
        <p>Not the least important of the Trinity House departments is that which deals with the pilotage system. Trinity House to this Hay supplies a pilot for every outgoing vessel, and, consequently, receiving information of every vessel leaving or entering our ports, must have been on many occasions enabled to supply in- formation of the highest national import. Over 700 pilots are under the direction of the Brethren. The lighthouse dues being now given over to the Board of Trade, the finances of the Trinity House are not so large as they were of old; but there still remain a number of charities which are administered by the Brethren, the incomes of which are derived from legacies. From the earliest days the Trinity House had, as we have seen, control of almshouses; and they now possess a consider- able number of these, devoted to the i*eds of decayed master-mariners, and the widows of mariners, while they also distribute alms and pensions to a very large number, both of men and women. in all parts of the British Isles. . .</p>
        <p>All thing's considered, then, it is right and fitting that a body which, as its annals show, has been of the greatest assistance in time of national peril, as on the occasion of the Nore Mutiny, and under the shadow o.f threatened invasion, which plays now so impor- tant a part in the routine of our commerce and navigation, should number among its aonorary Elder Brethren the most distinguished of our Princes and statesmen; while amongst its active Brethren are tried and true officers of the Boyal and Mercantile Marine. And from this great organisation, which first came into being among the mariners of Deptford Strond, the spirit of adventure and romance has not yet passed away; nor will, surely, so long as "From reef and rock and skerry—over head- land, r.seis and voe, Fhe Coastwise Lights of England watch the ships of England go!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>DEATH OF TWO EX-COUNCILLORS.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF TWO EX-COUNCILLORS.</p>
        <p>MR. D. S. DUNCAN.</p>
        <p>A townsman who in his day rendered conscientious and valuable services to the municipality and to the cause of Liberalism, passed away on Sunday in the person of Mr David Sedgxvick Duncan, of 41, Padgate- lane, for 12 years a representative of the Fairfield Ward on the Town Council. Mr Duncan, who was 71 years of age, had been in failing health for some con- siderable time. A native of Yorkshire, he was a member of a family who claimed to be direct descendants of King Duncan, of Scotland. A claim to certain estates in Scotland was made by a brother of the deceased gentleman but failed, owing, it is stated, to the fact that an important document was missing. Mr Duncan had forty years connection with the railway service and prior to taking up his residence in Warrington he was engaged on the Manchester, Sheffield and Lincolnshire Railway at ' Gorton. On his departure from Gorton, on March 20th. 1875, he was presented by his colleagues with a marble timepiece as a mark of their esteem. At Warrington he occupied the position of manager of the Cheshire Lines Committee works in Battersby-lane. Retiring in 1892, another token of the respect and goodwill of the railway employes was presented to him. Mr. Duncan entered the Town Council, in 1895 when, as a Liberal candidate, he defeated Mr. R. T. Fairclough by 37 votes. His excellent know- ledge of engineering rendered hfm a most useful acquisition to the Council and his advice always carried great weight. Several Committees had the benefit of his services, including the Sanitary Works, Gas, Fire Engine, Cemetery, and School Attendance Committees. He occupied the positions of deputy-chairman of the Gas and the Cemetery Committees. Mr Duncan held his seat on the Council for four terms, but failing health was the cause of his retirement in 1907. At that time he had a reasonable prospect of being elected to the next aldermanic chair.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon. The last rites were per- formed by the Rev A J Bratt. The chief mourners were Mrs Duncan (niece), Mr and Mrs G Duncan, Mr John Duncan (sen), Mr and Mrs Richard Alliday, Mr T Alliday, Mr J Alliday, Miss H Day, Mr D Day, Mr C Day, Mr Todd, Mr Dale, Councillor Craik, Coun- cillor Wilkinson, Messrs W S Haddock, W H Woodcock and Mr R Young.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>MR. THOMAS J. HULSE.</head>
        <p>MR. THOMAS J. HULSE.</p>
        <p>News was received with regret in Warrington of the death of Mr. Thomas Joseph Hulse, another ex-member of the Council, which took place at Abram on Monday. Mr. Hulse, who was in his 57th year, was, up to a few years :ago, one of the most familiar figures in Warrington, and his general disposition and kindly manner made him a general fa,vourite. For 25 years he was in the service of Messrs. Rylands Bros. and, prior to his retirement about six years ago, he occupied the position of secretary. In 1900 he was elected to the Council without a contest as the representative of Fairfield Ward. In 1903 he was returned by a majority of 38 votes over Mr Povey. On the completion of his second term of office in 1906 Mr. Hulse retired. Being actively connected with the Dramatic Society for a number cf years, Mr Hulse gained popularity in that direction. In' The Magis- trate" he was highly successful. He was also an officer in the" St. John Ambulance Brigade and a prominent Free Mason. The deceased gentleman was a Conservative in politics and was a member of the Conservative Association and the Conservative Club. The funeral was conducted on Thursday at Abram Church, ,the Rev W Wentworth Scott officiating. Amongst those present were Mr T Leslie (who represented the firm of Rylands Bros., Ltd., Warring- ton). Wreaths were sent by the widow, "George, Mabel and Dora," Miss Hulse (sister), Mr and Mrs W Barker and family, Mr and Mrs Turner, the firm of Rylands Bros., Ltd., and Staff of Rylands, Bros., Ltd.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES. POLICE COURT SEQUEL TO ACCIDENT. At Runcorn, on Monday, Lawrence Bernard Cobb, hawker, Halton-road, was fined 40s for obstructing the highway with a cart at Sutton on August 2.lth last, causing an Lccident to Mr. A. Lomas, farmer, Sutton.</p>
        <p>SUICIDE ON RAILWAY. Mr Brighouse held an inquest at Widnes on Saturday on Walter Hague, aged thirty-seven, shipping clerk, St Helens, whose mutilated body was discovered near Farnworth Station, on the Cheshire Lines Railway. Evidence was given by a brother, who said that Walter, a married Dian, bad been to Liverpool, but had not returned home since, and it is supposed that he had been wandering about since Monday. The Coroner read a letter which was found in the deceased man's possession, in which be said good-bye to his wife and children. The jury returned a verdict of" Suicide while temporarily insane." • EARLSTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>BROTHERS DROWNED AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>BROTHERS DROWNED AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Two boys, named Frederick Hemmings, aged thirteen, a telegraph messenger, and his brother, Harold, aged nine sons of James Hemmings, of 11, High-street, Newton-le-Willows, left home on Saturday afternoon to attend St. Peter's Church Sunday School. On the way they met two other boys, and the party decided to go for a walk. They went to Earlestown and played hide-and-seek near to Bradlegh Bridge, on the St. Helens and Sankey Canal. They soon tired of this and Harold Hemmings went to the canal to wash his bands. Bending down to reach the water, he overbalanced and fell in. His cries attracted the attention of the other boys. Frederick Hemmings bravely jumped into the water and endeavoured to get his brother to the bank, but the youngster struggled fiercely, and both boys disappeared. The other boys brought assistance, but the two bodies were not recovered until nearly seven o'clock—two hours after the accident occurred. MR. J. A. SEDDON AND HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>In the course of a speech at Rainford; Mr- 3 A Seddon, Labour candidate for the Newton Division, said he had been reading the speeches of Mr Rigby Swift, and apart from politics they all joined in con- gratulating him on being honoured by being made a K.C. He had been giving plausible reasons why Rome Rule should not be granted, and he referred to the Land Act, which, he said, had been a good thing for Ireland, but was it given with good will by the Conservative party ? Had they forgotten the days of coercion? It was passed not for love, but from fear. They admitted It had been a good thing for Ireland, because it had put more and mere people on to the soil instead of driving them away, but it had not settled their aspiratione which were still there. She made this the premie demand ; whatever else you give us falls into insignific- ance unless you give us the one desire of otr heart. (Applause). He bad travelled in Ireland, and he had never fonnd any bigotry and tyranny if they got outside of Ulster. It was the very reverse, and if they got into the west, the centre and the south of Ireland, they found that Protestants were sent to represent Catholics in the House of Commons, Protestants were sent to the County Councils, the Town Councils, and elected to public offices, and he had never come across a single Protestant who had complained of the tyranny of the Roman Catholics, but on the contrary their admiration had been very high for their fellow countrymen because they had never allowed religion to interfere with their friend- ship and their social relationships. (Hear, hear.) He challenged Mr Rigby Swift to make the same statement in St. Helens, where there was a large Catholic population, that Home Rule meant Rome Rule. Referring to the question of Welsh Disestablishment, Mr Seddon said he was a Nonconformist, but he had no antipathy for the Established Church. He had to remember that Wales for forty years had demanded the disestablishment of the Church in Wales, and that demand should be recognised. He admitted the import- ance of the Home Rule Bill, of the Welsh Disestablish- meat Bill, of the Reform Bill, but to his mind there was one outstanding question, and that was the question of labour unrest in this country. He referred to the various disputes which had taken place during the past year, and urged that if the workers were to work out their own salvation they must stand solidly together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>BY ROYAL COMMAND.</head>
        <p>BY ROYAL COMMAND.</p>
        <p>An Emperor Who Has a Pine Forest in His</p>
        <p>Palace.</p>
        <p>A NOVELTY EVERY HOME CAN SHARE.</p>
        <p>The aged Emperor of Austria for a long time has suffered severe bronchial trouble.</p>
        <p>When he bad consulted the Court physicians with but little result in easing the coughing and tightness about his chest, he gave commands for the interior of the Schoenbrunn Palace to be transformed into a pine forest in miniature. _ _</p>
        <p>The South Gallery of the Palace was filled with young growing pine trees and the floors sprinkled with pine needles. Consequently when His Majesty took his daily walking exercise in this indoor pine forest, he breathed into his system the self-same odours which make the Swiss peasant in his home amongst the pine-clad mountains a stranger to those twentieth-century plagues—consumption and bronchitis. The Emperor's throat and chest have received such benefit that he was quite hale and hearty for his 82nd birthday celebrations this autumn. A pine forest in every home, for rich or poor, is. supplied by the discovery of the wonderful breathe- able tablets called Peps, which, when you put them on your tongue, emit fumes which are equal in their effect to the fumes from the Emperor's pine trees. The discovery of Peps is the result of a long-felt need for a sensible throat, chest and lung medicine. Hitherto it has been the custom to swallow cough mixtures straight into the stomach when anything was the matter with the chest, the fact being quite over- looked that there is no direct passage whatever between the stomach and the lunge. _ _ . _ .</p>
        <p>As coughs, 13,1 onchitis, laryngitis, asthma, hoarse— ness, croup, and that influenza cough, have their origin in the throat or lungs, it necessarily follows that the remedy, to be a real remedy, must be introduced actually to these parts affected. But no liquid or solid medicine can pass down the breathing passages, a little device of Nature's compelling all liquids and solids to go down quite a different tube leading to the stomach. The ideal chest medicine must take the form of air. When a little Peps tablet is taken from its silver jacket and allowed to dissolve slowly on the tongue, health-giving fumes are then given off,;and breathed down into the chest, bathing the throat and windpipe with their soothing influence as they pass. This novel Peps treatment quickly eases a cough ; phlerzm, which has obstructed the breathing, is loosened and thrown up, soreness is alleviated, and the condition known as relaxed throat is soon at an end. _ _ _</p>
        <p>That familiar tightness of the chest, and also that backing night cough, disappear under the healing influence of these Peps. Indeed, these Peps tablets are so powerful that they will put an end to chronic bronchitis after it has endured for years while for their children's chest complaints, mothers will find Peps most agreeable and effective. -</p>
        <p>Peps suit old and young equally well. Peps are entirely free from all harmful drugs. Peps give wide and far-reaching benefit, and attack lung, throat, and chest disease in quite a different manner to ordinary medicine. You can get a box from your chemist for one-and-three-halfpence, or two-and-nine. Just:as his Majesty has a Pine Forest in his Palace so Pepe mean to you" A Pine Forest in Every Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Coughs and Lung Troubles</head>
        <p>Coughs and Lung Troubles</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>LIBERAL RALLY IN CO-OPERATIVE HALL</head>
        <p>LIBERAL RALLY IN CO-OPERATIVE HALL</p>
        <p>Solid Support for the Government. SPEECHES BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD, MR. TOM WING, AND MR. P. M. OLIVER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Challenge for Mr. Harold Smith.</head>
        <p>A Challenge for Mr. Harold Smith.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Liberal Association is to be con- gratulated upon the spleadid success of the mass meeting held under its auspices in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Wednesday evening. The room was filled to overflowing and the speeches were punctuated with demonstrations of enthusiastic approval. Cheers greeted the appearance on the plat- form of Mr and Mrs A H Crosfield and a rousing welcome was also extended to the principal speakers —Mr Tom Wing, ex M.P. for Grimsby, and Mr P M Oliver, prospective candidate for the Knutsford Division, who are great favourites with Warrington audiences. Mr Crosfield presided and amongst others present were :—Mrs Osborne, Councillor 0 Toole, Messrs C E Parker, P Peacock, E Wynne Jones, W Clegg, J Shaw, P Delaney, R Osborne, P If Brown, and EA Sanders.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD ON THE BALKANS WAR. The CHAIRMAN, who was again cheered on rising to address the meeting, first touched upon the outbreak of war in the Balkans. He was not going to enter into the difficult and delicate questions of the diplomatic situation as it concerned the larger Powers, but that morning he read in the " Manchester Guardian "—a paper which he greatly valued and respected—an article which, in some respects, seemed to strike a rather uncertain note. There were one or two aspects of the conflagration in the Balkans which must appeal to Englishmen very strongly and in no uncertain way. He was sure all present would subscribe very heartily indeed to what the Chancellor of the Exchequer said last Saturday—that whatever the issue of the conflict might be—whether it was arrived at after a short, sharp struggle, as one hoped, or a after more prolonged warfare—it would extend freedom and civilised government. (Applause). They could well onderstand that, in his high position, the Chancellor did not feel at liberty to go any further, but he (Mr Crosfleld) was afraid that it was necessary to realise— and it was well that they should say it frankly—that that extension of freedom and good government was not compatible with any increase but was only to be hoped for and arrived at by</p>
        <p>arrogate to itself the whole of that great kingdom. I am more afflicted in seeing that a very minute part of that small faction should be able to persuade any person here that on the support of their power the connection of the two kingdoms essentially depends." He (Mr Crosfield) would venture to commend Alit quotation from Burke to the attention of the Member for Warrington. (Hear, hear.) A second weapon which the Opposition were esing in their attack upon the Home Rule Bill was the cry that it was sprung upon the country unsuspectingly. (Laughter). Apart from the fact that the Liberal party had been identified with Home Rule since 18SB, it was clearly stated before the last election in the speeches of Liberal leaders that Home Rule was the first chief business they would have to take in hand after the Parliament Act. (Apphuse.) The Tory leaders also affirmed that Home :tule was the great &amp;opr they would have to face if the Parliament Act were pssed. Some of his bearers would remember the</p>
        <p>POSTERS TITAT WERE DISPLAYED IN WARRINGTON about American dollars and io reign g )id. (Hear, haal and laughter.) Not only had the haders of the Tory party enlarged npon the terrible danger to which, in their view, the country would be exposed, but they had also said that the Liberal leaders had made that position perfectly clear. Therefore, if any of their Tory friendsin Warrington said that due notice had not been given of the intention to pass 11,ons Rule, they %eta accusing their own leaders of a slight inaccuracy. (Hear, hear.) Toe quesjon had been amply put before the country. (Applause.) In the past the Liberals had been faced with an accumulation of at rears of work, owing to the insuperable obstacle of the House of Lords, bnt they now had the satisfaction of knowing that their efforts were not going to be thrown away. (Loud applause.) Finally Mr Crosfleld asked his hearers to contrast the aehievements of the Liberals with the record of the Tories during their term of office from 1995 to 1906. The Tories came into power very largely on a promise of old age pensions which was never fulfilled. Then there was the reckless diplomacy—or the lack of it, altogether—which landed us into the appalling South African ear, 20,600 lives being lost and .£250,000,060 squandered unnecessarily. The war resulted in a commercial debacle so terrible that the effects were felt in South Africa to this moment. Next they attempted to cover the affair up in the raging, tearing propaganda started in 1903. Turning to the Liners! administration, they bad the splendid vindication of tree Trade the progressives came into power in 1906 by giving the Tories the biggest political heating within the memory of man. (Laugnter and applause.) Then came the glorious record established for Liberal foreign policy in t-outtz Africa, followed by all the social and political reforms in which they had been engaged—the effort to diminish what they all admitted were the too great disparities between wealth aud poverty, the granting of old age pensions, and lastly the scheme which dealt in a way which had hitherto been impossible for the vast majority of the people, with the question of sickness and infirmity. (Applause.) Ziot lees important was the by-product of work of that kind in its action on our slum population. Whether they con- sidered the Liberal record in respect of legislation for the young or for the aged, the tendency of all Liberal flnauce to deal leniently with the middle and lower classas and let great wealth and luxury contribute as it had never done before to the national revenue—look at the work from whatever side they pleased and only compare it with the rory record, and he was sure the Liberal party need have no fear for the future. (Loud applause).</p>
        <p>MR. WING AND MR. HAROLD SMITH. CHALLENGE TO DEBATE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tom WING referred to the speech by Mr. Crosfleld and said it did seem regrettable that a voice so potent and actuated by such Liberal sentiments should not be the representative of Warrington in the House of Commons. Any elector present who made a mistake at the last election and voted for the other side in order to save Warrington from bankruptcy— (laughter)—would probably have realized that it would have been a far better thing to have sent a real min to represent him rather than a dead head. (Hear, hear and laughter.) He was very doubtful whether the Smiths would go over to Ulster. There were wooden guns to be wheeled on bicycle wheels. (Laughter.) He had asked an Irishman how it was that the guns were wooden and he had said they did not want to hurt the fellows behind them. (Laughter.) The last time he was in Warrington they were discussing the Insurance Bill, which was going to cause a great deal of trouble. People were going to be hurt by receiving sick pay—(laughter)—and mothers were going to cease to be mothers because of the prospect of receiving 30s. per time. (Laughter). Then it was a Bill but now it was an Act with its marvellous results. (Applause). By the passing of that Act within four months 4,500,000 people in Friendly Societies had trebled and become practically 14,000,000. (Applause.) That gave new life and energy and brought into exist- ence new societies and a keener, healthy competition amongst the peoples engaged in a worthy ambition, namely, the security of the workers in the hour of sick- ness. (Applause). He was told that Warrington was a Tariff Reform town.—(laughter)—thet it was repre- sented by one of the most distinguished Tariff Reformers in the country. (Laughter). He wondered how the Member excused himself when he went to other towns and had to admit that Warrington never had a period of prosperity equal to the present moment—and that under the burden of Free Trade. (Laughter) Money was rushing into the pockets of the workers and it was finding its way into the homes of the masses of the people. Tariff Reform was the easiest thing to knock iuto a accited hat that 13Q Qr,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>DINI INISHING THE CONTROL OF THE TIM K</head>
        <p>DINI INISHING THE CONTROL OF THE TIM K</p>
        <p>..... over the Christian peoples. At a grave and terrible time like this, one's memory went back to the days when they heard some very significant language from their old Chief—(applause).—At a time when the tergiversation, the trickery and wiliness of the Turk were being tried on, Mr Gladstone said : " There is one little word that is understood in Constantinople— the little word must.'" (Applause). " I am bound to say " continued Mr Crosfleld " that, looking back over the history of the last two and twenty years, one does feel that it is deplorable—end I would like to use a much stronger word—that the great Powers of Europe have not joined t)gether long ago—(bear, hear)—to exercise, as the Concert of Europe, their authority, and to use that word ' must' in Constantinople. (Applause.) Mr Gladstone used to speak straight enough about the days we must look to when the Turkish influence was to be removed, bag and baggage, out of the European States. It is well to bear in mind the difficulties that have to be faced by those who who undoubtedly are working for what they see to be the best pos,ible— which we so often have to do in this world. Where you cannot go for the best absolute, you must go for the best possible. Let us make every allowance for the difficulties of those who have to face a complicated situation, hut if the time is not ripe now or in the immediate future for that "bag and baggage" policy, we must recognize very clearly that it is only by moving in that direction that you can hope to see a real and permanent amelioration of the condition of things in the Balkans. We have all read of what has happened under Turkish rulers, under the old regime and—l am truly and deeply sorry to say—under the new regime of the young Turks as well. If any of you had been in the East and had heard, as I have, the evidence of men at first hand who have been witnesses of these practices, your feeling in the matter would be stronger than ever. Mr Gladstone called the system of Government there and the influence of Turkish rule THE NEGATION OF GOD UPON EARTH.'</p>
        <p>You are dealing with people who, unfortunately, have no idea of toleration for followers of other religions— people who actually treat the whole of woman kind as having been born without a soul and who, given the chance, are capable of the most vile and obscene atrocities. It may be said 'Oh yes, but you have had reprisals from these Christian populations in the Balkans.' Yes, I have no doubt there have been some reprisals, and there is no wonder at it—(hear, hear)— but that is no excuse for the perpetuation of the subjection of these Christian peoples to this re-actionary rule. (Applause.) When I was last in Constantinople, I had a talk with a man in a very high position indeed and he said ` Well, taking it all round, and with a few very minor exceptions, we are just about GOO years behind the times here.' That is the grave situation that we are faced with in that part of the world." Coming a little nearer home, said Mr Crosfield, they had had—he was thankful to say—not warfare—(laughter)—and he thought they need not anticipate that they were, after all, on the brink of war. (Laughter.) But they had had a kind of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>MILITARY G EANT</head>
        <p>MILITARY G EANT</p>
        <p>—(laughter)—and—P-OliiiCe-W04c1.1; a sorry business indeed if there were not a humorous side to it. (Hear, hear.) He fancied it was a long time since the House of Commons had enjoyed a better laugh than it had over the picture of King Carson—(laughter and applause)—received with quasi-military honours in Ulster, is the kind of pageantry which, as Colonel Seely gravely explidned to the House, was only customary in the case of reigning Sovereigns, Princes of the Wed, Dr a president of a ilepublc. " I wonclir " COMaiented Mr Crosfield " whether they are going to malt s Sir Edward President of Belfast—(Faughter)—and has Warrington to assist In that proceeding ? " (Laughter.) Continuing he asked the audience not to take that colossal bluff, that Orange political braggadocio too seriously. It was not that the Government had not,in all honesty and seriousness, given full consideration to the claims of Ulster or were not willing to do so all the time. The position that they were faced with, how- ever, was this : They had had great effort and agita- tion and had fought general elections in order that they might qualify the veto of the House of Lords. They had got rid of the absolute veto of that Assembly, so the Tories thought they would</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>-TTY THE ULSTER VETO</head>
        <p>-TTY THE ULSTER VETO</p>
        <p>in place of it. (Applause, and a VOICE : "It won't come off.") There was an old saying about the cry of " Wolf," and, in Warrington as elsewhere, they had ' become familiar in their own time with the preposterous claim that the small section of Orange fanatics were making in that quarter. He remembered arguing that question at considerable length twenty years ago, after their good old friend Mr Houston had stood for War- rington. (Applause.) In a letter to the "Examiner " in 1892, he (Mr Crosfleld) quoted as follows from an article in the" Contemporary] Review " by an Irish Presbyterian minister An enlighten.d age, and enlightened country will no longer allow a small but arrogant faction to seize on all the public and municipal appoint- ments of the country and use them for their own aggrandisement and the degradation of their fellow citizens." The same writer remarked—and it was pretty signifi- cant in view of what we had recently witnessed and heard during the Carson campaigo,—that "Notwithstanding all the pressure that is brought to bear upon Protestant clergymen by some of their moneyed anti-National supporters, he is acquainted with about forty Presbyterian Ministers who are Home Rulers, and will fight for Home Rule candi- dates at the General Election." "There are," he</p>
        <p>added "probably a hundred now who are more or less in favour of Home Rule." In the same letter to the " Examiner " he came across a quotation from what was said in Dublin by the Reverend Dr Forbes at the meeting of the Presbyterian General Assembly. He said: "The Presbyterians of Dublin, the south and the west, expressed themselves to the effect that they did not entertain any of the fears that Belfast Protestants entertained with regard to the intro- duction of Home Rule. He had made the acquaintance of a number of Tories in Dublin and after conversing with them he had to affirm that they did not in the least sympathise with the Orange rant in the North." It was also instructive to note what was said about the " Orange rant in the North" by Mr. Joseph Chamberlain in 1881 "What shall I say of the self-styled loyalists who, with fulsome professions of their devotion to 1 the Crown, insult and defy the representatives of the Crown in Ireland, and who break the law them- selves while they pretend to defend it ? I believe at this moment, if there is any danger to the peace in Ireland, it lies in the proceedings of a certain section of the population in Ulster, led by men of education who know enough to know better." So far back as 1826 Lord Palmerston, writing from Derry, alluded to the same kind of Ulster bluff and a much greater man—Edmund Burke—writing long before that time, declared " I am afflicted, deeply and bitterly afflicted, to see that a very small faction of Ireland should</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>lift. rtAnoto smtvt</head>
        <p>lift. rtAnoto smtvt</p>
        <p>•37as at Any time desirous of having an entertainment with his constituents, he (Mr. Wing) would be very pleased to meet the Member on that question on that platform. (Applause.) He would invite him to tea, after which they could adjourn to that hall. At the time of the American War of Independence, when the good old Tories were in power, there was a disposition or a desire to meet some of the current costs that were incurred—we were paying for some of it at the present moment. There was a suggestion that the game tax should be raised to 7s. Gd. in the pound. but Pitt said it would cause a revolution. He also said " You can get money by an easier method ; by the method of indirect taxation—that was Tariff Reform. You can tax the last rag off a man's back or the last mouthful of food from his mouth and he will not know what is injuring him. Ile may grumble about hard times but he will not know that the hard times have been pro- duced by taxation." At the present time there were two great political forces, Toryism and Liberalism. The forces of Liberalism interpreted the law of pro- gress. Toryism was really a right-about policy—or a wrong-about policy, but no matter which way it was, the people always found themselves just where they were. (Hear, bear.) What they wanted was not a stand- still policy but a policy of onward; not a policy of halt! but one of march ! (Applause). Continuing the speaker said the Chairman had rightly said in speaking of war that we had not vet overcome the effects of the war in South Africa. If the men and women only realised what war meant to them they would chase from power every man who talked about it. There was not a man or woman who was not mulcted in Is or is Gd in the .£ as a result of that war, coupled with the Russia- Japanese war. In war time the dividends of armament firms might go up but the purchasing power of the people decreased. Nothing was settled in these days unless it was setled right. The treaty of " peace with honour " brought to us in 1873 by the late Lord Beaconsfield and the late Marquis of Salisbury, a treaty which SOLD 1115 CHRISTIAN PEOPLES IN TITS BALKANS and handed them back to the rule of the Turk, when it ought to have released them, had simply risen again to the surface, and the disappointed people, after 32 years of earnestly seeking for the fulfilment of the promises that were never fulfilled, now said : " Peace is no use. For 32 yearslwe have exercised this pace ; there is nothing but the arbitrament of the sword." The haR and baggage policy of Gladstone was the only rightful policy and the trouble never would be settled until the Parks were out of Europe and the Christians were self-governing. (Applause.) In that sense it was the question of self-government of the people in the Balkans against the over-lordship of a power that was never known to govern Christian communities. (Hear. hear). How we had changed the whole outlook. The difficulties of the foreign minister had been great but amid all the complexities of foreign policies we had kept the place without the sacrifice of any honour on the part of the British Empire. Con- tinuing, Mr Wing dealt with the question of the social welfare of the people and said be had asked men who were experienced in the question and there was not one of them who could remember a period, or ever reading of a period in English history when so many good minds were being centred upon the social welfare of the people in this island. At Church, Nonconformist, Teachers' and Labour Conferenceu they had them all lifting up the question of the social improvement of the masses of the people and the Government had kept in harmony with all. After dealing at some length with the Insurance Act and its innumerable benefits and the way the Government were safeguarding the coming generation the speaker said he had read in the paper of</p>
        <p>the scene in the Mires of Commons the previous night. The Ffouse of Caumons might houn I kir Lloyd George down.:bist tte people outside would ch,ir ;him (Hear, hear.) ktolerring to the great land question which was upset Ling the representatives 1 the tio-cslled noble houses, the speaker said that what was naikitig them so th4eaPy WaS that thry knew they were enjii3 log the income caned hy other people, The Hoveinment bad no wish to he unjust to the landowners. Tnerm was a value attached t o land which h.ci breu creatd hit the community, whit h was pocketed by the individual. There was si tax un the industries a this country end the toil of the people which was not amongst those who owned the land its krge (panif les and reserved it for price. People titirht ;say : Why do you went to have land reform ? nu want ti destroy the</p>
        <p>PLEA•t:RE ariot-M)S OF TOE RICH? (A Volcr: : Yes.) Y. As long as there'cras a man with a spade ready and willing to dig and tte-re ',yes no land for him, then there was no right to be any " pleasure ground " withheld trom the people. (Hear, bear.) Every man who did not bear his proper burden of taxation in the country handed it over for someone else to ear and that man had naturally two burdens to carry. They were not going to tax improvements but to encourage them. Land reform would lesseu the burden of every worker and business man in the country. (Hear, hear.) In Warrington they might take the balance sheet of some of the companies. They were rated for maceinery. The better and more modern the machinery, the higher the rate. EVery improvement they made was rated. Land reform would release every penny off machinery. It had no right to be rated at all, for it was made by man. Tne land which they would tax was made by no man. The land was the basis of all things and what the Liberals wanted to do was to make it contributory in order that they might take the tax off improvements and carry on the back of the land taxes the Poor, Education and Police rates. The police many times whilst they were dressed in the clothing of the police, were very often only another name for game keepers. (Hear, hear.) He wanted the people to enter in that fight because it meant the com- plete reformation of the conditions which were handicapping the masses at the present day. Touching upon the question of the Disestriblishment of the Church in Wales, Mr Wing said he saw on the walls in the town a bill advertising a</p>
        <p>MEETING TO OPPOSE WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT. He wanted to ask those people, candidly and consider- ately, what was the use of holding a public meeting .f they would not allow public opinion to express itself. A public meeting to protest meant that Parliament should listen to their prayer. The people of Wales had already spoken plainly on the subject—as plainly as any portion of the kingdom. They bad said that they woula no longer submit to being dominaterd by a Church. To make one Church a State Church nfAde it the favourite Church to the disfavour of every other Church. In matters of religion every man and woman ought to stand equal before the Stsite as they stood before their Maker. (Hear, hear.) The questions of Home Rule and Welsh Disestablishment had been dis- cussed for a long time. He would say to those who were fond of that piny cry; "You have 'had a Liberal Government year after year why have you not settled these questions before ? that they would have been settled 191 years ago if the Liberal Party had had their voice. They had a chance now of getting their measures through. We were living in stirring times. No Parlia- ment in the memory of man had ever introduced measures so large and comprehensive and with such determination to carry them into effct. (Hear, hear.) Democracy had never yet had the pauer to express Itself that it had at the present time. The power of democracy in Great Britain would come to he the greatest in the world anQ fntqr.. would be so different from the past, for the GGvarnment which was formerly the Govanment merely of the few would be the Government of the many in the interests of the many. (Hear hear and applause. MR OLIVER AND THE CONSERVATIVE YOUNG</p>
        <p>MAN. Mr P M OUvER, the prospective candidate for the Knutsford division, delivered a racy speech which he commenced by referring to land reform. Mr Lloyd George had been badgered in the House of Commons the previous night and the Tories had asked him the names of certain neople who gave him information on the question. "I am glad to think," added Mr Oliver "that I am one of the people who told him." (Applause.) He then proceeded to refer to electoral reform and said manhood suffrage might mean some- thing more than manhood suffrage. He personally hoped that it would, but he knew there were diverse opinions on that point. Manhood suffrage was more important than the abolition of the plural vote, because it would mean the enfranchisement of the young men. Ile could not conceive why any young man should vote Conservative. Some of those present had no doubt been in dusty museums and seen poor little butterflies stuck in a g.ass case with pins through their middle. (Laughter) "That seems to me" commented Mr. Oliver. "the case of the young man who votes Conservative. Poor Mr. Harold Smith is a butterfly in the glass case—(laughter)—of privilege and prejudice. It is a pity be is in that case but, I BM afraid be is rather asphyxiated and likely to remain there." (Laughter). The young men who were going to be enfranchised would, he thought, have a little more enthusiasm and idealism than Mr. Harold Smith. As to the argument that the Bill would enfranchise a lot of uneducated people, all those so called uneducated people had been compulsorily educated by the State, and if the State system of education was not sufficient to educate men in order to exercise the primary duties of citizenship, it was a fraud and a farce. They could not have State education without extending the fran- chise to the whole of the people who benefited by it. One Conservative member bad said that as present legislation was attacking the land, the rich man should have the plural vote in defence of his land. In reply to that it might be argued that as the Tory Party were attacking the food of the people, the poor man should have the plural vote in defence of his food. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. PARKER, in proposing a vote of thanks to the speakers, emphasised Mr Wing's point that war "hit us In the stomach " by withdrawing labour from pro- ducing the things we required and thus reducing the purchasing power of wages. Work which did not pro- duce the products that we needed was wasted work,but the tariff reformers did not seem to realise that fact</p>
        <p>EXCELLENT GOVERNMENT POSTS FOR YOUNG LADIES, AGE 16-20. Girl and Ladv Clerkships, Salary £65-4300. Post Office Learnerships, Salsry £31.-493, NEARLY TWO HUNDRED APPOINTMENTS MADE DURING THE PAST FEW MONTHS. TO SECURE THESE APPOINTqENTS JOIN 17 KERRY'S COLLEGE. Hundreds of Ladies from all over England, as well as from Liverpool, enter the Service through the College Classes. Carefully graduated courses of study for pupils who canisot attend the Oral Classes. The College System ensures certain success for the Enthusiastic Student. The COLLEGE Civil Service Guide Book is sent Post Free to tt 11. 9 7 I 4 LIVERPOOL. EECE STREET</p>
      </div>
