      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>EDUCATION CAMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION CAMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Teachers and the Insurance Act.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Education Committee will be heid at the Town Hall on Monday evening win-n the business to be considered will include the following :</p>
        <p>TEACHERS AND THE INSURANCE ACT. In order to meet the position which has arisen under the National Insurance Act, 1911, it was resolved, that the scale of salaiies for public elementary school teachers adopted by the committee at their meeting on the 25th June, 1906, be amended by the addition of the following paragraph, viz. : "If a teacher who does not come within the opera- tion of the Elementary School Tenchers' (Superannua- tion) Acts, 1898 and 1912, is continuously absent from duty through illness, or any other reasonable cause, the full salary will be paid to the teacher for the first six weeks of absence. The payment (if any) to be made in respect of any further absence will be deter- mined by the Committee."</p>
        <p>311N1311n1 I'ERIOD OF SERVICE FOR TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>The Director of Education was instructed to vrepare, for submission to the School Management Committee at their next meeting, a draft circular to the Managers of the schools in the Borough upon the question of the place of residence and a minimum period of service in respect of teachers hereafter appointed to the schools. "NECESSARY AND DENEI. IC I 11, " EMPLOYMENT. A letter was read to the Fchool Attendance Com- mittee from Messrs Armitage and Rigby, Limited, as to the employment of girls by them, and the Director was instructed to inform the firm that, after careful consideration of their statement, the committee are not prepared to relax their present method of admin- istering the bye laii s whereby they Becure, as far as possible, that only those children who are shown to the satisfaction of the c, mmittee to be both neces- sarily and beneficially employed are permitted to leave school before reaching the age of 14 years. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. Visit of Mr. Lodge Percy's Co.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. Visit of Mr. Lodge Percy's Co.</p>
        <p>The lover of thoroughgoing drama needs no more assurance than that the Lodge Percy combination. are presenting their repertoire to be satisfied that he is in for a good thing, The Company are a draw in almost every town and this week has proved no exception to the rule, for the popular Rylands-street House has been crowded nightly. The Company have had no light task to present their plays twice nightly and one cannot fail to admire the able manner in which each individual artiste has played his or her part in either of the three plays produced during the week. On Monday and Thursday the Company pre- seated the sensational anarchist romance "Called back." Mr Lodge Percy soon installed himself a favourite with the audience in the role of Arthur Kenyon, the bashful lover of Marion Vaughan. The Marion of Hugh Conway's novel lives again under the treatment of Miss Henrietta Sol:tiler ' whose finished 'toting earns unstinted appreciation Mr Walter E Kirby is a capital Gilbert Vaughan, for he is a clever elocutionist as well as a gifted actor Mr George Cruickshanks as Paoli Macrai is the typical anarchist, his whole manner and style making the part as repugnant as it should be. Mr F Douglas Howard is a capital Dr Cenari. The other characters were all well-sustained.—On Tuesday evening "The Unpardonable Sin" was presented with equal success and last night "The Sorrows of Satan" was given. To-night (Saturday) the ever-popular domestic drama " East Lynne" will be staged, and to all those who have not visited the.theatre this week we would urge them to witness this combination of clever players to-night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>MISS GORNALL'S MATINEE CONCERT,</head>
        <p>MISS GORNALL'S MATINEE CONCERT,</p>
        <p>Miss Mary Gornall's concert in Bold-street school- room on Wednesday afternoon attracted only a moderate audience chiefly, of course, ladies. Miss Gornall herself took part as a piano soloist, being assisted in presenting a programme of distinctly modern music by Mrs. Norman Melland, Miss Amethe Leadbetter, and Miss Evelyn Hall. Of the singing the only thing one can say which is not praise is that among fifteen songs there were found so many airy trifles, and co little that is really satisfying. The best group was the last in the programme, "I will make you brooches," "In youth is pleasure," and " Blackbird's sona." The violin solos, like those for the piano, were fairly [interesting and were on the whole well played, but the outstanding feature of the concert was unquestionably the harp solos which probably proved something in the nature of a revela- tion to many. That Miss Evelyn Hall's technique as a harpist was of the highest order was attested by everything she played and one was the more moved by her finished rendering of the various solos because it was felt that allied with it there was the temperament of the real artiste. The accompaniments to M. Norman Melland's songs were most tastefully played by Mr. It. J. Forbes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>LOSS OF SUBMARINE 82.</head>
        <p>LOSS OF SUBMARINE 82.</p>
        <p>Of the 15 officers and men who lost their lives in this disaster off Dover the other day, eight were assured with the Prudential Assurence Company for a total amount of £734.</p>
        <p>- -- TO CORRESPONDENTS. H.J.W.—Your letter reached 113 too late for insertion this week.—Ed.</p>
        <p>The Smoke Nuisaoc3. Sir, —The Municipal Elections are approaching and I venture to suggest that this is a suitable time for all who sympathise with your recent crusade against the Smoke Nuisance to put their opinions into practice. I shall not personally vote for or in any way support any candidate who is not able to satisfy me that he is prepared to carry out the law regarding this important subject instead of winking at its evabion.— Faithfully yours,</p>
        <p>ARTHUR BENNETT, Warrington, 18th October,.lol2. Latchford Election.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l read with some amusement a letter in your contemperary of Saturday last signed "Bystander." He is evidently greatly alarmed lest the electors should not know what I am in poliCcs. I make no attempt to hide them ; I could not if I attempted. .1 he active part I have taken for years in politics is well known. I should, however, like to ask "Bystander" what has party politics to do with Municipal matters. The Nomination Paper does not require a man to state whether be is Liberal, Labour or Tory. The ballot paper makes no distinction ; you are simply asked to vote for " Jones " or "Smith," and the electors are called upon to vote for whom they consider the best man. In this case the best man is " Jones."— Yours tkc ,</p>
        <p>E. WYNNE JONES.</p>
        <p>Latcbfor,l, 17th October, 1012. -- Motor Buses for Outlying Districts.</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 noticed from your report of the Yast Council meeting, that the above question was under considera- tion :—lf the Whitley Superior and Stretton Parish Council petitions can receive consideration at the hands cf the Town Council, I think it is time the resi- dents in the Orford Ward received some attention. This district, and the Lovely-lane district (to which Alderman Smethurst made allusion) are, I think the only two parts of the town that are wit served by travelling conveniences. I am afraid that, if the inhabitants in Orford Ward district are to wait until the tramways are extended up Pinner's Brow, it will be a case of " Hope deferred " which " maketh the heart grow sick." Therefore, the only solution, for the present is the motor 'bus,' and I hope the Town Council will press it forward. The 'buses could be run up Pinner's Prow, Orford-lane Orford-avenue, Norris-street and Oorsey-lane. There is a large population in the vicinity of the roads named, and the travelling convenience would, I fed sure, be appreciated by the residents. I do not think there would be any question about the 'buses paying on this route.</p>
        <p>It is to be hoped that the electors in Orford Ward will make this a prominent question in the coming November elections.</p>
        <p>lam not in the Ward named (being an outsider), but if the 'buses were run along Norris-street, they would prove a convenience to the residents at Orford Green.—Yours truly, , F. I'AYLOB, " Thorn Lea," Birtles-road, Orford Green.</p>
        <p>The Town Planning Act. Sir,—The Corporation of Warrington by adopting the Act and applying for the necessary powers without further delay merit the thanks of the community. As one listened to the clear and able statement of the Deputy Town Clerk presenting the case to the Commissioner, one could not fail to be struck with the fact that the Act would lead in a practical way to the better housing of the people—to say nothing of the prevention of future congested areas, and also to the provision of new wide roads, now so necessary to Vas increasing motor and other vehicular traffic, and to the better and more definite planning out of the town.</p>
        <p>So far, however, as one can foresee, the great increase and requirements of the people will not be so much within the Borough but on the outskirts of the town. The inhabitants are naturally gravitating in those directions.</p>
        <p>It therefore seems a pity that the Warrington Rural District Authority and the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Authority, as was elicited at the Enquiry, had up to the present, failed to co-operate with Warrington in adopting the Act. That they have considered the matter to some extent I believe is true, but it is evident that the Corporation of Warrington had become weary of waiting, and thus very wisely have promptly acted on their own. It appears to me that the two authorities named need spurring to some extent. That some of their representatives are taking no interest in the matter was proved at the attendance at the enquiry of Dr Murray and Mr Furness, the Penketh representatives, and also Mr. Newcombe, the Surveyor —but other representatives so far as I could see were conspicuous by their absence.</p>
        <p>So far as I en gather the two authorities have no settled plans with regard to the laying out of their district—with the result that in some cases the build- ing lines are all " higgledy piggledy." We have had, too, in our own district a case where a row of houses was built right across what many a the residents were hoping would have been a useful highway —but which may now lead to future increased expenditure. Many can cite such instances of shortsightedness. The adoption of a Town Planning Act will prevent- cases of this nature taking place and also lead to the better and definite laying out of districts. It should be borne in mind that the Act improves the provisions of open areas—and also to the limiting of a certain quantity of houses to each acre of land. On mere humanitarian grounds I am sure the Act will appeal to most thoughtful people. May I suggest to the inhabitants of the rural districts that they should approach the members of their various Parish Councils and District Councils to have the Act without further delay adopted and carried out within their areas.—Yours faithfully, E. ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>1 ters ti 11, I -1.-:.Lajj Lgt to thq ki EDITOR*. _ NOTICE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-19_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PROPERTY OWNERS' LAMENT. Alarm at Land Valuation.</head>
        <p>PROPERTY OWNERS' LAMENT. Alarm at Land Valuation.</p>
        <p>The Government's land policy came in for warm criticism at a meeting of " property owners, builders and all who are interested in laud, buildings and , property generally," which was held in the Masonic ' liali, Bold-street, on Thursday afternoon, under the auspices of the Warrington Branch of the Land Union. The principal speaker was Mr A J Lawman, organising secretary of the Union in the North of England. Mr Joseph Twiss presided over a good attendance which included the Rev SC E Carleton, Councillor W H J Jenkins, Messrs H B Melville, A Lister Kaye, Sydney Wellhead, J Hesketh, H J Longton, E England, W Furness, W B Forshaw, E Bradshaw, W Skelton and J Eustace Seeker (secretary of the branch). ' Tbe CHantmeri,after complaining of the complicated and uncertain character of the provisional valuation forms, said the Socialistic tendencies of the day were such that they meant, if possible, to tax property owners' capital out of existence—to make the property utterly valueless,ao this t they would be ready in despair, to throw it over to the Government. Then he supposed, they might go in for the old age pension. (Laughter.) He had been told that, whatever party got into power, the imposts on land would continue, hut he thought they might reasonably demand a simplification of them, and a statement like they got in the case of the local rates. The working of the Finance Act had killed the property market and destroyed confidence in the money market. Men had been going about this district for months, unable to secure a mortgage, and builders were almost ready to commit suicide. A letter appeared in one of the local papers on Saturday from Mr E K Muspratt, president of the Financial Reform ' Association, in which he said : "The tax imposed in Mr Lloyd George's Budeet has already had the effect of bringing into use many spaces that were being held up for a rise in value, and the building trades have been greatly benefited thereby." If that were true, he (the speaker) would not stand before them for five minutes, but it was not. There might have been St single isolated case in some far distant town but in this district, instead of having benefited the building trades it had utterly demoralized them and in this town they had been tumbling over one another to come to grief indirectly in consequence of the Act. Mr LAWMAN, who addressed his speech entirely to the "small man," stigmatised the statement quoted from Mr Muspratt's letter as an infamous lie, and said Mr Mustrall must have known that'it was. He further asserted that the Government were under-valuing town property' for the purpose of getting increment value duty and over-valuing agricultural property for the purpose of getting undeveloped land duty. The incre- ment value duty was supposed te be levied only on the</p>
      </div>
