        <p>USINESS HOURS 9 to 8 DAII:</p>
        <p>tt benefit which had accrued to !the property owing to Novi energies and activities of the community. too they found that it was being levied od a builde,e profit. They would hardly imagine, after beautiful things they were told in support of t' Budget, that a poor man who bought a leasehold uousl for £270, and, having to leave town through lose °id work, sold it for £260 and incurred £l5 ',oats, vioZ:et be asked to pay £3 18s as increment value duty._., that had occurred. That man came to the Land un,'"'„ and the authorities were ashamed to enforce the vtlit tion. There were loads of cases like that. In suPrijr of his contention that the valuations were useless, ts, Lawman said that two properties which were absnigtery, identical had been valued at £620 and £560 respective Be could tell them of streets where houses with to rooms extra had been valued at less than the ottieo houses. Whea the valuation did Coma in—if it ever wiat completed—it would not be worth that (a snap °f tay fingers), because it would not be a valuation upon ee,a basis or principle. Referring to Mr Lloyd Georgdid land campaign, Mr Lawman said the Governaten,t oes not want to improve the housing of the working but to tax the land to the fullest extent and thus 6110, possession of. the property. They meant to go t,°, ttbe end with that campaign and they would do so, property owners did not become less apathetic. CePr4- Pretyman had declared that if the Unionist. Gov',,'„t, ment did not bring in a Bill to repeal the inerem—t value duty and the undeveloped land tax, he would 0°). serve upon any Conservative administration. (APPleuseof Mr SECKER said he could mention two jots ja cottage property at Warrington, exactly simileLso. character, which had been valued at £650 and respectively, merely as the result of a bargain. is The Rev E C E CARLETON said they were getting at a very bad way and they would not only have to figto for their liberties but some of them would soon have tight for their very existence. Mr W. FURNEIS, in proposing a vote of thankei:: Mr Lawman, said he did not share the.pessimistki of some of his friends as to the future. 13,_e was Inclined to think that the enormous taxation whinu met being piled upon land and property would react egs_ob the Government, because they could not impose °our taxes without the man who occupied the house ultinlea having to pay them. There would then be led to revulsion of feeling that if the Government spPee the' the country within the next six or twelve months would be swept oil the board. ied- Mr P LAWTON seconded and the vote wo narr-</p>
